# ================================================================
# 2.14.0 — the six geo entry points whose guards had a scale baked in
# ================================================================
# ⛔ EVERY ONE OF THESE FABRICATES RATHER THAN FAILS. `geo_ray_plane`,
# `geo_ray_sphere`, `geo_ray_triangle` and `geo_ray_capsule` return 0, which this
# module DEFINES as "miss"; `geo_closest_point_on_sphere` returns a point that
# really is on the sphere but in a direction the caller never asked for; and
# `geo_barycentric_coords` returns (1/3, 1/3, 1/3), which sums to 1 and lies
# inside the triangle. None of them is an Inf or a NaN a caller could test for.
#
# ⚠ THE OBVIOUS ASSERTION IS THE VACUOUS ONE. Checking |cp - center| == radius
# passes on the sphere fabrication — measured equal — so these assertions are on
# DIRECTION and on the returned t, never on a magnitude the fallback shares.
# [measured: independent verification of all six sites, 2026-09-09]

# ================================================================
# 2.15.0 — cx_div / cx_inv: the defect complex.cyr has named since 2.6.14
# ================================================================
# ⛔ `complex.cyr:58` has carried a comment describing this exact class since
# 2.6.14, and `cx_div` — the function named IN that comment — kept doing it.
# 2.6.13 fixed the EXPONENT (squared modulus vs a squared tolerance) and left the
# SCALE, so a legitimate small divisor was still flushed to exactly 0+0i: a value
# bit-identical to a real zero quotient, with no error channel to separate them.
#
# ⭐ AND NO THRESHOLD FIXES IT — this is why the repair is structural. `cx_div` is
# degree 0 in a common scale k, so `cx_div(k*a, k*b)` must not contain k at all.
# Measured over 401 decades, counting FAILING COMPONENTS out of 802:
#     1e-12 (pre-2.6.13) ...... 482      DBL_MIN .................. 186
#     1e-24 (shipped) ......... 470      no guard at all .......... 178
#     sqrt(DBL_MIN) ........... 340      Smith's algorithm .......... 0
# `br² + bi²` overflows to +Inf above k ~ 1e154 and underflows to 0 below 1e-154,
# so the quantity is destroyed BEFORE any comparison runs and removing the guard
# entirely still fails 178. ⚠ **Judging the repair against 178 rather than 0 would
# have licensed leaving 89 decades broken.** Smith's divides through by the LARGER
# component first, so the intermediate is bounded by 1 and the squaring never
# happens. [measured: probe over 401 decades; shipped 470, repaired 0, 2026-09-10]

# ================================================================
# 2.15.0 — the exponential maps: 296 decades of discarded rotation
# ================================================================
# ⛔ Every one of these guarded a LENGTH — the norm of the caller's axis, or the
# rotation angle — against an absolute 1e-12, and every fallback is a structurally
# valid element (identity rotation, identity boost) returned with no rc. A caller
# cannot tell "I rotated by your tiny angle" from "I gave up and returned I".
#
# ⭐ THE DISCRIMINATOR IS EXACTNESS, NOT A TOLERANCE. For t below ~1e-8, sin(t) IS
# t in f64 — the Taylor correction is below the last bit — so `so3_exp((0,0,t))`
# must put exactly `t` in the (0,1) slot. That is a bit-exact assertion over 500
# scales, and the shipped guard puts exactly 0 there for every t < 1e-12.
#
# ⚠ THE REPAIR IS A SET, NOT A LINE. `so3_exp` delegates to `so3_from_axis_angle`,
# and `su2_exp` is tied to `so3_exp` by the SU(2) -> SO(3) cover invariant already
# pinned by the "Lie: SU(2)->SO(3) double cover (half-angle convention)" group.
# Fixing one alone just moves the binding constraint, so all seven norm-guards in
# lie/lie_ext move together.
# [measured: docs/audit/2026-09-09-epsilon-open.json]

# ================================================================
# 2.15.0 — four more guards on quantities the CALLER chooses the units of
# ================================================================
# ⚠ These four were repaired before this block existed and every mutant SURVIVED,
# because nothing in the tree exercised them off unit scale. A repair with no
# assertion is a repair nobody can keep.
# [measured: 9-mutant run; 4 survivors before this block]

# ================================================================
# 2.15.0 — the numerical solvers: pivots and curvature tests
# ================================================================
# ⛔ `num_tridiag_solve`'s three pivots and `optimize`'s four curvature/beta tests
# all compared a quantity carrying the CALLER'S units against an absolute 1e-12.
# ⚠ These refuse rather than fabricate — HSB_ERR_DIVISION_BY_ZERO with `out` left
# untouched, or a silently zeroed beta — so the failure mode is a solvable problem
# declared unsolvable. Still wrong, and still on public entry points.

# ================================================================
# 2.15.0 — collision_core: a tolerance used as an ORDER and as a SIGN
# ================================================================
# ⛔ Three different misuses of one constant. (1) `sequential_impulse` tested a
# sum of INVERSE MASSES — units 1/mass — so whether two bodies counted as "both
# static" depended on kilograms vs tonnes. (2) `_col_xy_greater` is a SORT
# COMPARATOR and an epsilon-equality is not transitive, so the relation was not a
# total order at all; a sort driven by a non-order can drop or duplicate elements,
# and the hull inherits that silently. (3) Four convexity tests asked whether
# twice-the-signed-area exceeded 1e-12 instead of asking its SIGN, so a strictly
# convex vertex of a small polygon was classified concave — a plausible DIAGNOSIS
# rather than a plausible number, which is worse.
# [measured: docs/audit/2026-09-09-epsilon-open.json]
# ⭐ `_col_point_in_tri` in the same file has always used the exact sign test on
# this same quantity, so the repair is consistency, not a new rule.

# ================================================================
# 2.15.0 — geo_advanced: bilinearity, slabs and a measured null bound
# ================================================================
# ⛔ EIGHT OF THE EIGHTEEN GUARDED NOTHING AT ALL. The CGA products skipped a term
# when a blade COEFFICIENT was below 1e-12 — but a coefficient is not a
# denominator, it is one factor of a_i*b_j, and these products are exactly
# BILINEAR. Any non-zero threshold destroys that: scale a multivector down and
# terms start vanishing that contributed at the original scale.
# ⛔ The BVH slab tests guarded `1/d` with an absolute cut on a ray direction that
# `GeoRay` does not require to be unit, so a short direction vector made whole
# slabs "parallel" and the traversal missed real hits.
# ⭐ And `cga_blade_inverse` was one line doing two jobs — "can I divide by this"
# and "is this blade null" — which need different tests. The null bound is now
# MEASURED (8 x DBL_EPSILON) rather than borrowed: at 1e-12 it is ~4500x looser
# than the observed residual and discards resolvable geometry.
# [measured: docs/audit/2026-09-09-epsilon-open.json]

test_group("geo_advanced 2.15.0: CGA bilinearity and BVH slabs");

# --- BILINEARITY. gp(k*a, b) == k*gp(a, b), exactly, at every k. Two general
# multivectors so the sparse skip actually has terms to skip.
fn _ga_mv2(s, t, u) {
    var m = cga_mv_zero();
    cga_mv_set(m, 0, s);
    cga_mv_set(m, 1, t);
    cga_mv_set(m, 2, u);
    cga_mv_set(m, 6, f64_add(s, t));
    return m;
}
var _GA_RUN = 0;
var _GA_BAD = 0;
for (var _ga_e = 0; _ga_e < 400; _ga_e = _ga_e + 1) {
    var _ga_k = (1023 - _ga_e) << 52;
    var _ga_a1 = _ga_mv2(F64_ONE, F64_TWO, f64_from(3));
    var _ga_b = _ga_mv2(f64_from(5), f64_from(7), f64_from(11));
    var _ga_ak = _ga_mv2(_ga_k, f64_mul(F64_TWO, _ga_k), f64_mul(f64_from(3), _ga_k));
    var _ga_r1 = cga_geometric_product(_ga_a1, _ga_b);
    var _ga_rk = cga_geometric_product(_ga_ak, _ga_b);
    _GA_RUN = _GA_RUN + 1;
    for (var _ga_i = 0; _ga_i < 32; _ga_i = _ga_i + 1) {
        # k * (a.b)_i, bit-exact for a power-of-two k.
        if (cga_mv_get(_ga_rk, _ga_i)
          != f64_mul(_ga_k, cga_mv_get(_ga_r1, _ga_i))) {
            _GA_BAD = _GA_BAD + 1;
        }
    }
    # the OUTER product and both contractions are bilinear in exactly the same way
    if (cga_mv_get(cga_outer_product(_ga_ak, _ga_b), 6)
      != f64_mul(_ga_k, cga_mv_get(cga_outer_product(_ga_a1, _ga_b), 6))) {
        _GA_BAD = _GA_BAD + 1;
    }
    if (cga_mv_get(cga_left_contraction(_ga_ak, _ga_b), 0)
      != f64_mul(_ga_k, cga_mv_get(cga_left_contraction(_ga_a1, _ga_b), 0))) {
        _GA_BAD = _GA_BAD + 1;
    }
    if (cga_mv_get(cga_right_contraction(_ga_ak, _ga_b), 0)
      != f64_mul(_ga_k, cga_mv_get(cga_right_contraction(_ga_a1, _ga_b), 0))) {
        _GA_BAD = _GA_BAD + 1;
    }
}
assert_eq(_GA_RUN, 400, "the CGA bilinearity sweep actually ran 400 scales");
assert_eq(_GA_BAD, 0,
  "cga products: bilinear in the left operand at every scale from 2^0 to 2^-399");

# --- cga_rotor / cga_plane: the axis and the normal are DIRECTIONS, so their
# length is a unit choice and the result must not move at all.
# ⛔ 2.17.0 — THIS SWEEP RAN 400 BINADES DOWNWARD AND THE ARITHMETIC FLOOR IS AT
# 2^-538, so it stopped 138 binades short of the defect and had no upward half at
# all. Both functions took their axis norm as a naive sum of squares, and the
# 2.14.0 note beside `cga_rotor` — "an absolute 1e-12 turned a real rotation into
# the identity rotor for a short axis vector, with no error channel" — described a
# failure that was STILL LIVE, 500 binades lower. Measured on the 2.16.0 tree:
# **995 of 2041 scales wrong for each, and 991 of the rotor's were the fabricated
# identity.** Moving a threshold does not help when the quantity it tests has
# already lost the value.
# ⭐ Now 2041 scales, BOTH directions, and an OFF-AXIS axis: (3k, 4k, 12k) has a
# norm of 13k, so the normalised axis has three non-zero components and a partial
# repair cannot pass by leaving one of them alone.
# [measured: 2041-binade two-sided sweep before and after, 2026-09-10]
var _GR_RUN = 0;
var _GR_BAD = 0;
var _GR_IDENT = 0;
var _gr_ref = cga_rotor(f64_from(3), f64_from(4), f64_from(12), F64_PI_2);
var _gp_ref = cga_plane(f64_from(3), f64_from(4), f64_from(12), F64_ONE);
for (var _gr_e = 0 - 1020; _gr_e <= 1020; _gr_e = _gr_e + 1) {
    var _gr_k = (1023 + _gr_e) << 52;
    var _gr_r = cga_rotor(f64_mul(f64_from(3), _gr_k), f64_mul(f64_from(4), _gr_k),
      f64_mul(f64_from(12), _gr_k), F64_PI_2);
    # the fabrication has a name: an identity rotor for a perfectly good axis.
    if (cga_mv_get(_gr_r, 10) == 0) { _GR_IDENT = _GR_IDENT + 1; }
    # ⚠ `d` MUST SCALE WITH THE NORMAL. cga_plane divides d by |n| as well, so
    # (0,0,k,1) is the plane at distance 1/k — a genuinely different plane, not the
    # same one in other units. The first draft passed a fixed d and "failed" 798
    # checks against correct output: exactly 2 per scale, the two components that
    # carry d. [measured: 2 x 399 failing components, 2026-09-10]
    var _gr_p = cga_plane(f64_mul(f64_from(3), _gr_k), f64_mul(f64_from(4), _gr_k),
      f64_mul(f64_from(12), _gr_k), _gr_k);
    _GR_RUN = _GR_RUN + 1;
    for (var _gr_i = 0; _gr_i < 32; _gr_i = _gr_i + 1) {
        if (cga_mv_get(_gr_r, _gr_i) != cga_mv_get(_gr_ref, _gr_i)) {
            _GR_BAD = _GR_BAD + 1;
        }
        if (cga_mv_get(_gr_p, _gr_i) != cga_mv_get(_gp_ref, _gr_i)) {
            _GR_BAD = _GR_BAD + 1;
        }
    }
}
assert_eq(_GR_RUN, 2041, "the rotor/plane axis sweep actually ran 2041 scales");
assert_eq(_GR_BAD, 0,
  "cga_rotor / cga_plane: bit-identical for axis lengths 2^-1020 to 2^1020");
assert_eq(_GR_IDENT, 0,
  "cga_rotor: never fabricates the identity rotor for a non-zero axis (did at 991 scales)");
# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION: an exactly zero axis has no rotation and an exactly zero
# normal has no plane.
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_rotor(0, 0, 0, F64_PI_2), 0), 0x3FF0000000000000,
  "cga_rotor: an exactly zero axis still returns the identity rotor");
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_plane(0, 0, 0, F64_ONE), 1), 0,
  "cga_plane: an exactly zero normal returns the zero multivector");
# ⚠ AND THE NaN CONTRACTS. Through 3.3.2 they differed: `cga_plane` folded a NaN
# normal into the null multivector (its guard was `f64_gt(norm, 0) == 0`, and
# `f64_gt(NaN, 0)` is 0) while `cga_rotor` propagated NaN, and 2.17.0 kept both
# as shipped contracts. ⛔ 3.3.3 reversed the plane's: the zero multivector is
# the answer for "no plane", and a NaN normal is not a missing normal, so the
# fold was the fabricated-answer class. Both guards are now `f64_eq(norm, 0)`
# and both siblings propagate; both are pinned here.
# ⚠ AND A SUBNORMAL AXIS, WHICH SEPARATES `f64_div` FROM A RECIPROCAL AND WHICH
# THE SWEEP ABOVE CANNOT PRODUCE — `(1023 + e) << 52` encodes only NORMAL powers
# of two, so `norm` never falls below DBL_MIN there. `1 / 2^-1030` is +Inf, so a
# multiply-by-reciprocal turns every zero component into `0 * Inf` = NaN while
# dividing leaves it at 0. Both mutants SURVIVED the 2041-scale sweep and die on
# these two assertions. [measured: 16-mutant run, 2026-09-10]
var _gr_sub = 1 << 44;                            # 2^-1030, subnormal
# (The rotor's zero component is -0.0 since cyrius 6.6.8's IEEE `f64_neg`; the property
# is "zero, not NaN", so it is compared as a value.)
assert_eq(f64_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_rotor(_gr_sub, 0, 0, F64_PI_2), 6), 0), 1,
  "cga_rotor: a subnormal axis divides, it does not multiply by an infinite reciprocal");
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_plane(_gr_sub, 0, 0, 0), 2), 0,
  "cga_plane: the same, on the component that would become 0 * Inf = NaN");
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_plane(_gr_sub, 0, 0, 0), 1), 0x3FF0000000000000,
  "cga_plane: and the live component still normalises to 1");

var _gr_nan = cga_mv_get(cga_rotor(0x7FF8000000000000, 0, 0, F64_PI_2), 10);
assert_eq(f64_eq(_gr_nan, _gr_nan), 0,
  "cga_rotor: a NaN axis propagates, it is not folded into the identity rotor");
var _g6_pin_pl = cga_mv_get(cga_plane(0x7FF8000000000000, 0, 0, F64_ONE), 1);
assert_eq(f64_eq(_g6_pin_pl, _g6_pin_pl), 0,
  "cga_plane: a NaN normal propagates, not folded to the null multivector (reversed 3.3.3)");

# === 3.3.3 G6: cga_plane at a NaN normal, every coefficient ===
# The pin above reads e1 only. The true value is settled on the function's own
# arithmetic rather than on its output: the norm it divides by is the chained
# `ganita_f64_hypot`, which propagates a NaN leg (the oracle control below), and
# x / NaN is NaN for every x, so e1, e2, e3 and the ninf slot that carries d are
# all NaN. The second fixture puts the NaN in the INNER hypot's second leg with a
# finite outer leg, so a guard that inspected only `nx` or only the outer call
# could not pass it. (`_g6_nan` is the quiet NaN the geo block defines.)
var _g6_hn = ganita_f64_hypot(ganita_f64_hypot(_g6_nan, 0), 0);
assert_eq(f64_eq(_g6_hn, _g6_hn), 0,
  "oracle: cga_plane's chained-hypot norm of a NaN normal is itself NaN");
var _g6_pl = cga_plane(_g6_nan, 0, 0, F64_ONE);
var _g6_pl_nan = 0;
for (var _g6_i = 1; _g6_i < 6; _g6_i = _g6_i + 1) {
    if (_g6_i != 4) {
        if (f64_eq(cga_mv_get(_g6_pl, _g6_i), cga_mv_get(_g6_pl, _g6_i)) == 0) {
            _g6_pl_nan = _g6_pl_nan + 1;
        }
    }
}
assert_eq(_g6_pl_nan, 4,
  "cga_plane: a NaN normal is NaN in all four of e1, e2, e3 and ninf");
var _g6_pl2 = cga_plane(0, _g6_nan, F64_ONE, F64_ONE);
assert_eq(f64_eq(cga_mv_get(_g6_pl2, 3), cga_mv_get(_g6_pl2, 3)), 0,
  "cga_plane: a NaN y beside a finite z still poisons the norm, so e3 is NaN as well");

# === 3.3.3 R4ga: cga_plane with the NaN in nz ===
# Neither fixture above puts the NaN in the OUTER hypot's leg, so a fold of 3.3.2's
# kind restricted to nz (`if nz is NaN, return the zero multivector`) passed every
# suite. With the NaN there the norm is NaN, and e3 and ninf divide to NaN.
# [measured: V6 mutant P2 on the 3.3.3 sources, re-run by R4ga on this tree, 2026-10-01; cycc 6.6.12]
var _r4ga_plz = cga_plane(0, 0, _g6_nan, F64_ONE);
assert_eq(f64_eq(cga_mv_get(_r4ga_plz, 3), cga_mv_get(_r4ga_plz, 3)), 0,
  "cga_plane(0, 0, NaN, 1): e3 is NaN, not folded to the zero multivector");
assert_eq(f64_eq(cga_mv_get(_r4ga_plz, 5), cga_mv_get(_r4ga_plz, 5)), 0,
  "cga_plane(0, 0, NaN, 1): ninf, which carries d, is NaN as well");

# --- BVH SLAB TESTS. `GeoRay` does not require a unit direction — `_graw` builds
# one directly and so does any caller that sets the field — so `|dx| < 1e-12`
# declared a slab "parallel" purely because the caller's direction vector was
# short. The two boxes below straddle x = 1; an x-aligned ray through them hits
# both at every direction magnitude, because a ray is a set of points and scaling
# its direction reparameterises it without moving it.
var _GB_RUN = 0;
var _GB_BAD = 0;
var _gb_arr = alloc(16);
store64(_gb_arr, geo_aabb_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), hvec3_new(F64_ONE, F64_ONE, F64_ONE)));
store64(_gb_arr + 8, geo_aabb_new(hvec3_new(F64_ONE, 0, 0),
    hvec3_new(F64_TWO, F64_ONE, F64_ONE)));
var _gb_tree = bvh_build(_gb_arr, 2);
for (var _gb_e = 0; _gb_e < 400; _gb_e = _gb_e + 1) {
    var _gb_k = (1023 - _gb_e) << 52;
    # ⚠ NOT geo_ray_new — it normalises, which erases the very quantity these
    # guards get wrong. The y and z components are exactly 0, so the y and z slab
    # guards fire on EVERY iteration and the x guard on none: all three sites are
    # exercised, which a diagonal ray would not do.
    var _gb_r = alloc(sizeof(GeoRay));
    GeoRay_set_origin(_gb_r, hvec3_new(F64_HALF, F64_HALF, F64_HALF));
    GeoRay_set_direction(_gb_r, hvec3_new(_gb_k, 0, 0));
    _GB_RUN = _GB_RUN + 1;
    if (vec_len(bvh_query_ray(_gb_tree, _gb_r)) != 2) { _GB_BAD = _GB_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_GB_RUN, 400, "the BVH direction-magnitude sweep actually ran 400 scales");
assert_eq(_GB_BAD, 0,
  "bvh_query_ray: an interior x-ray hits both boxes for |d| from 2^0 to 2^-399");
# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION: the parallel-slab branch must still reject a ray that runs
# alongside the boxes and outside them.
var _gb_out = alloc(sizeof(GeoRay));
GeoRay_set_origin(_gb_out, hvec3_new(F64_HALF, f64_from(5), F64_HALF));
GeoRay_set_direction(_gb_out, hvec3_new(F64_ONE, 0, 0));
assert_eq(vec_len(bvh_query_ray(_gb_tree, _gb_out)), 0,
  "bvh_query_ray: a ray parallel to the slabs and outside them still misses");

# --- cga_blade_inverse. nsq(kB) = k^2 nsq(B), so (kB)^-1 = (1/k) B^-1. The old
# absolute cut on a SQUARED norm called ordinary blades null.
var _GI_RUN = 0;
var _GI_BAD = 0;
var _gi_ref = cga_blade_inverse(_ga_mv2(F64_ONE, F64_TWO, f64_from(3)));
for (var _gi_e = 0; _gi_e < 250; _gi_e = _gi_e + 1) {
    var _gi_k = (1023 - _gi_e) << 52;
    var _gi_b = _ga_mv2(_gi_k, f64_mul(F64_TWO, _gi_k), f64_mul(f64_from(3), _gi_k));
    var _gi_r = cga_blade_inverse(_gi_b);
    _GI_RUN = _GI_RUN + 1;
    for (var _gi_i = 0; _gi_i < 32; _gi_i = _gi_i + 1) {
        if (cga_mv_get(_gi_r, _gi_i)
          != f64_div(cga_mv_get(_gi_ref, _gi_i), _gi_k)) {
            _GI_BAD = _GI_BAD + 1;
        }
    }
}
assert_eq(_GI_RUN, 250, "the blade-inverse scale sweep actually ran 250 scales");
assert_eq(_GI_BAD, 0,
  "cga_blade_inverse: (kB)^-1 = (1/k)B^-1 bit-exact for scales 2^0 to 2^-249");
# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION, and it is why the two tests are SPLIT: a conformal point is
# genuinely null — its nsq is bit-exactly 0 — and must still return the zero
# multivector rather than dividing by it.
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_blade_inverse(cga_point(F64_ONE, F64_TWO, f64_from(3))), 0), 0,
  "cga_blade_inverse: a conformal point is null and still inverts to zero");
# ⭐ AND THE NULL BOUND ITSELF, which the scale sweep cannot reach. Reusing
# `EPSILON_F64` for it is ~4500x looser than the measured residual and throws away
# resolvable geometry: a sphere of radius 10 centred at 1e4 has nsq = 100 exactly
# — a ratio of 2.0e-14 to its own magnitude, 90x above the observed noise floor —
# yet a 1e-12 relative bound declares it null and returns the zero multivector.
# It is a real sphere and it must invert. [measured: ratio 2.0e-14 vs floor 2.2e-16]
var _gi_sph = cga_sphere(f64_from(10000), 0, 0, f64_from(10));
var _gi_sinv = cga_blade_inverse(_gi_sph);
var _gi_nz = 0;
for (var _gi_j = 0; _gi_j < 32; _gi_j = _gi_j + 1) {
    if (cga_mv_get(_gi_sinv, _gi_j) != 0) { _gi_nz = _gi_nz + 1; }
}
assert(_gi_nz > 0,
  "cga_blade_inverse: a radius-10 sphere at 1e4 is not null and inverts");

# === 3.3.4 D052: the null test is covariant ===
# Through 3.3.3 the bound was 2^-49 * sum b_i^2, which in the null basis counts n0^2
# and ninf^2, in no term of the norm²: degree four in a sphere's scale against a norm²
# of degree two. A unit sphere from distance ~6.9e3 and every origin sphere with r
# below ~4.2e-8 or above ~4.7e7 got the zero multivector. The bound is now 2^-49 * T,
# T = sum |b_i| |b_partner(i)|, the magnitudes of the terms the norm² is summed from.
# [measured: D052 sweep on the 3.3.4 base tree and this tree, scratch, 2026-10-03; cycc 6.6.14]
# The scalar partner table: one partner per blade, an involution, and exactly the
# blade whose product with it has a scalar part.
var _d52_pt = _cga_scalar_partner_tbl();
var _D52_PBAD = 0;
for (var _d52_i = 0; _d52_i < 32; _d52_i = _d52_i + 1) {
    var _d52_pi = load64(_d52_pt + _d52_i * 8);
    if (load64(_d52_pt + _d52_pi * 8) != _d52_i) { _D52_PBAD = _D52_PBAD + 1; }
    for (var _d52_j = 0; _d52_j < 32; _d52_j = _d52_j + 1) {
        var _d52_ei = cga_mv_zero();
        cga_mv_set(_d52_ei, _d52_i, F64_ONE);
        var _d52_ej = cga_mv_zero();
        cga_mv_set(_d52_ej, _d52_j, F64_ONE);
        var _d52_sc = cga_scalar_part(cga_geometric_product(_d52_ei, _d52_ej));
        var _d52_has = 0;
        if (f64_eq(_d52_sc, 0) == 0) { _d52_has = 1; }
        var _d52_want = 0;
        if (_d52_j == _d52_pi) { _d52_want = 1; }
        if (_d52_has != _d52_want) { _D52_PBAD = _D52_PBAD + 1; }
    }
}
assert_eq(_D52_PBAD, 0, "cga scalar partners: an involution, and the only pairs with a scalar product");
# A unit sphere at (2^k + 1, 0, 0) is stored exactly and its norm² is exactly 1, so its
# inverse is itself. It inverts out to k = 23; at k = 24, |c| = 2^24 r, it meets the
# bound (T = 2|c|^2 - 1 > 2^49). 3.3.3 returned the zero multivector from k = 13.
var _D52_URUN = 0;
var _D52_UBAD = 0;
for (var _d52_k = 0; _d52_k <= 23; _d52_k = _d52_k + 1) {
    var _d52_us = cga_sphere(f64_add((1023 + _d52_k) << 52, F64_ONE), 0, 0, F64_ONE);
    var _d52_ui = cga_blade_inverse(_d52_us);
    _D52_URUN = _D52_URUN + 1;
    for (var _d52_q = 1; _d52_q <= 5; _d52_q = _d52_q + 1) {
        if (cga_mv_get(_d52_ui, _d52_q) != cga_mv_get(_d52_us, _d52_q)) { _D52_UBAD = _D52_UBAD + 1; }
    }
}
assert_eq(_D52_URUN, 24, "the unit-sphere translation sweep actually ran 24 distances");
assert_eq(_D52_UBAD, 0, "cga_blade_inverse: a unit sphere at 2^k + 1 inverts to itself for k = 0..23");
var _d52_u24 = cga_blade_inverse(cga_sphere(f64_add((1023 + 24) << 52, F64_ONE), 0, 0, F64_ONE));
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(_d52_u24, 4), 0, "cga_blade_inverse: at |c| = 2^24 + 1 a unit sphere meets the null bound");
# Dilation: a sphere of centre 2^k c and radius 2^k r, r = 2^-12 |c|, all full mantissas.
# Its inverse is the k = 0 inverse with e_i scaled by 2^-k and n0 by 4^-k, bit for bit,
# for k = -480..480. 3.3.3 got 934 of these 961 wrong (the zero multivector).
var _d52_c1 = 0x3FEA997FDC52A43F;
var _d52_c2 = 0xBFDA6499B134A6EA;
var _d52_c3 = 0x3FD15CE084089A80;
var _d52_r = 0x3F35F0C8768A6994;
var _d52_i0 = cga_blade_inverse(cga_sphere(_d52_c1, _d52_c2, _d52_c3, _d52_r));
# Pinned, not an oracle: this is hisab's computed n0. The exact inverse of the stored
# blade has ...EA786E there, and of the intended sphere ...168CA6, both within the
# norm's conditioning; the sweep checks that every scale reproduces this value.
# [measured: Fraction inverse of the stored and the intended sphere, scratch, 2026-10-03]
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(_d52_i0, 4), 0x41610477D5E8D0BA, "pinned: hisab's n0 of the dilation sweep's unit-scale inverse");
var _D52_DRUN = 0;
var _D52_DBAD = 0;
for (var _d52_dk = 0 - 480; _d52_dk <= 480; _d52_dk = _d52_dk + 1) {
    var _d52_s = (1023 + _d52_dk) << 52;
    var _d52_inv_s = (1023 - _d52_dk) << 52;
    var _d52_inv_s2 = (1023 - 2 * _d52_dk) << 52;
    var _d52_ds = cga_sphere(f64_mul(_d52_c1, _d52_s), f64_mul(_d52_c2, _d52_s), f64_mul(_d52_c3, _d52_s),
      f64_mul(_d52_r, _d52_s));
    var _d52_di = cga_blade_inverse(_d52_ds);
    _D52_DRUN = _D52_DRUN + 1;
    for (var _d52_e = 1; _d52_e <= 3; _d52_e = _d52_e + 1) {
        if (cga_mv_get(_d52_di, _d52_e) != f64_mul(cga_mv_get(_d52_i0, _d52_e), _d52_inv_s)) {
            _D52_DBAD = _D52_DBAD + 1;
        }
    }
    if (cga_mv_get(_d52_di, 4) != f64_mul(cga_mv_get(_d52_i0, 4), _d52_inv_s2)) { _D52_DBAD = _D52_DBAD + 1; }
    if (cga_mv_get(_d52_di, 5) != cga_mv_get(_d52_i0, 5)) { _D52_DBAD = _D52_DBAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_D52_DRUN, 961, "the sphere dilation sweep actually ran 961 scales");
assert_eq(_D52_DBAD, 0, "cga_blade_inverse: a sphere's inverse is dilation-covariant from 2^-480 to 2^480");
# Origin spheres, radius 2^k r0 for k = -255..255: the inverse is n0 / r^2 - ninf / 2,
# n0 scaling by 4^-k and ninf fixed. 3.3.3 got 461 of these 511 wrong.
var _d52_r0 = 0x3FF3C0C5E2D83415;
var _d52_o0 = cga_blade_inverse(cga_sphere(0, 0, 0, _d52_r0));
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(_d52_o0, 4), 0x3FE4FECEBFF10568, "oracle: n0 of the origin-sphere sweep's unit inverse");
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(_d52_o0, 5), 0xBFE0000000000000, "oracle: ninf of the origin-sphere sweep's unit inverse");
var _D52_ORUN = 0;
var _D52_OBAD = 0;
for (var _d52_ok = 0 - 255; _d52_ok <= 255; _d52_ok = _d52_ok + 1) {
    var _d52_oi = cga_blade_inverse(cga_sphere(0, 0, 0, f64_mul(_d52_r0, (1023 + _d52_ok) << 52)));
    _D52_ORUN = _D52_ORUN + 1;
    if (cga_mv_get(_d52_oi, 4) != f64_mul(cga_mv_get(_d52_o0, 4), (1023 - 2 * _d52_ok) << 52)) {
        _D52_OBAD = _D52_OBAD + 1;
    }
    if (cga_mv_get(_d52_oi, 5) != cga_mv_get(_d52_o0, 5)) { _D52_OBAD = _D52_OBAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_D52_ORUN, 511, "the origin-sphere radius sweep actually ran 511 radii");
assert_eq(_D52_OBAD, 0, "cga_blade_inverse: an origin sphere inverts at every radius from 2^-255 to 2^255");
# ON the bound: a e1 + n0 + b ninf whose computed |norm²| EQUALS the computed bound
# (both 0x3E04000000000000) is null; one ulp lower in b, the norm² is above it. The
# tie is in computed values only: the exact norm², 5.869e-10, is 0.8% above the bound.
# [measured: Fraction norm² and bound of this blade, scratch, 2026-10-03]
var _d52_tb = cga_mv_zero();
cga_mv_set(_d52_tb, 1, 0x40794C583ADA5B59);
cga_mv_set(_d52_tb, 4, F64_ONE);
cga_mv_set(_d52_tb, 5, 0x40F3FFFFFFFFFFF6);
assert_eq(cga_norm_sq(_d52_tb), 0x3E04000000000000, "pinned: the tie fixture's computed norm²");
assert_eq(_cga_null_bound(_d52_tb), 0x3E04000000000000, "pinned: the tie fixture's computed null bound equals it");
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_blade_inverse(_d52_tb), 1), 0, "cga_blade_inverse: a norm² equal to the bound is null");
cga_mv_set(_d52_tb, 5, 0x40F3FFFFFFFFFFF5);
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_blade_inverse(_d52_tb), 1), 0x4263465B9A8DFC74,
  "cga_blade_inverse: one ulp lower in ninf, the norm² is above the bound and inverts");
# A null blade whose partner products have both signs: the dual of a point (grade 4).
# sum b_i b_partner(i) cancels to the size of the residual; T, of magnitudes, does not.
var _d52_dp = cga_blade_inverse(cga_dual(cga_point(0x3FF3A5C91E2B7D41, 0xBFE5E0F1A2B3C4D5, 0x4003C2B1A0F9E8D7)));
var _D52_DPNZ = 0;
for (var _d52_di2 = 0; _d52_di2 < 32; _d52_di2 = _d52_di2 + 1) {
    if (cga_mv_get(_d52_dp, _d52_di2) != 0) { _D52_DPNZ = _D52_DPNZ + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_D52_DPNZ, 0, "cga_blade_inverse: the dual of a point is null (T sums magnitudes, not signed products)");
# An infinite coefficient whose partner is 0 never enters the norm² (here e1 + Inf n0,
# norm² 1), and the bound is Inf * 0 = NaN, so the blade divides: its inverse is itself,
# Inf in n0 and 1 in e1, the limit as the n0 coefficient grows. 3.3.3 summed b_i^2 to
# +Inf and called it null (the zero multivector).
var _d52_ib = cga_mv_zero();
cga_mv_set(_d52_ib, 1, F64_ONE);
cga_mv_set(_d52_ib, 4, F64_POS_INF);
var _d52_ii = cga_blade_inverse(_d52_ib);
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(_d52_ii, 4), F64_POS_INF, "cga_blade_inverse(e1 + Inf n0): Inf in n0, not the zero multivector");
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(_d52_ii, 1), F64_ONE, "cga_blade_inverse(e1 + Inf n0): 1 in e1");
# cga_project inherits it: e1 onto the unit sphere at (1e4, 0, 0) is 1e4 * S, e1 part 1e8.
var _d52_e1 = cga_mv_zero();
cga_mv_set(_d52_e1, 1, F64_ONE);
var _d52_pj = cga_project(_d52_e1, cga_sphere(f64_from(10000), 0, 0, F64_ONE));
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(_d52_pj, 1), f64_from(100000000),
  "cga_project: e1 onto a unit sphere at 1e4 is 1e4 S, not 0 (3.3.3: the zero multivector)");

# === 3.3.3 R4ga: cga_blade_inverse with a norm² that is not finite ===
# cga_norm_sq now answers +Inf where 3.3.2 answered NaN (the "3.3.3 R4ga"
# compensated-sum block), and the relative null test reads Inf <= tol * Inf as
# true, so without its own guard this function called 2^600 e1, whose inverse is
# 2^-600 e1, a null blade: the zero multivector. It refuses instead, NaN in every
# coefficient, as 3.3.2 did; the projection onto that blade stays NaN with it.
var _r4ga_bigb = cga_mv_zero();
cga_mv_set(_r4ga_bigb, 1, (1023 + 600) << 52);                # 2^600 e1, finite
assert_eq(cga_norm_sq(_r4ga_bigb), 0x7FF0000000000000, "oracle: cga_norm_sq(2^600 e1) overflows to +Inf");
var _r4ga_bi = cga_blade_inverse(_r4ga_bigb);
var _r4ga_bin = 0;
for (var _r4ga_i = 0; _r4ga_i < 32; _r4ga_i = _r4ga_i + 1) {
    if (f64_eq(cga_mv_get(_r4ga_bi, _r4ga_i), cga_mv_get(_r4ga_bi, _r4ga_i)) == 0) { _r4ga_bin = _r4ga_bin + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_r4ga_bin, 32, "cga_blade_inverse(2^600 e1): NaN in all 32 coefficients, not the zero multivector");
var _r4ga_infb = cga_mv_zero();
cga_mv_set(_r4ga_infb, 1, 0x7FF0000000000000);
var _r4ga_bi2 = cga_mv_get(cga_blade_inverse(_r4ga_infb), 1);
assert_eq(f64_eq(_r4ga_bi2, _r4ga_bi2), 0, "cga_blade_inverse(+Inf e1): NaN, not the zero multivector");
# A norm² of -Inf as well: Inf n0 + ninf, whose two n0.ninf terms are -Inf.
var _r4ga_ninfb = cga_mv_zero();
cga_mv_set(_r4ga_ninfb, 4, 0x7FF0000000000000);
cga_mv_set(_r4ga_ninfb, 5, F64_ONE);
assert_eq(cga_norm_sq(_r4ga_ninfb), 0xFFF0000000000000, "oracle: cga_norm_sq(Inf n0 + ninf) = -Inf");
var _r4ga_bi3 = cga_mv_get(cga_blade_inverse(_r4ga_ninfb), 5);
assert_eq(f64_eq(_r4ga_bi3, _r4ga_bi3), 0,
  "cga_blade_inverse(Inf n0 + ninf), norm² -Inf: NaN, not the zero multivector");
var _r4ga_e1 = cga_mv_zero();
cga_mv_set(_r4ga_e1, 1, F64_ONE);
var _r4ga_pj = cga_mv_get(cga_project(_r4ga_e1, _r4ga_bigb), 1);
assert_eq(f64_eq(_r4ga_pj, _r4ga_pj), 0, "cga_project(e1, 2^600 e1): NaN, not a projection of 0");

# --- _epa_degenerate_normal. It runs only when GJK ends with FEWER THAN FOUR
# simplex vertices — an exactly tangent contact — so the ordinary EPA tests never
# reach it. Its two guards compare a SQUARED simplex edge length, and a squared
# cross product, against the unsquared 1e-12, so both bite as soon as the scene is
# small: a sphere of radius k touching a box at 2k has simplex edges of order k
# and their squares of order k^2.
# ⚠ The tangency normal is +x by continuity with the overlapping case, which is
# asserted above; here it must stay +x at every scale.
var _GE_RUN = 0;
var _GE_BAD = 0;
var _ge_n = alloc(8);
var _ge_d = alloc(8);
for (var _ge_e = 0; _ge_e < 150; _ge_e = _ge_e + 1) {
    var _ge_k = (1023 - _ge_e) << 52;
    var _ge_sph = hvec4_new(0, 0, 0, _ge_k);
    var _ge_box = _mpr_mkbox(f64_mul(F64_TWO, _ge_k), 0, 0, _ge_k, _ge_k, _ge_k);
    _GE_RUN = _GE_RUN + 1;
    if (gjk_epa_3d(&_mpr_sup_sphere, _ge_sph, &_mpr_sup_box, _ge_box,
        _ge_n, _ge_d) != 1) {
        _GE_BAD = _GE_BAD + 1;
    } else {
        if (HVec3_x(load64(_ge_n)) != 0x3FF0000000000000) { _GE_BAD = _GE_BAD + 1; }
        # ⚠ y is APPROXIMATE and x is EXACT, deliberately. EPA refines
        # iteratively, so the off-axis component carries its convergence residual
        # even at unit scale — a bit-exact 0 there fails on correct output at
        # every scale. The SIGN and magnitude of x is what discriminates.
        if (f64_gt(f64_abs(HVec3_y(load64(_ge_n))), LOOSE_TOL_M) == 1) {
            _GE_BAD = _GE_BAD + 1;
        }
    }
}
# ⚠ 150 decades, and the floor is MEASURED: the x component first goes wrong
# at 2^-158, where the support functions and the portal arithmetic reach their
# own limits rather than any guard. The defect being tested bites at ~2^-20,
# since the guard compares a SQUARED edge length against 1e-12.
# [measured: first failing decade 158 of 300, 2026-09-10]
assert_eq(_GE_RUN, 150, "the EPA tangency scale sweep actually ran 150 scales");
assert_eq(_GE_BAD, 0,
  "gjk_epa_3d: the tangent normal is exactly +x for scene scales 2^0 to 2^-149");

# ⭐ AND THE PATH THAT ACTUALLY REACHES `_epa_degenerate_normal`: GJK encloses the
# origin but the Minkowski difference has NO VOLUME, so the portal seed fails too.
# Two zero-thickness segments crossing at the origin do it — the hull is a planar
# parallelogram, so the contact normal is +/-z with depth exactly 0.
# ⚠ THE EXISTING UNIT-SCALE TEST FOR THIS CANNOT DISCRIMINATE, and a reachability
# probe proved it: replacing the whole function body with a constant +z passes
# every assertion in the tree, because they check only x = 0, y = 0 and unit
# length — and +z satisfies all three. Returning +x instead does fail them, so the
# function IS reached; it is the ASSERTIONS that were too weak, not the coverage.
# ⛔ Its two guards compare a SQUARED simplex edge length, and a squared cross
# product, against the unsquared 1e-12, so both bite once the scene is small.
# [measured: reachability probe with +z (passes) and +x (fails), 2026-09-10]
var _GD_RUN = 0;
var _GD_BAD = 0;
var _gd_n = alloc(8);
var _gd_d = alloc(8);
# The unit-scale answer, taken as the reference the whole sweep must reproduce.
gjk_epa_3d(&_mpr_sup_box, _mpr_mkbox(0, 0, 0, F64_ONE, 0, 0),
  &_mpr_sup_box, _mpr_mkbox(0, 0, 0, 0, F64_ONE, 0), _gd_n, _gd_d);
var _gd_ref_x = HVec3_x(load64(_gd_n));
var _gd_ref_y = HVec3_y(load64(_gd_n));
var _gd_ref_z = HVec3_z(load64(_gd_n));
# and it is a real unit vector, not the null HVec3 a failed path would leave.
assert(f64_approx_eq(f64_add(f64_add(f64_mul(_gd_ref_x, _gd_ref_x),
        f64_mul(_gd_ref_y, _gd_ref_y)), f64_mul(_gd_ref_z, _gd_ref_z)),
    F64_ONE, LOOSE_TOL_M) == 1,
  "the degenerate-simplex reference normal is a unit vector");
for (var _gd_e = 0; _gd_e < 150; _gd_e = _gd_e + 1) {
    var _gd_k = (1023 - _gd_e) << 52;
    var _gd_sa = _mpr_mkbox(0, 0, 0, _gd_k, 0, 0);
    var _gd_sb = _mpr_mkbox(0, 0, 0, 0, _gd_k, 0);
    _GD_RUN = _GD_RUN + 1;
    if (gjk_epa_3d(&_mpr_sup_box, _gd_sa, &_mpr_sup_box, _gd_sb,
        _gd_n, _gd_d) != 1) {
        _GD_BAD = _GD_BAD + 1;
    } else {
        # ⚠ NOT +/-z, and measuring said so: GJK ends this configuration with a
        # two-vertex simplex, so the code correctly takes its segment-perpendicular
        # fallback and returns (-1/sqrt2, -1/sqrt2, 0). The plane normal was my
        # assumption, not the geometry.
        # ⭐ The property that DOES hold, and the one that interrogates the guards,
        # is that the configuration is SELF-SIMILAR: scaling both segments scales
        # nothing about the direction, so the normal must be bit-identical at every
        # scale and the depth exactly zero.
        if (HVec3_x(load64(_gd_n)) != _gd_ref_x) { _GD_BAD = _GD_BAD + 1; }
        if (HVec3_y(load64(_gd_n)) != _gd_ref_y) { _GD_BAD = _GD_BAD + 1; }
        if (HVec3_z(load64(_gd_n)) != _gd_ref_z) { _GD_BAD = _GD_BAD + 1; }
        if (load64(_gd_d) != 0) { _GD_BAD = _GD_BAD + 1; }
    }
}
assert_eq(_GD_RUN, 150, "the degenerate-simplex sweep actually ran 150 scales");
assert_eq(_GD_BAD, 0,
  "gjk_epa_3d: the crossing-segment normal is bit-identical at scales to 2^-149");

# ⭐ AND A PLANAR degenerate simplex, which the crossing segments do NOT give.
# There GJK ends with two vertices, so the code takes its segment-perpendicular
# branch and the CROSS-PRODUCT test is never the deciding one — measured, two of
# the three EPA mutants survive that fixture. Two coplanar FLAT boxes give a
# genuinely planar hull, so the second-direction search runs and the plane normal
# is the closed form +/-z.
# [measured: 11-mutant run over geo_advanced.cyr, 2026-09-10]
var _GF_RUN = 0;
var _GF_BAD = 0;
var _gf_n = alloc(8);
var _gf_d = alloc(8);
for (var _gf_e = 0; _gf_e < 150; _gf_e = _gf_e + 1) {
    var _gf_k = (1023 - _gf_e) << 52;
    var _gf_a = _mpr_mkbox(0, 0, 0, _gf_k, _gf_k, 0);
    var _gf_b = _mpr_mkbox(f64_mul(_gf_k, F64_HALF), f64_mul(_gf_k, F64_HALF), 0,
      _gf_k, _gf_k, 0);
    _GF_RUN = _GF_RUN + 1;
    if (gjk_epa_3d(&_mpr_sup_box, _gf_a, &_mpr_sup_box, _gf_b, _gf_n, _gf_d) != 1) {
        _GF_BAD = _GF_BAD + 1;
    } else {
        # The affine hull is the z = 0 plane, so the normal is exactly +/-z.
        if (f64_approx_eq(f64_abs(HVec3_z(load64(_gf_n))), F64_ONE, LOOSE_TOL_M) != 1) {
            _GF_BAD = _GF_BAD + 1;
        }
        if (f64_gt(f64_abs(HVec3_x(load64(_gf_n))), LOOSE_TOL_M) == 1) {
            _GF_BAD = _GF_BAD + 1;
        }
        if (f64_gt(f64_abs(HVec3_y(load64(_gf_n))), LOOSE_TOL_M) == 1) {
            _GF_BAD = _GF_BAD + 1;
        }
        if (load64(_gf_d) != 0) { _GF_BAD = _GF_BAD + 1; }
    }
}
assert_eq(_GF_RUN, 150, "the planar degenerate sweep actually ran 150 scales");
assert_eq(_GF_BAD, 0,
  "gjk_epa_3d: coplanar flat boxes give a unit +/-z normal at scales to 2^-149");

# ⚠ ONE EPA MUTANT SURVIVES AND IS RECORDED. Reverting ONLY the loop that PICKS
# e1 (leaving the early-return guard repaired) passes both fixtures. That is a
# genuine equivalence for these hulls rather than a hole: the loop chooses which
# spanning edge to use, and for a planar or segment hull ANY non-degenerate edge
# spans the same affine hull, so the plane normal is unchanged. The observable
# consequence of the old test — e1 left at zero and the fabricated unit_x returned
# — belongs to the early-return guard, and that mutant IS killed above.
# [measured: 11-mutant run over geo_advanced.cyr, 10 killed / 1 survivor]

# --- time_of_impact. `speed` is a relative SPEED, dimensions L/T, so an absolute
# 1e-12 made "are these bodies approaching?" depend on metres-per-second versus
# kilometres-per-hour. Two unit spheres 5 apart close over 3 units of distance, so
# scaling the velocity by k scales the impact TIME by 1/k — and max_t with it.
var _GT_RUN = 0;
var _GT_BAD = 0;
var _gt_a = hvec4_new(0, 0, 0, F64_ONE);
var _gt_b = hvec4_new(f64_from(5), 0, 0, F64_ONE);
var _gt_t = alloc(8);
var _gt_n = alloc(8);
for (var _gt_e = 0; _gt_e < 300; _gt_e = _gt_e + 1) {
    var _gt_k = (1023 - _gt_e) << 52;
    var _gt_vel = hvec3_new(f64_neg(_gt_k), 0, 0);
    var _gt_max = f64_mul(f64_from(10), (1023 + _gt_e) << 52);   # 10/k
    _GT_RUN = _GT_RUN + 1;
    if (time_of_impact(&_mpr_sup_sphere, _gt_a, hvec3_zero(),
        &_mpr_sup_sphere, _gt_b, _gt_vel, _gt_max, _gt_t, _gt_n) != 1) {
        _GT_BAD = _GT_BAD + 1;
    } else {
        # t*k is the scale-free impact distance, and it is 3 for these spheres.
        var _gt_d = f64_mul(load64(_gt_t), _gt_k);
        if (f64_gt(_gt_d, F64_TWO) != 1) { _GT_BAD = _GT_BAD + 1; }
        if (f64_lt(_gt_d, f64_from(4)) != 1) { _GT_BAD = _GT_BAD + 1; }
    }
}
assert_eq(_GT_RUN, 300, "the time_of_impact speed sweep actually ran 300 scales");
assert_eq(_GT_BAD, 0,
  "time_of_impact: impact distance is 3 for closing speeds from 2^0 to 2^-299");
# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION: two bodies with exactly equal velocity never collide.
assert_eq(time_of_impact(&_mpr_sup_sphere, _gt_a, hvec3_zero(),
    &_mpr_sup_sphere, _gt_b, hvec3_zero(), f64_from(10), _gt_t, _gt_n), 0,
  "time_of_impact: an exactly zero relative velocity is still no impact");

test_group("collision_core 2.15.0: order, sign and inverse mass are scale-free");

# --- triangulate_polygon / _col_ring_is_convex. A square is a square at every
# size: 4 vertices, 2 triangles, 6 indices. Measured before the repair: at 2^-20
# it returned an EMPTY triangulation.
# [measured: docs/audit/2026-09-09-epsilon-open.json]
var _CC_RUN = 0;
var _CC_BAD = 0;
for (var _cc_e = 0; _cc_e < 250; _cc_e = _cc_e + 1) {
    var _cc_s = (1023 - _cc_e) << 52;
    var _cc_q = vec_new();
    vec_push(_cc_q, hvec2_new(0, 0));
    vec_push(_cc_q, hvec2_new(_cc_s, 0));
    vec_push(_cc_q, hvec2_new(_cc_s, _cc_s));
    vec_push(_cc_q, hvec2_new(0, _cc_s));
    _CC_RUN = _CC_RUN + 1;
    if (vec_len(triangulate_polygon(_cc_q, 4)) != 6) { _CC_BAD = _CC_BAD + 1; }
    # and the hull of the same four points is still all four of them.
    if (vec_len(convex_hull_2d(_cc_q, 4)) != 4) { _CC_BAD = _CC_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_CC_RUN, 250, "the polygon scale sweep actually ran 250 scales");
assert_eq(_CC_BAD, 0,
  "triangulate_polygon / convex_hull_2d: a square is a square from 2^0 to 2^-249");
# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION: a genuinely degenerate polygon must still produce nothing.
var _cc_line = vec_new();
vec_push(_cc_line, hvec2_new(0, 0));
vec_push(_cc_line, hvec2_new(F64_ONE, 0));
vec_push(_cc_line, hvec2_new(F64_TWO, 0));
assert_eq(vec_len(triangulate_polygon(_cc_line, 3)), 0,
  "triangulate_polygon: three colinear points still triangulate to nothing");

# ⚠ AND A CONCAVE POLYGON, because a convex one does not reach the defect.
# `_col_ring_is_convex` only decides which vertices BLOCK an ear, and a convex
# quad blocks nothing whatever the predicate says — measured, that mutant survived
# the square sweep. A reflex vertex is what makes the blocked set load-bearing.
# [measured: 5-mutant run over collision_core.cyr, 2 survivors before this]
var _CV_RUN = 0;
var _CV_BAD = 0;
for (var _cv_e = 0; _cv_e < 250; _cv_e = _cv_e + 1) {
    var _cv_s = (1023 - _cv_e) << 52;
    var _cv_p = vec_new();
    vec_push(_cv_p, hvec2_new(0, 0));
    vec_push(_cv_p, hvec2_new(f64_mul(F64_TWO, _cv_s), 0));
    vec_push(_cv_p, hvec2_new(f64_mul(F64_TWO, _cv_s), f64_mul(F64_TWO, _cv_s)));
    vec_push(_cv_p, hvec2_new(_cv_s, _cv_s));                 # the reflex vertex
    vec_push(_cv_p, hvec2_new(0, f64_mul(F64_TWO, _cv_s)));
    _CV_RUN = _CV_RUN + 1;
    if (vec_len(triangulate_polygon(_cv_p, 5)) != 9) { _CV_BAD = _CV_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_CV_RUN, 250, "the concave polygon sweep actually ran 250 scales");
assert_eq(_CV_BAD, 0,
  "triangulate_polygon: a concave 5-gon gives 3 triangles from 2^0 to 2^-249");

# ⚠ ONE COLLISION MUTANT SURVIVES AND IS RECORDED. Restoring `EPSILON_F64` in
# `_col_ring_is_convex` alone passes everything here, and that is a genuine
# behavioural equivalence rather than a test gap: the epsilon form can only ever
# under-report convexity, which marks MORE vertices blocked, and an over-blocked
# set is conservative — every containment check it adds is one that was already
# going to pass. It can never mark a reflex vertex unblocked, because a reflex
# vertex has cross < 0 and fails `cross > eps` for any non-negative eps.
# ⭐ The EAR test is not conservative in that way and its mutant IS killed above;
# the ring predicate is repaired so the two agree, and because `_col_point_in_tri`
# in this same file has always used the exact sign test on this same quantity.

# ⭐ THE COMPARATOR MUST BE A TOTAL ORDER, which a tolerance never is. Three
# points spaced closer than 1e-12 in x: with an epsilon-equality, a > b and b > c
# can both be false while a > c is true, and the sort built on it is undefined.
# Exact comparison is transitive by construction.
var _cc_t = vec_new();
vec_push(_cc_t, hvec2_new(0, 0));
vec_push(_cc_t, hvec2_new((1023 - 60) << 52, F64_ONE));
# ⚠ NOT (2^-59, 2): that is COLINEAR with the first two (both segments have slope
# 2^60), so a hull of 2 would be correct and the assertion would be testing the
# fixture rather than the code. The first draft of this line made exactly that
# mistake and "failed" against correct output.
vec_push(_cc_t, hvec2_new((1023 - 59) << 52, 0));
assert_eq(vec_len(convex_hull_2d(_cc_t, 3)), 3,
  "convex_hull_2d: three points 2^-60 apart are three distinct hull vertices");
# ⭐ AND THE CASE THAT ACTUALLY BREAKS A NON-TRANSITIVE COMPARATOR: a convex ring
# whose ENTIRE x-extent fits inside one epsilon. Then every pair compares "equal"
# in x under the old rule, the sort degenerates to y alone, and monotone chain is
# fed an order that is not lexicographic at all. Eight points on a circle of
# ⚠ THE RADIUS IS MEASURED, NOT PICKED. Non-transitivity needs ADJACENT pairs
# inside one epsilon while DISTANT pairs are outside it — then a > b and b > c are
# both false while a > c is true. At 2^-38 the ring spans several epsilons and the
# old comparator loses points; at 2^-41 and below EVERY pair is within one epsilon,
# the relation degenerates to an exact order on y alone, and the mutant survives.
# [measured: baseline and mutant re-run at radii 2^-38 / 2^-41 / 2^-44]
var _cc_r = vec_new();
for (var _cc_i = 0; _cc_i < 16; _cc_i = _cc_i + 1) {
    var _cc_th = f64_mul(f64_from(_cc_i),
      f64_div(f64_mul(F64_TWO, F64_PI), f64_from(16)));
    var _cc_rad = (1023 - 38) << 52;
    vec_push(_cc_r, hvec2_new(f64_mul(_cc_rad, f64_cos(_cc_th)),
        f64_mul(_cc_rad, f64_sin(_cc_th))));
}
assert_eq(vec_len(convex_hull_2d(_cc_r, 16)), 16,
  "convex_hull_2d: sixteen points on a 2^-38 circle are all hull vertices");

# --- sequential_impulse. lambda = (1+e)*pen / inv_mass, so with inv_mass = 2k and
# pen = 0.5 the impulse is 0.25/k — it GROWS as the masses grow. The shipped guard
# skipped the contact entirely below 1e-12 and left the output slot at +0.0,
# indistinguishable from a real zero-impulse contact.
var _CI_RUN = 0;
var _CI_BAD = 0;
for (var _ci_e = 0; _ci_e < 400; _ci_e = _ci_e + 1) {
    var _ci_k = (1023 - _ci_e) << 52;
    var _ci_c = contact_new(0, 1, hvec3_unit_y(), F64_HALF, 0, 0, _ci_k, _ci_k);
    var _ci_cs = vec_new();
    vec_push(_ci_cs, _ci_c);
    var _ci_out = alloc(16);
    store64(_ci_out, 0);
    var _r31t, _r31v = sequential_impulse(_ci_cs, 1, 10, _ci_out);
    _CI_RUN = _CI_RUN + 1;
    # 0.25 / k, i.e. 0.25 * 2^e — exact at every power of two.
    if (f64_approx_eq(f64_div(load64(_ci_out), f64_div(F64_ONE, _ci_k)),
        f64_div(F64_ONE, f64_from(4)), LOOSE_TOL_M) != 1) {
        _CI_BAD = _CI_BAD + 1;
    }
}
assert_eq(_CI_RUN, 400, "the sequential_impulse mass sweep actually ran 400 scales");
assert_eq(_CI_BAD, 0,
  "sequential_impulse: lambda = 0.25/k for inverse masses from 2^0 to 2^-399");
# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION: two genuinely static bodies must still be skipped.
var _ci_st = contact_new(0, 1, hvec3_unit_y(), F64_HALF, 0, 0, 0, 0);
var _ci_scs = vec_new();
vec_push(_ci_scs, _ci_st);
var _ci_sout = alloc(16);
store64(_ci_sout, 0);
var _r32t, _r32v = sequential_impulse(_ci_scs, 1, 10, _ci_sout);
assert_eq(load64(_ci_sout), 0,
  "sequential_impulse: two exactly static bodies are still skipped");

test_group("2.15.0: tridiagonal pivots and optimizer curvature are scale-free");

# --- num_tridiag_solve. T = [[2,1,0],[1,2,1],[0,1,2]] with x = (1,1,1) gives
# d = (3,4,3). Scaling BOTH the matrix and the right-hand side by k leaves x
# unchanged — the same linear system in different units.
var _TD_RUN = 0;
var _TD_BAD = 0;
for (var _td_e = 0; _td_e < 500; _td_e = _td_e + 1) {
    var _td_k = (1023 - _td_e) << 52;
    var _td_a = alloc(24); var _td_b = alloc(24);
    var _td_c = alloc(24); var _td_d = alloc(24); var _td_x = alloc(24);
    # ⚠ The sub-diagonal is indexed `a_i = a[i-1]`, so its first entry is a[0] —
    # NOT a[1]. The first draft of this fixture offset it by one and failed 1500
    # of 2000 checks against correct code.
    store64(_td_a, _td_k); store64(_td_a + 8, _td_k); store64(_td_a + 16, 0);
    store64(_td_c, _td_k); store64(_td_c + 8, _td_k); store64(_td_c + 16, 0);
    for (var _td_i = 0; _td_i < 3; _td_i = _td_i + 1) {
        store64(_td_b + _td_i * 8, f64_mul(_td_k, F64_TWO));
        store64(_td_x + _td_i * 8, 0);
    }
    store64(_td_d, f64_mul(_td_k, f64_from(3)));
    store64(_td_d + 8, f64_mul(_td_k, f64_from(4)));
    store64(_td_d + 16, f64_mul(_td_k, f64_from(3)));
    _TD_RUN = _TD_RUN + 1;
    var _ri5t, _ri5v = num_tridiag_solve(_td_a, _td_b, _td_c, _td_d, 3, _td_x);
    if (is_err_result(_ri5t) == 1) {
        _TD_BAD = _TD_BAD + 1;
    }
    # ⚠ The n == 1 FAST PATH has its own pivot guard and the n = 3 sweep never
    # reaches it — that mutant survived until this line existed.
    # [measured: 9-mutant run over the solver guards, 2026-09-10]
    var _td_1x = alloc(8);
    store64(_td_1x, 0);
    var _ri6t, _ri6v = num_tridiag_solve(_td_a, _td_b, _td_c, _td_d, 1, _td_1x);
    if (is_err_result(_ri6t) == 1) {
        _TD_BAD = _TD_BAD + 1;
    }
    if (f64_approx_eq(f64_div(load64(_td_1x), f64_div(f64_from(3), F64_TWO)),
        F64_ONE, LOOSE_TOL_M) != 1) {
        _TD_BAD = _TD_BAD + 1;
    }
    for (var _td_j = 0; _td_j < 3; _td_j = _td_j + 1) {
        if (f64_approx_eq(load64(_td_x + _td_j * 8), F64_ONE, LOOSE_TOL_M) != 1) {
            _TD_BAD = _TD_BAD + 1;
        }
    }
}
assert_eq(_TD_RUN, 500, "the tridiagonal scale sweep actually ran 500 scales");
assert_eq(_TD_BAD, 0,
  "num_tridiag_solve: x = (1,1,1) for the same system at scales 2^0 to 2^-499");
# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION: a genuinely singular pivot must still be refused, and `out`
# must be left as the caller had it.
var _td_sa = alloc(8); var _td_sb = alloc(8);
var _td_sc = alloc(8); var _td_sd = alloc(8); var _td_sx = alloc(8);
store64(_td_sa, 0); store64(_td_sb, 0); store64(_td_sc, 0);
store64(_td_sd, F64_ONE); store64(_td_sx, 0x4059000000000000);
var _rq106t, _rq106v = num_tridiag_solve(_td_sa, _td_sb, _td_sc, _td_sd, 1, _td_sx);
assert_eq(is_err_result(_rq106t), 1, "num_tridiag_solve: an exactly zero pivot is still refused");
assert_eq(_rq106v, HSB_ERR_DIVISION_BY_ZERO, "num_tridiag_solve: an exactly zero pivot is still refused");
assert_eq(load64(_td_sx), 0x4059000000000000,
  "num_tridiag_solve: a refusal leaves the caller's out-array untouched");

# --- the optimizers. ⚠ SCALE THE VARIABLES, NOT THE OBJECTIVE, and the first
# draft of this fixture got that wrong. With f = k*((x0-1)^2 + ...) the gradient
# scales by k while the DISTANCE to the minimum does not, so a steepest-descent
# step of size ~k cannot traverse a distance of ~1 — measured, at k = 2^-60 the
# step is below the ULP of x and `x` never moves at all, for any iteration budget.
# That is arithmetic, not a guard, and the fixture was asking for the impossible.
# ⭐ The honest statement of "the same problem in different units" scales the
# VARIABLES: f(x) = (x0 - k)^2 + (x1 - 2k)^2 has its minimum at (k, 2k), and
# gradient, distance and step all scale together. `sy` then goes as k^2, so the
# shipped 1e-12 guard starts rejecting the curvature update around k = 1e-6.
# [measured: probe over decades 0/60/200/400, both before and after the fix]
var _OS_K = F64_ONE;
# ⚠ ILL-CONDITIONED ON PURPOSE, and the first draft was not. A round bowl
# converges by steepest descent alone, so BFGS still reached the minimum with its
# curvature update rejected at every step and all five optimizer mutants SURVIVED.
# Condition number 10^5 makes the quasi-Newton update load-bearing within the
# 400-iteration budget: with it, a handful of steps; without it, far more than
# the budget allows. ⚠ The choice is MEASURED, not guessed — 10^4 is too easy
# (the gamma mutant survives it) and 10^6 too hard (the baseline itself fails).
# [measured: baseline re-run at 10^4 / 10^5 / 10^6, 2026-09-10]
var _OS_C = 0x40F86A0000000000;                      # 100000.0
fn _os_f(x) {
    var a = f64_sub(load64(x), _OS_K);
    var b = f64_sub(load64(x + 8), f64_mul(F64_TWO, _OS_K));
    return f64_add(f64_mul(a, a), f64_mul(_OS_C, f64_mul(b, b)));
}
fn _os_g(x, out) {
    var a = f64_sub(load64(x), _OS_K);
    var b = f64_sub(load64(x + 8), f64_mul(F64_TWO, _OS_K));
    store64(out, f64_mul(F64_TWO, a));
    store64(out + 8, f64_mul(F64_TWO, f64_mul(_OS_C, b)));
    return 0;
}
var _OS_RUN = 0;
var _OS_BAD = 0;
for (var _os_e = 0; _os_e < 200; _os_e = _os_e + 1) {
    _OS_K = (1023 - _os_e) << 52;
    var _os_tol = f64_mul(_OS_K, f64_div(F64_ONE, f64_from(100000000)));
    for (var _os_s = 1; _os_s < 4; _os_s = _os_s + 1) {
        var _os_x0 = alloc(16);
        store64(_os_x0, f64_neg(f64_mul(_OS_K, F64_HALF)));
        store64(_os_x0 + 8, f64_mul(_OS_K, f64_div(f64_from(5), F64_TWO)));
        var _os_ox = alloc(16); var _os_of = alloc(8);
        # ⚠ 3.0.0 — same dispatch shape as the bowl sweep above: each arm binds
        # both halves and records success in a flag, so an unmatched `_os_s`
        # still counts as a failure the way the `0 - 99` sentinel did.
        var _os_ok = 0;
        if (_os_s == 1) {
            var _ost1, _osv1 = opt_conjugate_gradient(&_os_f, &_os_g, _os_x0, 2, _os_tol,
              400, _os_ox, _os_of);
            _os_ok = is_ok(_ost1);
        }
        if (_os_s == 2) {
            var _ost2, _osv2 = opt_bfgs(&_os_f, &_os_g, _os_x0, 2, _os_tol, 400,
              _os_ox, _os_of);
            _os_ok = is_ok(_ost2);
        }
        if (_os_s == 3) {
            var _ost3, _osv3 = opt_lbfgs(&_os_f, &_os_g, _os_x0, 2, 5, _os_tol, 400,
              _os_ox, _os_of);
            _os_ok = is_ok(_ost3);
        }
        _OS_RUN = _OS_RUN + 1;
        if (_os_ok != 1) { _OS_BAD = _OS_BAD + 1; }
        # Compare the RATIO, since the answer itself now spans 200 decades.
        if (f64_approx_eq(f64_div(load64(_os_ox), _OS_K), F64_ONE, LOOSE_TOL_M) != 1) {
            _OS_BAD = _OS_BAD + 1;
        }
        if (f64_approx_eq(f64_div(load64(_os_ox + 8), _OS_K), F64_TWO, LOOSE_TOL_M) != 1) {
            _OS_BAD = _OS_BAD + 1;
        }
    }
}
_OS_K = F64_ONE;
assert_eq(_OS_RUN, 600, "the optimizer scale sweep ran 3 solvers x 200 scales");
assert_eq(_OS_BAD, 0,
  "cg / bfgs / lbfgs: the minimum is (k, 2k) for variable scales 2^0 to 2^-199");

var _GV_A = 0x3FF0000000000000;
fn _sv_mv1(x) {
    var y = alloc(8);
    store64(y, f64_mul(_GV_A, load64(x)));
    return y;
}
fn _sv_mv2(x) {
    var y = alloc(24);
    var i = 0;
    while (i < 3) { store64(y + i * 8, f64_mul(F64_TWO, load64(x + i * 8))); i = i + 1; }
    return y;
}

test_group("2.17.0 solvers: the convergence norms are scale-free");

# ⛔ EVERY CONVERGENCE TEST IN `optimize.cyr` AND `linalg_ext.cyr` IS `norm < tol`,
# AND THE NORM SQUARED BEFORE SUMMING. A gradient, residual or Krylov vector whose
# largest component falls below 2^-538 has a norm of exactly 0, so the test fires
# on the first iteration and the solver returns **HSB_ERR_NONE with `out_x` still
# equal to `x0`**. That is not a wrong number; it is a confident success at the
# starting point, which is strictly worse because the caller has no signal.
#
# ⚠ THE FIXTURE SCALES THE OBJECTIVE / THE RESIDUALS, NOT THE VARIABLES. Scaling
# the variables scales the gradient AND the distance to the minimum together, so
# the step falls below the ULP of x and the run stalls for a reason that is not
# this defect — the trap the 2.15.0 sweep above documents.
# ⚠ AND IT ASSERTS THE FABRICATION, NOT THE ANSWER. Multiplying an objective by a
# positive constant leaves its argmin exactly where it was, but it does NOT leave
# the natural STEP SIZE alone: steepest descent moves by −α·g, which is degree one
# in the objective's scale, so far below 1 the solver genuinely cannot reach the
# minimum and honestly says so. What must never happen is claiming it did.
# Measured on the 2.16.0 tree: **446 of 1001 objective scales for conjugate
# gradient and 461 of 1001 residual scales for Levenberg-Marquardt returned
# HSB_ERR_NONE with x untouched.** Both are 0 now.
# [measured: 1001-scale objective and residual sweeps, before and after]
var _SV_RUN = 0;
var _SV_SILENT = 0;
var _SV_C = 0x3FF0000000000000;
var _SV_K = 0x40F86A0000000000;                   # 1e5 — ill-conditioned on purpose
fn _sv_f(x) {
    var a = f64_sub(load64(x), F64_ONE);
    var b = f64_sub(load64(x + 8), F64_TWO);
    return f64_mul(_SV_C, f64_add(f64_mul(a, a), f64_mul(_SV_K, f64_mul(b, b))));
}
fn _sv_g(x, out) {
    var a = f64_sub(load64(x), F64_ONE);
    var b = f64_sub(load64(x + 8), F64_TWO);
    store64(out, f64_mul(_SV_C, f64_mul(F64_TWO, a)));
    store64(out + 8, f64_mul(_SV_C, f64_mul(F64_TWO, f64_mul(_SV_K, b))));
    return 0;
}
# Rosenbrock as least squares, residuals scaled by _SV_C. Fresh buffer per call;
# no longer required (3.3.4: the solver copies each residual vector).
fn _sv_res(x) {
    var o = alloc(16);
    var a = load64(x);
    var b = load64(x + 8);
    store64(o, f64_mul(_SV_C, f64_mul(f64_from(10), f64_sub(b, f64_mul(a, a)))));
    store64(o + 8, f64_mul(_SV_C, f64_sub(F64_ONE, a)));
    return o;
}
fn _sv_jac(x, out) {
    store64(out, f64_mul(_SV_C, f64_neg(f64_mul(f64_from(20), load64(x)))));
    store64(out + 8, f64_mul(_SV_C, f64_from(10)));
    store64(out + 16, f64_mul(_SV_C, f64_neg(F64_ONE)));
    store64(out + 24, 0);
    return 0;
}
for (var _sv_e = 0; _sv_e < 1001; _sv_e = _sv_e + 1) {
    _SV_C = (1023 - _sv_e) << 52;                 # 2^-e
    var _sv_tol = f64_mul(_SV_C, 0x3EB0C6F7A0B5ED8D);          # C * 1e-6
    _SV_RUN = _SV_RUN + 1;

    # conjugate gradient: x0 = (-0.5, 2.5), minimum at (1, 2)
    var _sv_x0 = alloc(16);
    store64(_sv_x0, f64_neg(F64_HALF));
    store64(_sv_x0 + 8, 0x4004000000000000);
    var _sv_ox = alloc(16);
    var _sv_of = alloc(8);
    var _sv_rc_t, _sv_rc_v = opt_conjugate_gradient(&_sv_f, &_sv_g, _sv_x0, 2, _sv_tol, 400,
      _sv_ox, _sv_of);
    if (is_ok(_sv_rc_t) == 1 && load64(_sv_ox) == f64_neg(F64_HALF)) {
        _SV_SILENT = _SV_SILENT + 1;
    }

    # gradient descent / bfgs / lbfgs all converge through `_opt_norm` rather
    # than `_opt_norm_ss`, so they cover the third call site.
    var _sv_gx = alloc(16);
    store64(_sv_gx, f64_neg(F64_HALF));
    store64(_sv_gx + 8, 0x4004000000000000);
    var _sv_gox = alloc(16);
    var _sv_gof = alloc(8);
    var _svg_t, _svg_v = opt_gradient_descent(&_sv_f, &_sv_g, _sv_gx, 2, 0x3F1A36E2EB1C432D,
      _sv_tol, 400, _sv_gox, _sv_gof);
    if (is_ok(_svg_t) == 1 && load64(_sv_gox) == f64_neg(F64_HALF)) {
        _SV_SILENT = _SV_SILENT + 1;
    }
    var _sv_box = alloc(16);
    var _sv_bof = alloc(8);
    var _svb_t, _svb_v = opt_bfgs(&_sv_f, &_sv_g, _sv_gx, 2, _sv_tol, 400, _sv_box, _sv_bof);
    if (is_ok(_svb_t) == 1 && load64(_sv_box) == f64_neg(F64_HALF)) {
        _SV_SILENT = _SV_SILENT + 1;
    }
    var _sv_lbx = alloc(16);
    var _sv_lbf = alloc(8);
    var _svl_t, _svl_v = opt_lbfgs(&_sv_f, &_sv_g, _sv_gx, 2, 5, _sv_tol, 400, _sv_lbx, _sv_lbf);
    if (is_ok(_svl_t) == 1 && load64(_sv_lbx) == f64_neg(F64_HALF)) {
        _SV_SILENT = _SV_SILENT + 1;
    }

    # Levenberg-Marquardt: x0 = (-1.2, 1), minimum at (1, 1)
    var _sv_lx0 = alloc(16);
    store64(_sv_lx0, f64_neg(0x3FF3333333333333));
    store64(_sv_lx0 + 8, F64_ONE);
    var _sv_lox = alloc(16);
    store64(_sv_lox, 0);
    var _sv_lrc_t, _sv_lrc_v = opt_levenberg_marquardt(&_sv_res, &_sv_jac, _sv_lx0, 2, 2,
      _sv_tol, 300, _sv_lox);
    if (is_ok(_sv_lrc_t) == 1 && load64(_sv_lox) == f64_neg(0x3FF3333333333333)) {
        _SV_SILENT = _SV_SILENT + 1;
    }
}
_SV_C = 0x3FF0000000000000;
assert_eq(_SV_RUN, 1001, "the solver objective/residual sweep actually ran 1001 scales");
assert_eq(_SV_SILENT, 0,
  "cg / gd / bfgs / lbfgs / lm: never report HSB_ERR_NONE while leaving x at x0");

# ⭐ `solve_gmres` — the residual norm, measured end to end on A = 2I where the
# answer is b/2 at every scale and the caller scales `tol` with the problem as a
# careful caller would. Measured on the 2.16.0 tree: 461 of 1001 wrong, first at
# 2^-539. ⚠ A Krylov solver has a second failure mode the optimisers do not: the
# line after the convergence test is `1 / r_norm`, so a fabricated zero norm does
# not merely stop early, it normalises by infinity.
# [measured: 1001-scale sweep on solve_gmres before and after, 2026-09-10]
var _GM_RUN = 0;
var _GM_BAD = 0;
for (var _gm_e = 0; _gm_e < 1001; _gm_e = _gm_e + 1) {
    var _gm_k = (1023 - _gm_e) << 52;
    var _gm_b = alloc(24);
    store64(_gm_b, f64_mul(F64_TWO, _gm_k));
    store64(_gm_b + 8, f64_mul(f64_from(4), _gm_k));
    store64(_gm_b + 16, f64_mul(f64_from(6), _gm_k));
    var _gm_x0 = alloc(24);
    store64(_gm_x0, 0);
    store64(_gm_x0 + 8, 0);
    store64(_gm_x0 + 16, 0);
    var _gm_tol = f64_mul(_gm_k, 0x3CD203AF9EE75616);          # k * 1e-15
    var _gm_x = solve_gmres(&_sv_mv2, _gm_b, _gm_x0, 3, _gm_tol, 20, 3);
    _GM_RUN = _GM_RUN + 1;
    if (_gm_x == 0) { _GM_BAD = _GM_BAD + 1; }
    if (_gm_x != 0) {
        if (f64_gt(f64_abs(f64_div(f64_sub(load64(_gm_x), _gm_k), _gm_k)),
            0x3D06849B86A12B9B) == 1) { _GM_BAD = _GM_BAD + 1; }
    }
}
assert_eq(_GM_RUN, 1001, "the gmres residual sweep actually ran 1001 scales");
assert_eq(_GM_BAD, 0,
  "solve_gmres: A = 2I gives x = b/2 for b from 2^0 down to 2^-1000");

# ⭐ AND THE GIVENS RADIUS ON ITS OWN, ISOLATED SO IT CANNOT BORROW THE FIX ABOVE.
# n = 1, b = [1], x0 = [0], A = a*I. Then r = b so `_lext_norm` sees 1 exactly,
# and wj = A*v0 - h00*v0 = 0 so it sees 0 exactly — NEITHER call is near the flush
# band, and the only remaining scale-carrying quantity is the Givens radius
# sqrt(hjj² + hj1j²) with hjj = a. Measured: with `_lext_norm` repaired and this
# line left alone, **463 of 1001 still fail**, first at a = 2^-538 returning x = 0
# where the answer is 2^538. ⚠ In the silent middle the stored rotation is not
# orthogonal (cs² + sn² != 1), which no residual check downstream would catch.
# [measured: isolating sweep with each half of the repair applied alone]
var _GV_RUN = 0;
var _GV_BAD = 0;
for (var _gv_e = 0; _gv_e < 1001; _gv_e = _gv_e + 1) {
    _GV_A = (1023 - _gv_e) << 52;
    var _gv_b = alloc(8);
    store64(_gv_b, F64_ONE);
    var _gv_x0 = alloc(8);
    store64(_gv_x0, 0);
    var _gv_x = solve_gmres(&_sv_mv1, _gv_b, _gv_x0, 1, 0x3CD203AF9EE75616, 20, 1);
    _GV_RUN = _GV_RUN + 1;
    if (_gv_x == 0) { _GV_BAD = _GV_BAD + 1; }
    if (_gv_x != 0 && load64(_gv_x) != (1023 + _gv_e) << 52) { _GV_BAD = _GV_BAD + 1; }
}
_GV_A = 0x3FF0000000000000;
assert_eq(_GV_RUN, 1001, "the isolated Givens sweep actually ran 1001 scales");
assert_eq(_GV_BAD, 0,
  "solve_gmres: n=1, A = a*I gives x = 1/a bit-exactly for a from 2^0 to 2^-1000");

# ⛔ `cmat_inverse` FABRICATED "SINGULAR" FOR 498 OF 1010 EXPONENTS. Its pivot
# search tracked `cx_norm_sq` — a SQUARED modulus — and then compared it against
# two degree-ONE quantities, so a perfectly invertible matrix below 2^-512 was
# rejected outright. ⚠ Three lines had to move together: measured, repairing only
# the open-coded `cx_abs` (the column-scale `f64_sqrt(cx_norm_sq(...))` in the
# pivot search, linalg_ext.cyr:1219 before this repair) takes 489 of 2001 to 488.
# [measured: 1010-exponent cmat_inverse sweep before and after, 2026-09-10]
var _CI_RUN = 0;
var _CI_BAD = 0;
for (var _ci_e = 0; _ci_e < 1010; _ci_e = _ci_e + 1) {
    var _ci_a = (1023 - _ci_e) << 52;
    var _ci_m = cmat_new(2, 2);
    cmat_set(_ci_m, 0, 0, cx_new(_ci_a, 0));
    cmat_set(_ci_m, 0, 1, cx_zero());
    cmat_set(_ci_m, 1, 0, cx_zero());
    cmat_set(_ci_m, 1, 1, cx_new((1023 - _ci_e + 1) << 52, 0));
    var _ci_v = cmat_inverse(_ci_m);
    _CI_RUN = _CI_RUN + 1;
    if (_ci_v == 0) { _CI_BAD = _CI_BAD + 1; }
    if (_ci_v != 0 && HComplex_re(cmat_get(_ci_v, 0, 0)) != (1023 + _ci_e) << 52) {
        _CI_BAD = _CI_BAD + 1;
    }
}
assert_eq(_CI_RUN, 1010, "the cmat_inverse exponent sweep actually ran 1010 scales");
assert_eq(_CI_BAD, 0,
  "cmat_inverse: diag(2^-e, 2^-(e+1)) inverts exactly for e from 0 to 1009");
# ⚠ AND A MIXED-MAGNITUDE MATRIX, which a diagonal sweep cannot produce: the
# pivot column and the column scale sit 20 binades apart, so this exercises the
# RELATIVE test rather than the DBL_MIN floor.
var _ci_mm = cmat_new(2, 2);
cmat_set(_ci_mm, 0, 0, cx_new((1023 + 500) << 52, 0));
cmat_set(_ci_mm, 0, 1, cx_new((1023 + 520) << 52, 0));
cmat_set(_ci_mm, 1, 0, cx_zero());
cmat_set(_ci_mm, 1, 1, cx_new(F64_ONE, 0));
assert_neq(cmat_inverse(_ci_mm), 0,
  "cmat_inverse: [[2^500, 2^520], [0, 1]] is invertible, not singular");
# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION: a genuinely singular matrix must still be rejected, or the
# repair is a deletion wearing a comment.
var _ci_s = cmat_new(2, 2);
cmat_set(_ci_s, 0, 0, cx_new(F64_ONE, 0));
cmat_set(_ci_s, 0, 1, cx_new(F64_TWO, 0));
cmat_set(_ci_s, 1, 0, cx_new(F64_TWO, 0));
cmat_set(_ci_s, 1, 1, cx_new(f64_from(4), 0));
assert_eq(cmat_inverse(_ci_s), 0, "cmat_inverse: [[1,2],[2,4]] is still singular");
var _ci_z = cmat_new(2, 2);
cmat_set(_ci_z, 0, 0, cx_zero());
cmat_set(_ci_z, 0, 1, cx_zero());
cmat_set(_ci_z, 1, 0, cx_zero());
cmat_set(_ci_z, 1, 1, cx_zero());
assert_eq(cmat_inverse(_ci_z), 0, "cmat_inverse: the all-zero matrix is still singular");

# ⭐ THE TWO n-LEG NORM HELPERS, TESTED DIRECTLY. The solver sweeps above prove
# the CONSEQUENCE — no silent success, right answer at every scale — but they
# cannot discriminate the helpers' own bands, and measurement says so: six of
# thirteen mutants survived the consequence tests alone. A Krylov solve on A = 2I
# converges in one iteration whatever the residual norm reads, so a norm that is
# 33% wrong still produces the right x; only the norm itself shows it.
# ⚠ These are internal helpers and are called by name on purpose. That is what the
# suite already does for `_col_dl_incircle` and friends, and it is the only way to
# aim at a band the public path smooths over.
# ⭐ The fixture is the Pythagorean quadruple (3, 4, 12 | 13): every leg and the
# hypotenuse are exact at every power of two, so this compares bit for bit.
# [measured: 13-mutant run, 6 survived the solver sweeps and die here, 2026-09-10]
var _NH_RUN = 0;
var _NH_BAD = 0;
var _nh_v = alloc(24);
for (var _nh_e = 0 - 1020; _nh_e <= 1020; _nh_e = _nh_e + 1) {
    var _nh_k = (1023 + _nh_e) << 52;
    store64(_nh_v, f64_mul(f64_from(3), _nh_k));
    store64(_nh_v + 8, f64_mul(f64_from(4), _nh_k));
    store64(_nh_v + 16, f64_mul(f64_from(12), _nh_k));
    var _nh_w = f64_mul(f64_from(13), _nh_k);
    _NH_RUN = _NH_RUN + 1;
    if (_lext_norm(_nh_v, 3) != _nh_w) { _NH_BAD = _NH_BAD + 1; }
    if (_opt_norm(_nh_v, 3) != _nh_w) { _NH_BAD = _NH_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_NH_RUN, 2041, "the n-leg norm helper sweep actually ran 2041 scales");
assert_eq(_NH_BAD, 0,
  "_lext_norm / _opt_norm: |(3k,4k,12k)| = 13k bit-exact from 2^-1020 to 2^1020");

# ⚠ THE LOSSY SUBNORMAL BAND, which a power-of-two sweep cannot reach. For a
# single component v the sum is v², and for v = 1.5*2^-538 that is 0.5625*2^-1074,
# which ROUNDS to 1*2^-1074 — so the naive sqrt reads 2^-537 against a truth of
# 0.75*2^-537, **33% high**. A trigger narrowed from `< F64_TINY` to `== 0` passes
# the whole sweep above and fails here, which is exactly what it did.
# [measured: 13-mutant run over optimize.cyr + linalg_ext.cyr, 2026-09-10]
store64(_nh_v, f64_mul(f64_from(3), (1023 - 539) << 52));
store64(_nh_v + 8, 0);
store64(_nh_v + 16, 0);
assert_eq(_lext_norm(_nh_v, 3), f64_mul(f64_from(3), (1023 - 539) << 52),
  "_lext_norm: a lone component whose square is a LOSSY subnormal is exact");
assert_eq(_opt_norm(_nh_v, 3), f64_mul(f64_from(3), (1023 - 539) << 52),
  "_opt_norm: the same");

# ⚠ AND THE OVERFLOW END, where the SUM is +Inf but the answer is an ordinary
# double. |(2^600, 2^600, 0)| = 2^600*sqrt(2); without the +Inf rescue the naive
# sum is +Inf and the norm comes back +Inf for a perfectly representable vector.
store64(_nh_v, (1023 + 600) << 52);
store64(_nh_v + 8, (1023 + 600) << 52);
store64(_nh_v + 16, 0);
assert_eq(_lext_norm(_nh_v, 3), f64_mul((1023 + 600) << 52, 0x3FF6A09E667F3BCD),
  "_lext_norm: a sum that overflows still gives 2^600*sqrt(2), not +Inf");
assert_eq(_opt_norm(_nh_v, 3), f64_mul((1023 + 600) << 52, 0x3FF6A09E667F3BCD),
  "_opt_norm: the same");
# ⭐ An ACTUALLY infinite component must still give +Inf, and that needs the exit
# inside the scaled helper — `Inf/Inf` is NaN.
store64(_nh_v, F64_POS_INF);
store64(_nh_v + 8, F64_ONE);
store64(_nh_v + 16, 0);
assert_eq(_lext_norm(_nh_v, 3), F64_POS_INF, "_lext_norm: +Inf in, +Inf out");
assert_eq(_opt_norm(_nh_v, 3), F64_POS_INF, "_opt_norm: +Inf in, +Inf out");
# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION plus the NaN contract. NaN never reaches the scaled helper —
# a NaN sum satisfies neither trigger — so it falls through to f64_sqrt(NaN).
store64(_nh_v, 0);
store64(_nh_v + 8, 0);
assert_eq(_lext_norm(_nh_v, 3), 0, "_lext_norm: the zero vector still has norm 0");
assert_eq(_opt_norm(_nh_v, 3), 0, "_opt_norm: the zero vector still has norm 0");
store64(_nh_v, 0x7FF8000000000000);
var _nh_n1 = _lext_norm(_nh_v, 3);
var _nh_n2 = _opt_norm(_nh_v, 3);
assert_eq(f64_eq(_nh_n1, _nh_n1), 0, "_lext_norm: NaN propagates");
assert_eq(f64_eq(_nh_n2, _nh_n2), 0, "_opt_norm: NaN propagates");

# === 3.3.4 krylov: eigen_power's m * 2^e helpers (linalg_ext) ===
# _lext_xexp / _lext_xman split a double exactly into m * 2^e with 0.5 <= |m| < 1, and
# _lext_xld rounds m * 2^e back ONCE. Below 2^-1022 it forms (m * 2^(e + 1074)) * 2^-1074;
# going through 2^-1022 first rounds twice. Expected bits from exact rational rounding
# (Fractions, ties to even). m = 0x3FE95F2E4FA69611 at e = -1026 is 223172716475569 *
# 2^-1074 rounded once, ...568 rounded twice.
# [measured: krylov double-rounding search, 200000 random (m, e), python3 fractions, 2026-10-03]
var _k4x_m = 0x3FE95F2E4FA69611;
assert_eq(_lext_xld(_k4x_m, 0 - 1026), 0x0000CAF9727D34B1, "_lext_xld: one rounding below 2^-1022 (twice gives ...B0)");
assert_eq(_lext_xld(_k4x_m, 0 - 1022), 0x000CAF9727D34B08, "_lext_xld: e = -1022, the one-multiply edge");
assert_eq(_lext_xld(_k4x_m, 0 - 1060), 0x00000000000032BE, "_lext_xld: e = -1060");
assert_eq(_lext_xld(_k4x_m, 0 - 1074), 1, "_lext_xld: e = -1074 rounds up to 2^-1074");
assert_eq(_lext_xld(_k4x_m, 0 - 1075), 0, "_lext_xld: e = -1075 rounds to +0");
assert_eq(_lext_xld(f64_neg(_k4x_m), 0 - 3000), 0x8000000000000000,
  "_lext_xld: far below the range keeps the sign, -0");
# The early return has to catch e = -2098: there the factor 2^(e + 1074) would carry the
# biased exponent -1, and (-1) << 52 is the bit pattern of -Inf.
assert_eq(_lext_xld(f64_neg(_k4x_m), 0 - 2098), 0x8000000000000000,
  "_lext_xld: e = -2098 keeps the sign, -0 (the shifted factor would be -Inf)");
assert_eq(_lext_xld(0x3FE8000000000000, 1024), 0x7FE8000000000000, "_lext_xld: e = 1024, 0.75 * 2^1024 = 1.5 * 2^1023");
assert_eq(_lext_xld(0xBFE8000000000000, 1025), 0xFFF0000000000000, "_lext_xld: e = 1025 overflows to -Inf");
assert_eq(_lext_xld(0x8000000000000000, 5), 0x8000000000000000, "_lext_xld: -0 stays -0");
assert_eq(_lext_xexp(3), 0 - 1072, "_lext_xexp: 3 * 2^-1074 = 0.75 * 2^-1072");
assert_eq(_lext_xman(3), 0x3FE8000000000000, "_lext_xman: 3 * 2^-1074 -> m = 0.75");
assert_eq(_lext_xexp(0xC018000000000000), 3, "_lext_xexp: -6 = -0.75 * 2^3");
assert_eq(_lext_xman(0xC018000000000000), 0xBFE8000000000000, "_lext_xman: -6 -> m = -0.75");
assert_eq(_lext_xman(0x8000000000000000), 0x8000000000000000, "_lext_xman: -0 -> m = -0");
assert_eq(_lext_xexp(0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF), 1024, "_lext_xexp: DBL_MAX = (1 - 2^-53) * 2^1024");
assert_eq(_lext_xld(_lext_xman(0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF), _lext_xexp(0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF)), 0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF,
  "_lext_xld(_lext_xman(x), _lext_xexp(x)) == x at DBL_MAX");
assert_eq(_lext_xld(_lext_xman(1), _lext_xexp(1)), 1, "_lext_xld(_lext_xman(x), _lext_xexp(x)) == x at 2^-1074");
# _lext_power_wide reads A's entries through _lext_xexp / _lext_xman, which are for finite
# doubles; eigen_power never calls it on a non-finite A (its plain run cannot converge
# there), and it refuses one itself rather than read +Inf as 0.5 * 2^1025.
var _k4x_A = ganita_mat_new(2, 2);
ganita_mat_set(_k4x_A, 0, 0, F64_ONE);
ganita_mat_set(_k4x_A, 0, 1, F64_POS_INF);
ganita_mat_set(_k4x_A, 1, 1, F64_TWO);
var _k4x_val = alloc(8);
var _k4x_vec = alloc(16);
assert_eq(_lext_power_wide(_k4x_A, 2, 50, _k4x_val, _k4x_vec), HSB_ERR_NO_CONVERGENCE,
  "_lext_power_wide: an infinite entry -> NO_CONVERGENCE, not a finite answer");
ganita_mat_set(_k4x_A, 0, 1, F64_ONE);
assert_eq(_lext_power_wide(_k4x_A, 2, 200, _k4x_val, _k4x_vec), 0, "_lext_power_wide: [[1,1],[0,2]] converges");
assert_eq(f64_lt(f64_abs(f64_sub(load64(_k4x_val), F64_TWO)), 0x3D819799812DEA11), 1,
  "_lext_power_wide: [[1,1],[0,2]] lambda == 2 to 2e-12");

# ⚠ TWO OPTIMIZER MUTANTS SURVIVE THIS BLOCK AND ARE RECORDED, NOT HIDDEN.
# (a) Replacing the BFGS curvature test with a BARE SIGN TEST (`sy > 0`) passes
#     everything here. That is a real gap, not an equivalence: the census measured
#     a near-orthogonal pair with sy = 1e-300 at ||s|| = ||y|| = 1 — catastrophic
#     cancellation in the dot product rather than smallness of scale — producing
#     rho = 1e300 and a -inf that poisons q and hence x. A CONVEX QUADRATIC can
#     never generate it, because y = A*s makes sy = s'As > 0 for positive-definite
#     A; it needs an indefinite region, which this fixture has none of.
# (b) Restoring the gamma guard to `EPSILON_F64` on `yy` also survives. That one is
#     a convergence-RATE effect, and measured, L-BFGS reaches this minimum inside
#     40 iterations with or without the initial Hessian scaling — tightening the
#     budget to 40 does not separate them either.
# Both repairs are kept because they are correctly typed (a dimensionless cosine,
# and the quantity actually divided); neither is claimed to be proven by the suite.
# [measured: 9-mutant run at condition 10^5, budgets 400 and 40, 2026-09-10]

fn _ab_ode_dydt(t, y) { return y; }

# --- linearize_depth_reverse_z: near/ndc, and ndc is a normalised depth the
# caller's projection produces. ⛔ TWO defects on one line: the absolute 1e-12,
# and a comment whose meaning was INVERTED ("At far plane" — in reverse-Z, ndc = 0
# IS the infinite far plane and linear depth 0 is at the eye).
# ⚠ Not F64_TINY either, and that is measured: near/ndc stays FINITE for subnormal
# ndc across most of that band, so DBL_MIN would still fabricate 0 where the
# division succeeds. Only an exact zero has no answer.
# [measured: docs/audit/2026-09-09-epsilon-open.json]
var _LD_RUN = 0;
var _LD_BAD = 0;
# ⚠ 1023 scales, not 500: F64_TINY sits at 2^-1022, so a 500-deep sweep cannot
# tell it from the exact-zero test at all — that mutant survived a 500-deep one.
# [measured: 9-mutant run over the four sharpened sites, 2026-09-10]
for (var _ld_e = 0; _ld_e < 1023; _ld_e = _ld_e + 1) {
    var _ld_ndc = (1023 - _ld_e) << 52;               # 2^-e
    var _ld_want = (1023 + _ld_e) << 52;              # 2^+e = 1/ndc, near = 1
    _LD_RUN = _LD_RUN + 1;
    if (linearize_depth_reverse_z(_ld_ndc, F64_ONE) != _ld_want) { _LD_BAD = _LD_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_LD_RUN, 1023, "the reverse-Z depth sweep actually ran 1023 scales");
assert_eq(_LD_BAD, 0,
  "linearize_depth_reverse_z: near/ndc is exact for ndc from 2^0 to 2^-1022");
# ⭐ AND ONE STEP BELOW DBL_MIN, which the loop above cannot express: `(1023-e)<<52`
# encodes NORMAL powers of two only, so it stops exactly AT DBL_MIN — where `<`
# does not fire and F64_TINY is indistinguishable from the exact-zero test. A
# SUBNORMAL ndc is the case that separates them, and near/ndc = 2^1023 is finite
# there, so the division genuinely succeeds and the guard must stay silent.
# ⚠ 2^-1023 is the deepest that works: at 2^-1025 the reciprocal itself overflows.
assert_eq(linearize_depth_reverse_z(0x0008000000000000, F64_ONE), (1023 + 1023) << 52,
  "linearize_depth_reverse_z: a subnormal ndc still divides, exactly");

assert_eq(linearize_depth_reverse_z(0, F64_ONE), 0,
  "linearize_depth_reverse_z: ndc exactly 0 is the infinite far plane");

# --- f64_fmod: the header said "Returns 0 if y == 0" and the code tested
# |y| < 1e-12 (until 2.15.0). On this family the result is exact at every
# representable scale (the trunc form's was too; it was not exact in general, see
# the f64_fmod group), so any threshold above zero fabricates a 0 over a region
# that computes correctly.
var _FM_RUN = 0;
var _FM_BAD = 0;
# ⚠ Past 1022 the divisor is SUBNORMAL, which is exactly where a DBL_MIN guard
# and the exact-zero test stop agreeing — a 500-deep sweep left that mutant alive.
for (var _fm_e = 0; _fm_e < 1040; _fm_e = _fm_e + 1) {
    var _fm_y = 0;
    if (_fm_e < 1023) { _fm_y = (1023 - _fm_e) << 52; }
    if (_fm_e >= 1023) { _fm_y = 1 << (1074 - _fm_e); }   # subnormal 2^-e
    var _fm_h = f64_mul(_fm_y, F64_HALF);             # 0.5 * y, exact for 2^-e
    _FM_RUN = _FM_RUN + 1;
    # 2.5*y mod y = 0.5*y exactly, at every scale.
    if (f64_fmod(f64_add(f64_mul(F64_TWO, _fm_y), _fm_h), _fm_y) != _fm_h) {
        _FM_BAD = _FM_BAD + 1;
    }
    # and an exact multiple is exactly 0.
    if (f64_fmod(f64_mul(f64_from(3), _fm_y), _fm_y) != 0) { _FM_BAD = _FM_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_FM_RUN, 1040, "the f64_fmod divisor sweep actually ran 1040 scales");
assert_eq(_FM_BAD, 0,
  "f64_fmod: 2.5y mod y = 0.5y and 3y mod y = 0 for y from 2^0 into the subnormals");
assert_eq(f64_fmod(F64_ONE, 0), 0, "f64_fmod: y = 0 returns 0, as the header says");
# ⚠ NEGATIVE ZERO — asserted as a CONTRACT, not as a discriminator. IEEE-754 makes
# -0.0 compare equal to +0.0, so the bit test and `f64_eq(y, 0)` are equivalent
# mutants here; this pins the behaviour either way. An earlier draft of the comment
# beside the guard claimed the bit form was needed to catch -0.0. It was wrong.
assert_eq(f64_fmod(F64_ONE, 0x8000000000000000), 0,
  "f64_fmod: negative zero is also a zero divisor");

# --- ode_dopri45_trajectory: `tf - t` is a span in the caller's TIME units, and
# an absolute 1e-12 made a short integration return rc = 0 with a plausible SHORT
# trajectory — it had silently stopped at the first step. The direct property is
# that the integration REACHES tf.
var _OT_RUN = 0;
var _OT_BAD = 0;
for (var _ot_e = 0; _ot_e < 300; _ot_e = _ot_e + 1) {
    var _ot_T = (1023 - _ot_e) << 52;                 # total span 2^-e
    var _ot_ts = vec_new();
    var _ot_ys = vec_new();
    var _ot_t, _ot_v = ode_dopri45_trajectory(&_ab_ode_dydt, 0, F64_ONE, _ot_T,
      f64_mul(_ot_T, f64_div(F64_ONE, f64_from(10))), F64_HALF, _ot_ts, _ot_ys);
    _OT_RUN = _OT_RUN + 1;
    if (is_ok(_ot_t) == 0) { _OT_BAD = _OT_BAD + 1; }
    # It must take at least one real step, not just push the initial condition.
    if (vec_len(_ot_ts) < 2) { _OT_BAD = _OT_BAD + 1; }
    # and the last time recorded must actually be tf.
    if (vec_len(_ot_ts) >= 2) {
        if (vec_get(_ot_ts, vec_len(_ot_ts) - 1) != _ot_T) { _OT_BAD = _OT_BAD + 1; }
    }
}
assert_eq(_OT_RUN, 300, "the ode span sweep actually ran 300 scales");
assert_eq(_OT_BAD, 0,
  "ode_dopri45_trajectory: reaches tf exactly for spans from 2^0 down to 2^-299");
# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION, and it is the assertion the new non-advancement check exists
# for: a step too small to move `t` must be reported, not looped on. abuse.tcyr
# already pins the rc; this pins that it is now detected at the FIRST step rather
# than after exhausting a 100,000-step budget.
# [measured: tests/abuse.tcyr dt0 = 0 case, rc and vec_len both checked]
var _ot_z1 = vec_new();
var _ot_z2 = vec_new();
var _rq41t, _rq41v = ode_dopri45_trajectory(&_ab_ode_dydt, 0, F64_ONE, F64_ONE, 0, F64_HALF,
  _ot_z1, _ot_z2);
assert_eq(is_err_result(_rq41t), 1, "ode_dopri45_trajectory: dt0 = 0 still reports no convergence");
assert_eq(_rq41v, HSB_ERR_NO_CONVERGENCE, "ode_dopri45_trajectory: dt0 = 0 still reports no convergence");
assert_lte(vec_len(_ot_z1), 2,
  "ode_dopri45_trajectory: and it now says so at the first step, not the 100000th");

test_group("2.15.0: inverse_lerp / world_to_screen / unpremultiply / newton");

# --- hisab_inverse_lerp: the interval WIDTH is in the caller's units (metres,
# seconds, kelvin), and the midpoint of any interval is 0.5 of the way along it.
# ⛔ The fallback returned 0.0, which is ALSO the right answer for value == a, so
# the caller could not tell a refusal from a result.
var _IL_RUN = 0;
var _IL_BAD = 0;
for (var _il_e = 0; _il_e < 500; _il_e = _il_e + 1) {
    var _il_w = (1023 - _il_e) << 52;                 # 2^-e
    var _il_h = (1023 - _il_e - 1) << 52;             # exactly half of it
    _IL_RUN = _IL_RUN + 1;
    if (hisab_inverse_lerp(0, _il_w, _il_h) != 0x3FE0000000000000) {
        _IL_BAD = _IL_BAD + 1;
    }
}
assert_eq(_IL_RUN, 500, "the inverse_lerp width sweep actually ran 500 scales");
assert_eq(_IL_BAD, 0,
  "hisab_inverse_lerp: the midpoint is 0.5 for widths from 2^0 down to 2^-499");
assert_eq(hisab_inverse_lerp(F64_TWO, F64_TWO, F64_TWO), 0,
  "hisab_inverse_lerp: a genuinely zero-width interval still returns 0");

# --- world_to_screen: the MVP's overall scale is a unit choice; the origin
# projects to the middle of the viewport whatever that scale is.
var _WS_RUN = 0;
var _WS_BAD = 0;
for (var _ws_e = 0; _ws_e < 500; _ws_e = _ws_e + 1) {
    var _ws_k = (1023 - _ws_e) << 52;
    var _ws_m = m4_new();
    m4_set(_ws_m, 0, 0, _ws_k); m4_set(_ws_m, 1, 1, _ws_k);
    m4_set(_ws_m, 2, 2, _ws_k); m4_set(_ws_m, 3, 3, _ws_k);
    var _ws_p = world_to_screen(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), _ws_m, f64_from(800), f64_from(600));
    _WS_RUN = _WS_RUN + 1;
    if (HVec3_x(_ws_p) != 0x4079000000000000) { _WS_BAD = _WS_BAD + 1; }   # 400.0
    if (HVec3_y(_ws_p) != 0x4072C00000000000) { _WS_BAD = _WS_BAD + 1; }   # 300.0
}
assert_eq(_WS_RUN, 500, "the world_to_screen sweep actually ran 500 scales");
assert_eq(_WS_BAD, 0,
  "world_to_screen: the origin lands at (400,300) for MVP scales to 2^-499");

# --- unpremultiply_alpha: alpha is the homogeneous weight. A faint but real
# coverage must recover the colour, not collapse to transparent black.
var _UA_RUN = 0;
var _UA_BAD = 0;
for (var _ua_e = 0; _ua_e < 500; _ua_e = _ua_e + 1) {
    var _ua_a = (1023 - _ua_e) << 52;
    var _ua_h = (1023 - _ua_e - 1) << 52;             # 0.5 * a, premultiplied
    var _ua_c = unpremultiply_alpha(_ua_h, _ua_h, _ua_h, _ua_a);
    _UA_RUN = _UA_RUN + 1;
    if (HVec4_x(_ua_c) != 0x3FE0000000000000) { _UA_BAD = _UA_BAD + 1; }
    if (HVec4_w(_ua_c) != _ua_a) { _UA_BAD = _UA_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_UA_RUN, 500, "the unpremultiply sweep actually ran 500 alphas");
assert_eq(_UA_BAD, 0,
  "unpremultiply_alpha: 0.5 grey is recovered at alphas from 2^0 to 2^-499");
assert_eq(HVec4_x(unpremultiply_alpha(0, 0, 0, 0)), 0,
  "unpremultiply_alpha: alpha exactly 0 is still transparent black");

# --- num_newton: f'(x) has dimensions [f]/[x], so an absolute 1e-12 made Newton
# refuse any problem whose units make the derivative small. ⚠ Unlike the rest of
# this class it does NOT fabricate — it returns HSB_ERR_DIVISION_BY_ZERO and
# leaves `out` unwritten — so the defect is a false REFUSAL. Still wrong, still a
# public entry point. f(x) = k(x^2 - 2) has the root sqrt(2) at every k, and
# f'(1) = 2k crosses 1e-12 at k = 5e-13.
# ⚠ `tol` is compared against |f(x)|, so it must scale with the function too —
# otherwise the solver "converges" instantly at x0 and the fixture tests nothing.
var _NN_K = F64_ONE;
fn _nn_zero_f(x) { return F64_ONE; }
fn _nn_zero_df(x) { return 0; }
fn _nn_f(x) { return f64_mul(_NN_K, f64_sub(f64_mul(x, x), F64_TWO)); }
fn _nn_df(x) { return f64_mul(_NN_K, f64_mul(F64_TWO, x)); }

var _NN_RUN = 0;
var _NN_BAD = 0;
var _nn_out = alloc(8);
for (var _nn_e = 0; _nn_e < 300; _nn_e = _nn_e + 1) {
    _NN_K = (1023 - _nn_e) << 52;                    # 2^-e
    var _nn_tol = f64_mul(_NN_K, f64_div(F64_ONE, f64_from(10000000000)));
    store64(_nn_out, 0);
    var _nn_rc_t, _nn_rc_v = num_newton(&_nn_f, &_nn_df, F64_ONE, _nn_tol, 100, _nn_out);
    _NN_RUN = _NN_RUN + 1;
    if (is_ok(_nn_rc_t) == 0) { _NN_BAD = _NN_BAD + 1; }
    if (f64_approx_eq(load64(_nn_out), f64_sqrt(F64_TWO), LOOSE_TOL_M) != 1) {
        _NN_BAD = _NN_BAD + 1;
    }
}
_NN_K = F64_ONE;
assert_eq(_NN_RUN, 300, "the num_newton scale sweep actually ran 300 scales");
assert_eq(_NN_BAD, 0,
  "num_newton: finds sqrt(2) for f = k(x^2-2) at every k from 2^0 to 2^-299");
# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION: a genuinely zero derivative must still be refused, and `out`
# must be left alone rather than written with a fabricated root.
var _nn_flat = alloc(8);
store64(_nn_flat, 0x4059000000000000);               # 100.0, a sentinel
var _rq107t, _rq107v = num_newton(&_nn_zero_f, &_nn_zero_df, F64_ONE, EPSILON_F64, 10, _nn_flat);
assert_eq(is_err_result(_rq107t), 1, "num_newton: an exactly zero derivative is still refused");
assert_eq(_rq107v, HSB_ERR_DIVISION_BY_ZERO, "num_newton: an exactly zero derivative is still refused");
assert_eq(load64(_nn_flat), 0x4059000000000000,
  "num_newton: a refusal leaves the caller's out-parameter untouched");

# ================================================================
# 2.16.0 — the small-angle series: the four maps 2.15.0 could not repair
# ================================================================
# ⛔ 2.15.0 DEFERRED THESE BECAUSE LOWERING THE GUARD ALONE WOULD HAVE MADE THEM
# WORSE. `se3_exp` and `se3_log` divide by theta^2 and theta^3, and at
# theta <= 2.2e-162 both `theta*theta` and `1 - cos(theta)` are exactly 0, so a
# DBL_MIN guard turns the coefficient into 0/0 = NaN. The coefficients had to be
# made computable at small theta first — that is what this release is.
#
# ⛔ AND THE LOG MAPS HAD A SECOND, LARGER DEFECT THE GUARD WAS HIDING. Both
# recovered the angle with `acos`: `su2_log` as acos(w) with w = cos(theta/2), and
# `so3_log` as acos((tr-1)/2). For theta below ~1.5e-8 those arguments round to
# EXACTLY 1.0, acos returns 0, and the entire rotation is gone — not degraded,
# gone. Measured on the 2.15.0 tree: `su2_log(su2_exp((0,0,t)))` recovers t down to
# 2^-24 and returns exactly 0 from 2^-28 down.
# ⭐ The information lives in the SINE, which keeps full relative precision near 0
# where the cosine keeps none, so the angle now comes from `atan2`. Measured
# bit-exact to 2^-300, and it fixes theta > pi as a side effect, which acos could
# not represent at all.
# [measured: round-trip probes before and after, 2026-09-10]

test_group("lie 2.16.0: the small-angle series and the atan2 log maps");

# --- ROUND TRIP. log(exp(w)) is w for every rotation, at every angle.
var _LS_RUN = 0;
var _LS_BAD = 0;
# ⭐ 1022 DECADES — THE WHOLE NORMAL RANGE. The history of this one number is the
# history of three releases: through 2.15.0 the floor was **2^-26**, because both
# log maps took the angle from `acos` of a value that rounds to exactly 1.0;
# 2.16.0's `atan2` moved it to **2^-537**, where a COMPONENT SQUARED underflows;
# and 2.17.0's scaled norms move it to **2^-1022**, which is DBL_MIN — below that
# the quaternion components themselves are subnormal and the limit is f64's, not
# this code's. ⚠ Each of those floors was invisible until the one above it was
# repaired, which is why the sweep depth is re-derived every time rather than
# carried forward.
# [measured: floor bisect after each repair — 537 before 2.17.0, 1022 after]
for (var _ls_e = 0; _ls_e < 1022; _ls_e = _ls_e + 1) {
    var _ls_t = (1023 - _ls_e) << 52;
    var _ls_w = hvec3_new(0, 0, _ls_t);
    _LS_RUN = _LS_RUN + 1;
    # ⚠ Compare the RATIO: the angle itself spans 500 decades.
    var _ls_su = HVec3_z(su2_log(su2_exp(_ls_w)));
    if (f64_approx_eq(f64_div(_ls_su, _ls_t), F64_ONE, LOOSE_TOL_M) != 1) {
        _LS_BAD = _LS_BAD + 1;
    }
    var _ls_so = HVec3_z(so3_log(so3_exp(_ls_w)));
    if (f64_approx_eq(f64_div(_ls_so, _ls_t), F64_ONE, LOOSE_TOL_M) != 1) {
        _LS_BAD = _LS_BAD + 1;
    }
}
assert_eq(_LS_RUN, 1022, "the log/exp round-trip actually ran 1022 angles");
assert_eq(_LS_BAD, 0,
  "su2_log / so3_log: log(exp(w)) recovers w for angles from 2^0 down to 2^-1021");

# ⭐ 2.17.0 — `su2_from_quat` NORMALISES, so it carries the same norm and the same
# defect. A quaternion whose components are 2^-600 is perfectly well formed; the
# raw sum of squares makes its norm 0 and the function hands back the IDENTITY.
var _ln_q = su2_from_quat(hquat_new((1023 - 600) << 52, 0, 0, 0));
assert_eq(_su2_x(_ln_q), 0x3FF0000000000000,
  "su2_from_quat: a 2^-600 quaternion normalises to a unit x, not the identity");
assert_eq(_su2_w(_ln_q), 0,
  "su2_from_quat: and its w is 0, which the identity fallback would have made 1");
# ⛔ 3.3.4 — AND BELOW DBL_MIN THE GUARD STILL GAVE THE IDENTITY: through 3.3.3 `norm <
# F64_TINY` returned it for every non-zero quaternion whose scaled norm is subnormal, 52
# of 52 binades of (2^-k, 0, 0, 0), k = 1023..1074 (audit D086). Now that band normalises
# q * 2^1023, an exact copy, so the answer is the copy's bits: pinned for a full-mantissa
# q at |q| ~ 2^-1025, whose four components are within 0.99 ulp of the Decimal oracle.
# [measured: scratch harness and Decimal oracle, not reproducible in-tree, 2026-10-03; cycc 6.6.14]
var _LQ_RUN = 0;
var _LQ_BAD = 0;
for (var _lq_k = 0; _lq_k < 52; _lq_k = _lq_k + 1) {
    var _lq_u = su2_from_quat(hquat_new(1 << (51 - _lq_k), 0, 0, 0));   # 2^-1023 .. 2^-1074
    _LQ_RUN = _LQ_RUN + 1;
    if (_su2_x(_lq_u) != F64_ONE) { _LQ_BAD = _LQ_BAD + 1; }
    if (_su2_w(_lq_u) != 0) { _LQ_BAD = _LQ_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_LQ_RUN, 52, "the su2_from_quat subnormal sweep ran 52 magnitudes");
assert_eq(_LQ_BAD, 0, "su2_from_quat: (2^-k, 0, 0, 0) is x = 1 for k = 1023..1074 (3.3.3: the identity)");
var _lq_f = su2_from_quat(hquat_new(0x0001ABCDEF012345, 0x80009876543210FF, 0x00004567890ABCDE, 0x0000FEDCBA987654));
var _lq_c = su2_from_quat(hquat_new(0x3FCABCDEF0123450, 0xBFB30ECA86421FE0, 0x3FA159E242AF3780, 0x3FBFDB97530ECA80));
assert_eq(_su2_w(_lq_f), 0x3FDF0BC2FBC3B83F, "su2_from_quat, subnormal full-mantissa q: w (3.3.3: 1)");
assert_eq(_su2_x(_lq_f), 0x3FEA0E714BD03FE4, "su2_from_quat, subnormal full-mantissa q: x (3.3.3: 0)");
assert_eq(_su2_y(_lq_f), 0xBFD29276ED777F84, "su2_from_quat, subnormal full-mantissa q: y (3.3.3: 0)");
assert_eq(_su2_z(_lq_f), 0x3FC0E8B0E673DF86, "su2_from_quat, subnormal full-mantissa q: z (3.3.3: 0)");
var _lq_diff = 0;
for (var _lq_i = 0; _lq_i < 4; _lq_i = _lq_i + 1) {
    if (load64(_lq_c + _lq_i * 8) != load64(_lq_f + _lq_i * 8)) { _lq_diff = _lq_diff + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_lq_diff, 0, "su2_from_quat: all four words are the bits of q * 2^1023, normalised in range");
assert_eq(_su2_w(su2_from_quat(hquat_new(0, 0, 0, 0))), F64_ONE,
  "su2_from_quat: the zero quaternion still returns the identity");

# === 3.3.3 R5lie: su2_from_quat past the norm's overflow ===
# ⛔ 3.3.3 — the other end of the same norm. `_lie_norm4` is mx * sqrt(sum of (c/mx)^2),
# and once |q| passes DBL_MAX that product is +Inf with every component finite; inv was
# 1/Inf = 0 and the answer the ZERO quaternion, which su2_log read as the identity. Only
# that overflow is rescaled now. Expected bits checked against an 80-digit Decimal oracle.
# [measured: scratch harness and Decimal oracle, not reproducible in-tree, 2026-10-01; cycc 6.6.12]
var _R5L_DMAX = 0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
var _r5l_q = su2_from_quat(hquat_new(_R5L_DMAX, _R5L_DMAX, 0, 0));
assert_eq(_su2_x(_r5l_q), 0x3FE6A09E667F3BCC,
  "su2_from_quat(DBL_MAX, DBL_MAX, 0, 0): x is 1/sqrt(2) (oracle ...BCD, 0.56 ulp; 3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(_su2_y(_r5l_q), 0x3FE6A09E667F3BCC, "su2_from_quat(DBL_MAX, DBL_MAX, 0, 0): y as x (3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(_su2_w(_r5l_q) | _su2_z(_r5l_q), 0, "su2_from_quat(DBL_MAX, DBL_MAX, 0, 0): w and z are +0");
assert_eq(_su2_x(su2_from_quat(hquat_new(F64_ONE, F64_ONE, 0, 0))), 0x3FE6A09E667F3BCC,
  "su2_from_quat(1, 1, 0, 0) gives the same x bits (control: the rescale lands on the in-range answer)");
# su2_log reads it as the half turn about (1, 1, 0) it is: pi/sqrt(2) in x and y.
var _r5l_ql = su2_log(_r5l_q);
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r5l_ql), 0x4001C5831ADD62E4, "su2_log of it: x is pi/sqrt(2) (oracle-nearest; 3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_r5l_ql), 0x4001C5831ADD62E4, "su2_log of it: y is pi/sqrt(2) (3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_twist_omega(se3_log(se3_new(_r5l_q, hvec3_new(F64_ONE, F64_TWO, f64_from(3)))))),
  0x4001C5831ADD62E4, "se3_log over it: omega x is pi/sqrt(2) (3.3.2: 0, with v = t)");
# A full-mantissa quaternion in all four slots lands on the bits of the same direction
# scaled by 2^-1000 into range (each within 0.43 ulp of the oracle).
var _r5l_h = su2_from_quat(hquat_new(0x7FE5555555555555, 0xFFEABCDEF0123456,
    0x7FC2468ACE13579B, 0x7FD0FEDCBA987654));
assert_eq(_su2_w(_r5l_h), 0x3FCE9AEEC4EEA436, "su2_from_quat, huge full-mantissa q: w (3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(_su2_x(_r5l_h), 0x3FE335525FBF8C8D, "su2_from_quat, huge full-mantissa q: x (3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(_su2_y(_r5l_h), 0xBFE8130B05A6B286, "su2_from_quat, huge full-mantissa q: y (3.3.2: -0)");
assert_eq(_su2_z(_r5l_h), 0x3FC07481718CF446, "su2_from_quat, huge full-mantissa q: z (3.3.2: 0)");
# ON the boundary, with x = DBL_MAX: y_lo is the last y whose norm is finite and y_hi the
# next double. y_lo still runs the old lines, bit for bit; y_hi takes the rescale.
var _R5L_YLO = 0x7E53988E1409212D;
var _R5L_YHI = 0x7E53988E1409212E;
assert_eq(_lie_norm4(0, _R5L_DMAX, _R5L_YLO, 0), _R5L_DMAX, "boundary fixture: |q| at y_lo is DBL_MAX (precondition)");
assert_eq(_lie_norm4(0, _R5L_DMAX, _R5L_YHI, 0), 0x7FF0000000000000,
  "boundary fixture: |q| at y_hi overflows (precondition)");
var _r5l_lo = su2_from_quat(hquat_new(_R5L_DMAX, _R5L_YLO, 0, 0));
assert_eq(_su2_x(_r5l_lo), 0x3FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF, "su2_from_quat at y_lo: x, the old lines' bits, as in 3.3.2");
assert_eq(_su2_y(_r5l_lo), 0x3E53988E1409212D, "su2_from_quat at y_lo: y, as in 3.3.2");
var _r5l_hi = su2_from_quat(hquat_new(_R5L_DMAX, _R5L_YHI, 0, 0));
assert_eq(_su2_x(_r5l_hi), 0x3FEFFFFFFFFFFFFE, "su2_from_quat at y_hi: x (0.50 ulp of the oracle; 3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(_su2_y(_r5l_hi), 0x3E53988E1409212E, "su2_from_quat at y_hi: y (0.31 ulp; 3.3.2: 0)");
# The same overflow with DBL_MAX in y, in z and in w: the rescale's max must cover every
# slot, or it scales by the smaller one and lands an ulp away (or on NaN).
assert_eq(_su2_y(su2_from_quat(hquat_new(0x7E53988E1409212E, _R5L_DMAX, 0, 0))), 0x3FEFFFFFFFFFFFFE,
  "su2_from_quat, DBL_MAX in y: y as x at y_hi (3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(_su2_z(su2_from_quat(hquat_new(0x7E53988E1409212E, 0, _R5L_DMAX, 0))), 0x3FEFFFFFFFFFFFFE,
  "su2_from_quat, DBL_MAX in z: z as x at y_hi (3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(_su2_w(su2_from_quat(hquat_new(0x7E53988E1409212E, 0, 0, _R5L_DMAX))), 0x3FEFFFFFFFFFFFFE,
  "su2_from_quat, DBL_MAX in w: w as x at y_hi (3.3.2: 0)");
# An infinite component makes the norm +Inf too, but runs the old lines: DBL_MAX * 0 is
# +0 in x, where a rescale by mx = +Inf would make it NaN.
var _r5l_inf = su2_from_quat(hquat_new(_R5L_DMAX, 0x7FF0000000000000, 0, 0));
assert_eq(_su2_x(_r5l_inf), 0, "su2_from_quat(DBL_MAX, +Inf, 0, 0): x is +0, as in 3.3.2 (the old lines)");
assert_eq(f64_eq(_su2_y(_r5l_inf), _su2_y(_r5l_inf)), 0, "su2_from_quat(DBL_MAX, +Inf, 0, 0): y is NaN, as in 3.3.2");

# ⚠ AND THE INFINITY EXIT INSIDE THE SHARED NORM, tested on the HELPER'S OWN
# CONTRACT rather than through a caller. `Inf/Inf` is NaN, so a scaled norm
# without its own +Inf exit reports the norm of an infinite vector as NaN instead
# of +Inf. ⛔ A first draft of this asserted that `so3_from_axis_angle` therefore
# returns a non-NaN matrix, and that is FALSE both before and after: the caller
# does `component * (1/norm)` = `Inf * 0` = NaN whatever the norm says. The exit
# is about the norm's own answer, and that is what is asserted.
assert_eq(lie_norm3(0x7FF0000000000000, 0, 0), 0x7FF0000000000000,
  "lie_norm3: the norm of an infinite vector is +Inf, not NaN");
assert_eq(_lie_norm4(0, 0x7FF0000000000000, 0, 0), 0x7FF0000000000000,
  "_lie_norm4: the same for the quaternion form");
assert_eq(lie_norm3(0, 0, 0), 0, "lie_norm3: the zero vector still has norm 0");

# === 3.3.3 G7: NaN-true guards in the Lie norms and maps ===
# ⛔ 3.3.3 — stdlib `f64_max(a, b)` returns `b` when `a` is NaN, so the shared norms
# dropped a NaN in every slot but the last, and their `f64_gt(mx, 0) == 0` exit then
# returned 0: every map reading the norm returned the IDENTITY for a NaN input (audit
# D085). Five guards downstream were `f64_gt(x, 0) == 0` too, which is TRUE for NaN.
# Each assertion below sits at the input where one of them fired, and the 3.3.2 answer
# is in its message. NaN is tested by `f64_eq(v, v) == 0`, never by bits: x86 gives
# 0xFFF8... or 0x7FF8... depending on the operation that produced it, and both are NaN.
# [measured: scratch harness, not reproducible in-tree, 2026-10-01; cycc 6.6.12]
fn _g7_nan(v) {
    if (f64_eq(v, v) == 1) { return 0; }
    return 1;
}
var _G7_NAN = 0x7FF8000000000000;
var _G7_PINF = 0x7FF0000000000000;
var _G7_NZ = 0x8000000000000000;

# The norm itself. mx = 0: a NaN in x or y, dropped from the max, left `mx` = 0.
assert_eq(_g7_nan(lie_norm3(_G7_NAN, 0, 0)), 1, "lie_norm3: NaN in x is NaN (3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(lie_norm3(0, _G7_NAN, 0)), 1, "lie_norm3: NaN in y is NaN (3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(lie_norm3(_G7_NAN, _G7_NAN, 0)), 1, "lie_norm3: NaN in x and y is NaN (3.3.2: 0)");
# mx = +Inf: a dropped NaN beside an infinity left `mx` = +Inf.
assert_eq(_g7_nan(lie_norm3(_G7_NAN, _G7_PINF, 0)), 1, "lie_norm3: (NaN, +Inf, 0) is NaN (3.3.2: +Inf)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(lie_norm3(_G7_PINF, _G7_NAN, 0)), 1, "lie_norm3: (+Inf, NaN, 0) is NaN (3.3.2: +Inf)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(lie_norm3(0, _G7_PINF, _G7_NAN)), 1,
  "lie_norm3: (0, +Inf, NaN) is NaN, as it already was: the answer no longer depends on the slot");
# Outside 0 < mx < +Inf the norm is |x| + |y| + |z|, and the absolute values are
# load-bearing.
assert_eq(lie_norm3(_G7_NZ, _G7_NZ, _G7_NZ), 0, "lie_norm3: (-0, -0, -0) is +0, bit-exact (a plain sum gives -0)");
assert_eq(lie_norm3(0xFFF0000000000000, 0, 0), _G7_PINF,
  "lie_norm3: (-Inf, 0, 0) is +Inf, bit-exact (a plain sum gives -Inf)");
# The quaternion form drops w, x and y.
assert_eq(_g7_nan(_lie_norm4(_G7_NAN, 0, 0, 0)), 1, "_lie_norm4: NaN in w is NaN (3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(_lie_norm4(0, _G7_NAN, 0, 0)), 1, "_lie_norm4: NaN in x is NaN (3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(_lie_norm4(0, 0, _G7_NAN, 0)), 1, "_lie_norm4: NaN in y is NaN (3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(_lie_norm4(_G7_NAN, 0, _G7_PINF, 0)), 1, "_lie_norm4: (NaN, 0, +Inf, 0) is NaN (3.3.2: +Inf)");
assert_eq(_lie_norm4(_G7_NZ, _G7_NZ, _G7_NZ, _G7_NZ), 0, "_lie_norm4: all -0 is +0, bit-exact");
assert_eq(_lie_norm4(0, 0, 0xFFF0000000000000, 0), _G7_PINF, "_lie_norm4: a -Inf slot is +Inf, bit-exact");

# The maps that read the norm returned the identity (w = 1, R = I) for these.
assert_eq(_g7_nan(_su2_w(su2_exp(hvec3_new(_G7_NAN, 0, 0)))), 1, "su2_exp: NaN in x is NaN (3.3.2: identity)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(m3_get(so3_exp(hvec3_new(0, _G7_NAN, 0)), 0, 0)), 1, "so3_exp: NaN in y is NaN (3.3.2: I)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(_su2_w(su2_from_quat(hquat_new(_G7_NAN, 0, 0, 0)))), 1,
  "su2_from_quat: NaN in x is NaN (3.3.2: identity)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(_su2_x(su2_from_quat(hquat_new(0, 0, 0, _G7_NAN)))), 1,
  "su2_from_quat: NaN in w is NaN (3.3.2: identity)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(_su2_x(su2_from_axis_angle(hvec3_new(_G7_NAN, 0, 0), F64_ONE))), 1,
  "su2_from_axis_angle: NaN axis is NaN (3.3.2: identity)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(m3_get(so3_from_axis_angle(hvec3_new(_G7_NAN, 0, 0), F64_ONE), 0, 0)), 1,
  "so3_from_axis_angle: NaN axis is NaN (3.3.2: I)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(m4_get(lorentz_boost(hvec3_new(_G7_NAN, 0, 0), F64_ONE), 1, 1)), 1,
  "lorentz_boost: NaN direction is NaN (3.3.2: identity)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(m4_get(lorentz_rotate(hvec3_new(0, _G7_NAN, 0), F64_ONE), 1, 1)), 1,
  "lorentz_rotate: NaN axis is NaN (3.3.2: identity)");

# se3_exp's zero-twist guard. A NaN in z reached it through a norm that already
# propagated it, so this fixture isolates the guard; x goes through both repairs.
var _g7_v123 = hvec3_new(F64_ONE, F64_TWO, f64_from(3));
var _g7_sz = se3_exp(_twist_from(hvec3_new(0, 0, _G7_NAN), _g7_v123));
assert_eq(_g7_nan(_su2_w(_se3_rot(_g7_sz))), 1, "se3_exp: NaN omega z gives a NaN rotation (3.3.2: identity)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(HVec3_x(_se3_trans(_g7_sz))), 1, "se3_exp: and a NaN translation (3.3.2: t = v)");
var _g7_sx = se3_exp(_twist_from(hvec3_new(_G7_NAN, 0, 0), _g7_v123));
assert_eq(_g7_nan(HVec3_x(_se3_trans(_g7_sx))), 1, "se3_exp: NaN omega x gives a NaN translation (3.3.2: t = v)");

# su2_log's identity guard: z isolates it, x needs the norm repair as well.
assert_eq(_g7_nan(HVec3_z(su2_log(_su2_alloc(F64_HALF, 0, 0, _G7_NAN)))), 1,
  "su2_log: NaN in z is NaN (3.3.2: the zero vector)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(HVec3_x(su2_log(_su2_alloc(F64_HALF, _G7_NAN, 0, 0)))), 1,
  "su2_log: NaN in x is NaN (3.3.2: the zero vector)");
# se3_log's guard: w = NaN makes su2_log's omega NaN (through its non-finite test on w;
# before that test existed, atan2(0.5, NaN) was NaN too), and the guard then returned v = t
# beside it.
var _g7_lt = se3_log(se3_new(_su2_alloc(_G7_NAN, F64_HALF, 0, 0), _g7_v123));
assert_eq(_g7_nan(HVec3_x(_twist_v(_g7_lt))), 1, "se3_log: a NaN omega gives a NaN v (3.3.2: v = t)");

# so3_log's two antisymmetric-part guards. An off-diagonal NaN at (col 0, row 1)
# reaches `asym` through z, so the norm propagated it even in 3.3.2.
var _g7_ri = m3_identity();
m3_set(_g7_ri, 0, 1, _G7_NAN);
assert_eq(_g7_nan(HVec3_z(so3_log(_g7_ri))), 1, "so3_log: I with a NaN at (0,1) is NaN (3.3.2: zero vector)");
var _g7_rx = m3_identity();
m3_set(_g7_rx, 1, 2, _G7_NAN);
assert_eq(_g7_nan(HVec3_x(so3_log(_g7_rx))), 1, "so3_log: I with a NaN at (1,2) is NaN (3.3.2: zero vector)");
# The second guard sent a NaN into the eigenvector branch, which scales by
# atan2(NaN * 0.5, cos theta). Over 14 probed cosines that was NaN everywhere but
# cos theta = +-0, where ganita through 1.2.10 returned -pi/2 (D140) — so the fixture is
# a hand-built quarter turn whose trace is exactly 1 (so3_exp's rounds to 1 + 1 ulp),
# which the first guard's `cos theta > 0` also lets past.
# [measured: scratch harness, not reproducible in-tree, 2026-10-01; cycc 6.6.12]
# ⚠ 3.3.4 — ganita 1.2.11 (cyrius 6.6.13) repaired atan2, so under hisab's pin this
# fixture no longer discriminates the guard. It stays, correct: the branch now scales by
# NaN. Reverting the guard to `f64_gt(asym, 0) == 0` fails these two rows on cyrius
# 6.6.12 only; on 6.6.14 the Inf - Inf rows in "3.3.3 R4lie" below still fail.
# [measured: scratch mutation run, not reproducible in-tree, 2026-10-03; cycc 6.6.12 and 6.6.14]
var _g7_rq = m3_new();
m3_set(_g7_rq, 0, 1, F64_ONE);
m3_set(_g7_rq, 1, 0, f64_neg(F64_ONE));
m3_set(_g7_rq, 2, 2, F64_ONE);
assert_eq(HVec3_z(so3_log(_g7_rq)), 0x3FF921FB54442D18, "so3_log: the clean quarter turn is +pi/2 (control)");
m3_set(_g7_rq, 0, 1, _G7_NAN);
var _g7_oq = so3_log(_g7_rq);
assert_eq(_g7_nan(HVec3_z(_g7_oq)), 1, "so3_log: quarter turn with a NaN is NaN (3.3.2: -pi/2, finite)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(HVec3_x(_g7_oq)), 1, "so3_log: in every component (3.3.2: -0)");

# === 3.3.3 R4lie: su2_log and so3_log refuse non-finite input ===
# ⛔ 3.3.3 — NON-FINITE INPUT CAME BACK AS A ROTATION, OFTEN THE IDENTITY'S LOGARITHM.
# su2_log's clamp passed a NaN w through and made an infinite one +-1. With a zero
# vector part its exit then fired whatever w held, so (NaN, 0, 0, 0) — which
# su2_from_quat makes of (0, 0, 0, +Inf) — gave the zero vector, and se3_log returned
# v = t; with a vector part an infinite w gave a finite rotation. so3_log clamped an
# infinite trace's cosine to +-1, so the identity with +Inf at [0][0] gave the zero
# vector and any other rotation a finite wrong one, and an infinite entry off the
# diagonal gave 0 in every slot but its own. All now return 0x7FF8000000000000 in
# every slot, so these assert the bits. The 3.3.2 answer is in each message.
# [measured: scratch harness, not reproducible in-tree, 2026-10-01; cycc 6.6.12]
var _R4L_NAN = 0x7FF8000000000000;
var _R4L_PINF = 0x7FF0000000000000;
var _R4L_NINF = 0xFFF0000000000000;
var _r4l_o = su2_log(_su2_alloc(_R4L_NAN, 0, 0, 0));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r4l_o), _R4L_NAN, "su2_log: (NaN, 0, 0, 0) is NaN, bit-exact (3.3.2: the zero vector)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_r4l_o), _R4L_NAN, "su2_log: (NaN, 0, 0, 0) y is NaN, bit-exact");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_r4l_o), _R4L_NAN, "su2_log: (NaN, 0, 0, 0) z is NaN, bit-exact");
assert_eq(HVec3_x(su2_log(_su2_alloc(0xFFF8000000000000, 0x8000000000000000, 0, 0x8000000000000000))), _R4L_NAN,
  "su2_log: (-NaN, -0, 0, -0) is NaN, bit-exact (3.3.2: the zero vector)");
assert_eq(HVec3_x(su2_log(_su2_alloc(_R4L_PINF, 0, 0, 0))), _R4L_NAN,
  "su2_log: (+Inf, 0, 0, 0) is NaN, bit-exact (3.3.2: the zero vector; the clamp made w = 1)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(su2_log(_su2_alloc(_R4L_NINF, 0, 0, 0))), _R4L_NAN,
  "su2_log: (-Inf, 0, 0, 0) is NaN, bit-exact (3.3.2: the zero vector; the clamp made w = -1)");
# With a vector part an infinite w took the general path as +-1, and a NaN w came back
# as a NaN carrying its own sign. The guard is ahead of the clamp and the exit both.
var _r4l_op = su2_log(_su2_alloc(_R4L_PINF, F64_HALF, 0, 0));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r4l_op), _R4L_NAN, "su2_log: (+Inf, 0.5, 0, 0) is NaN, bit-exact (3.3.2: 0.927 about x)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_r4l_op), _R4L_NAN, "su2_log: (+Inf, 0.5, 0, 0) y is NaN, bit-exact (3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(HVec3_x(su2_log(_su2_alloc(_R4L_NINF, F64_HALF, 0, 0))), _R4L_NAN,
  "su2_log: (-Inf, 0.5, 0, 0) is NaN, bit-exact (3.3.2: 5.36 about x)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(su2_log(_su2_alloc(0xFFF8000000000000, F64_HALF, 0, 0))), _R4L_NAN,
  "su2_log: (-NaN, 0.5, 0, 0) is the guard's quiet NaN, bit-exact (3.3.2: -NaN in every slot)");
# On the boundary: the largest finite w still takes the zero-vector exit.
var _r4l_om = su2_log(_su2_alloc(0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF, 0, 0, 0));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r4l_om) | HVec3_y(_r4l_om) | HVec3_z(_r4l_om), 0,
  "su2_log: (DBL_MAX, 0, 0, 0) is still +0 in every slot, bit-exact");
# The public routes. su2_from_quat of a quaternion with an infinite w normalizes to
# (NaN, 0, 0, 0) — checked first, so the fixture provably is the input the exit took.
var _r4l_g = su2_from_quat(hquat_new(0, 0, 0, _R4L_PINF));
assert_eq(_g7_nan(_su2_w(_r4l_g)), 1, "su2_from_quat(0, 0, 0, +Inf): w is NaN (fixture precondition)");
assert_eq(_su2_x(_r4l_g) | _su2_y(_r4l_g) | _su2_z(_r4l_g), 0,
  "su2_from_quat(0, 0, 0, +Inf): the vector part is +0 (fixture precondition)");
assert_eq(HVec3_x(su2_log(_r4l_g)), _R4L_NAN, "su2_log(su2_from_quat(0, 0, 0, +Inf)) is NaN (3.3.2: 0)");
var _r4l_t = se3_log(se3_new(_r4l_g, _g7_v123));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_twist_omega(_r4l_t)), _R4L_NAN, "se3_log over it: omega is NaN, bit-exact (3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(HVec3_x(_twist_v(_r4l_t))), 1, "se3_log over it: v is NaN (3.3.2: v = t = 1)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(HVec3_z(_twist_v(_r4l_t))), 1, "se3_log over it: v.z is NaN (3.3.2: 3)");
var _r4l_m = m4_identity();
m4_set(_r4l_m, 0, 0, _R4L_PINF);
m4_set(_r4l_m, 3, 0, F64_ONE);
m4_set(_r4l_m, 3, 1, F64_TWO);
m4_set(_r4l_m, 3, 2, f64_from(3));
var _r4l_mt = se3_log(se3_from_mat4(_r4l_m));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_twist_omega(_r4l_mt)), _R4L_NAN,
  "se3_log(se3_from_mat4(I with +Inf at [0][0])): omega is NaN (3.3.2: the identity rotation)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(HVec3_y(_twist_v(_r4l_mt))), 1,
  "se3_log(se3_from_mat4(I with +Inf at [0][0])): v is NaN (3.3.2: v = t = 2)");
# In 3.3.2 so3_log of the same rotation was (0, 0, 0) too: both logs agreed, and both were
# wrong. 3.3.3's tests in so3_log make it NaN, and su2_log's w test makes it agree again.
assert_eq(_g7_nan(HVec3_x(so3_log(so3_from_quat(hquat_new(0, 0, 0, _R4L_PINF))))), 1,
  "so3_log(so3_from_quat(0, 0, 0, +Inf)) is NaN, as su2_log's now is (3.3.2: both 0)");

# so3_log: a non-finite trace, before the clamp. Every slot, bit-exact.
var _r4l_r = m3_identity();
m3_set(_r4l_r, 0, 0, _R4L_PINF);
var _r4l_ro = so3_log(_r4l_r);
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r4l_ro), _R4L_NAN, "so3_log: I with +Inf at [0][0] is NaN, bit-exact (3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_r4l_ro), _R4L_NAN, "so3_log: I with +Inf at [0][0], y (3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_r4l_ro), _R4L_NAN, "so3_log: I with +Inf at [0][0], z (3.3.2: 0)");
var _r4l_r2 = m3_identity();
m3_set(_r4l_r2, 1, 1, _R4L_NINF);
var _r4l_ro2 = so3_log(_r4l_r2);
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r4l_ro2), _R4L_NAN, "so3_log: I with -Inf at [1][1] is NaN, bit-exact (3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_r4l_ro2), _R4L_NAN, "so3_log: I with -Inf at [1][1], z (3.3.2: 0)");
# A rotation that is not the identity came back as a finite, different rotation.
var _r4l_w = hvec3_new(f64_div(f64_from(3), f64_from(10)), f64_div(f64_from(-2), f64_from(10)), F64_HALF);
var _r4l_r3 = so3_exp(_r4l_w);
assert_eq(HVec3_z(so3_log(_r4l_r3)), 0x3FE0000000000001,
  "so3_log(so3_exp((0.3, -0.2, 0.5))) recovers z = 0.5 + 1 ulp, as in 3.3.2 (control)");
m3_set(_r4l_r3, 2, 2, _R4L_PINF);
assert_eq(HVec3_z(so3_log(_r4l_r3)), _R4L_NAN, "so3_log: that rotation with +Inf at [2][2] is NaN (3.3.2: z = 0.43)");
m3_set(_r4l_r3, 2, 2, _R4L_NINF);
assert_eq(HVec3_x(so3_log(_r4l_r3)), _R4L_NAN, "so3_log: and with -Inf at [2][2] (3.3.2: x = 1.27)");
m3_set(_r4l_r3, 2, 2, _R4L_NAN);
assert_eq(HVec3_y(so3_log(_r4l_r3)), _R4L_NAN, "so3_log: and with NaN at [2][2], as in 3.3.2");
# A finite diagonal whose sum overflows is no rotation either.
var _r4l_r4 = m3_identity();
m3_set(_r4l_r4, 0, 0, 0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF);
m3_set(_r4l_r4, 1, 1, 0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF);
assert_eq(HVec3_x(so3_log(_r4l_r4)), _R4L_NAN, "so3_log: diag(DBL_MAX, DBL_MAX, 1), trace +Inf, is NaN (3.3.2: 0)");
# On the boundary: a trace of exactly DBL_MAX is finite and passes. diag(DBL_MAX, 0, 0)
# is no rotation, so its value means nothing; this asserts only that it is not NaN.
var _r4l_rb = m3_new();
m3_set(_r4l_rb, 0, 0, 0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF);
assert_eq(m3_trace(_r4l_rb), 0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF, "so3_log boundary fixture: the trace is DBL_MAX (precondition)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(HVec3_x(so3_log(_r4l_rb))), 0, "so3_log: a trace of DBL_MAX does not take the non-finite exit");

# so3_log: an infinite entry off the diagonal made `asym` +Inf and theta/asym exactly
# 0, so every slot but the infinite one's was 0. Every slot, bit-exact.
var _r4l_r5 = m3_identity();
m3_set(_r4l_r5, 0, 1, _R4L_PINF);
var _r4l_ro5 = so3_log(_r4l_r5);
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r4l_ro5), _R4L_NAN, "so3_log: I with +Inf at (0,1) is NaN, bit-exact (3.3.2: +0)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_r4l_ro5), _R4L_NAN, "so3_log: I with +Inf at (0,1), y (3.3.2: +0)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_r4l_ro5), _R4L_NAN, "so3_log: I with +Inf at (0,1), z (3.3.2: -NaN)");
var _r4l_r6 = m3_identity();
m3_set(_r4l_r6, 1, 2, _R4L_NINF);
var _r4l_ro6 = so3_log(_r4l_r6);
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_r4l_ro6), _R4L_NAN, "so3_log: I with -Inf at (1,2), y (3.3.2: +0)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_r4l_ro6), _R4L_NAN, "so3_log: I with -Inf at (1,2), z (3.3.2: +0)");
var _r4l_r7 = so3_exp(_r4l_w);
m3_set(_r4l_r7, 0, 1, _R4L_PINF);
var _r4l_ro7 = so3_log(_r4l_r7);
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r4l_ro7), _R4L_NAN, "so3_log: the rotation with +Inf at (0,1), x (3.3.2: +0)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_r4l_ro7), _R4L_NAN, "so3_log: the rotation with +Inf at (0,1), y (3.3.2: -0)");
# Finite entries: a difference that overflows, and two finite differences whose norm does.
var _r4l_r8 = m3_identity();
m3_set(_r4l_r8, 0, 1, 0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF);
m3_set(_r4l_r8, 1, 0, 0xFFEFFFFFFFFFFFFF);
assert_eq(HVec3_x(so3_log(_r4l_r8)), _R4L_NAN, "so3_log: DBL_MAX - (-DBL_MAX) off the diagonal is NaN (3.3.2: +0)");
var _r4l_r9 = m3_identity();
m3_set(_r4l_r9, 1, 2, 0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF);
m3_set(_r4l_r9, 2, 0, 0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF);
var _r4l_ro9 = so3_log(_r4l_r9);
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r4l_ro9), _R4L_NAN, "so3_log: |(DBL_MAX, DBL_MAX, 0)| overflows: NaN (3.3.2: the zero vector)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_r4l_ro9), _R4L_NAN, "so3_log: |(DBL_MAX, DBL_MAX, 0)| overflows: z NaN (3.3.2: +0)");
# A NaN `asym` takes the same exit. +Inf - +Inf at (1,2)/(2,1) is -NaN, and beside a
# non-zero z the scaled norm carries that sign, which the sc path then kept in every
# slot; the guard's NaN is the quiet one, so this pins that the test catches NaN too.
var _r4l_rn = m3_identity();
m3_set(_r4l_rn, 0, 1, F64_HALF);
m3_set(_r4l_rn, 1, 2, _R4L_PINF);
m3_set(_r4l_rn, 2, 1, _R4L_PINF);
var _r4l_ron = so3_log(_r4l_rn);
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r4l_ron), _R4L_NAN,
  "so3_log: Inf - Inf off the diagonal is the guard's quiet NaN, bit-exact (3.3.2: the zero vector)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_r4l_ron), _R4L_NAN, "so3_log: Inf - Inf off the diagonal, z (3.3.2: +0; before this exit, -NaN)");
# On the boundary: an `asym` of exactly DBL_MAX is finite and passes; again no rotation,
# so this asserts only that it is not NaN.
var _r4l_ra = m3_identity();
m3_set(_r4l_ra, 1, 2, 0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF);
assert_eq(lie_norm3(0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF, 0, 0), 0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF,
  "so3_log boundary fixture: asym is DBL_MAX (precondition)");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(HVec3_x(so3_log(_r4l_ra))), 0, "so3_log: an asym of DBL_MAX does not take the non-finite exit");

# === 3.3.3 R5lie: su2_log refuses a non-finite vector part ===
# ⛔ 3.3.3 — the pair of so3_log's test on `asym`. sin_half was +Inf for an infinite
# component, or finite ones whose norm overflows, and sc = 2*half/+Inf was exactly 0, so
# every slot but an infinite one's came back 0. No unit element has such a vector part,
# so these are white-box. All now return 0x7FF8000000000000 in every slot.
# [measured: scratch harness, not reproducible in-tree, 2026-10-01; cycc 6.6.12]
var _r5l_o1 = su2_log(_su2_alloc(F64_ONE, _R4L_PINF, 0, 0));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r5l_o1), _R4L_NAN, "su2_log: (1, +Inf, 0, 0) is NaN, bit-exact (3.3.2: -NaN)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_r5l_o1), _R4L_NAN, "su2_log: (1, +Inf, 0, 0) y is NaN, bit-exact (3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_r5l_o1), _R4L_NAN, "su2_log: (1, +Inf, 0, 0) z is NaN, bit-exact (3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(HVec3_x(su2_log(_su2_alloc(F64_HALF, 0, 0, _R4L_NINF))), _R4L_NAN,
  "su2_log: (0.5, 0, 0, -Inf) x is NaN, bit-exact (3.3.2: 0)");
var _r5l_e2 = _su2_alloc(F64_ONE, 0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF, 0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF, 0);
assert_eq(lie_norm3(_su2_x(_r5l_e2), _su2_y(_r5l_e2), _su2_z(_r5l_e2)), _R4L_PINF,
  "overflow fixture: its |(x, y, z)| is +Inf (precondition)");
var _r5l_o2 = su2_log(_r5l_e2);
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r5l_o2), _R4L_NAN, "su2_log: (1, DBL_MAX, DBL_MAX, 0) is NaN, bit-exact (3.3.2: 0)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_r5l_o2), _R4L_NAN, "su2_log: (1, DBL_MAX, DBL_MAX, 0) z is NaN, bit-exact (3.3.2: 0)");
# The test catches a NaN as well: past G7's exit this NaN vector part came back -NaN from
# the general path.
assert_eq(HVec3_x(su2_log(_su2_alloc(0, 0, 0xFFF8000000000000, F64_ONE))), _R4L_NAN,
  "su2_log: (0, 0, -NaN, 1) is the test's quiet NaN, bit-exact (3.3.2: 0; the general path: -NaN)");
# On the boundary: a sin_half of exactly DBL_MAX is finite and passes, as in 3.3.2.
assert_eq(HVec3_x(su2_log(_su2_alloc(F64_ONE, 0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF, 0, 0))), 0x400921FB54442D17,
  "su2_log: (1, DBL_MAX, 0, 0) still takes the general path: pi - 1 ulp, as in 3.3.2");

# === 3.3.3 R4lie: su2_log near w = -1, where sc overflowed ===
# ⛔ 3.3.3 — sc = 2*half/sin_half passed DBL_MAX for a unit element with w = -1 and a
# vector part below 2*pi/DBL_MAX (~2^-1021.4): the answer was (+Inf, NaN, NaN) where
# 2*pi along the vector's direction is right, and se3_log carried the NaN into v. Only
# that overflow takes the rescue, so the expected bits below were settled against an
# 80-digit oracle of 2*atan2(|v|, w) * v/|v|: exact where noted, else 1 ulp off it.
# [measured: scratch harness and Decimal oracle, not reproducible in-tree, 2026-10-01; cycc 6.6.12]
var _R4L_2PI = 0x401921FB54442D18;
var _R4L_M1 = 0xBFF0000000000000;
var _r4l_a = su2_log(su2_from_quat(hquat_new(0x0000000000100000, 0, 0, _R4L_M1)));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r4l_a), _R4L_2PI, "su2_log: w = -1, x = 2^-1054 gives exactly 2*pi (3.3.2: +Inf)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_r4l_a), 0, "su2_log: w = -1, x = 2^-1054, y is +0 (3.3.2: NaN)");
assert_eq(HVec3_x(su2_log(su2_from_quat(hquat_new(0x0010000000000000, 0, 0, _R4L_M1)))), _R4L_2PI,
  "su2_log: w = -1, x = DBL_MIN gives exactly 2*pi (3.3.2: +Inf)");
# Full-mantissa subnormal direction in all three slots; su2_from_quat keeps the bits.
var _r4l_f3 = su2_from_quat(hquat_new(0x000ABCDEF0123457, 0x8003456789ABCDEF, 0x0000123456789ABD, _R4L_M1));
assert_eq(_su2_y(_r4l_f3), 0x8003456789ABCDEF, "su2_from_quat keeps the subnormal y (fixture precondition)");
var _r4l_b = su2_log(_r4l_f3);
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r4l_b), 0x40180A9ADA867DB7, "su2_log: subnormal (x, y, z), w = -1: x (oracle-exact)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_r4l_b), 0xBFFD4BA6D6838A95, "su2_log: subnormal (x, y, z), w = -1: y (oracle ...A96, 1 ulp)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_r4l_b), 0x3FA4612625EC340E, "su2_log: subnormal (x, y, z), w = -1: z (oracle-exact)");
# ON the boundary. y fixed; x_lo is the last x whose sin_half overflows sc and x_hi the
# next double. The two paths round differently there, so x_hi pins that the rescue is
# NOT taken where sc is finite (it would give ...ACF and ...6F7).
var _R4L_BY = 0x0011A2B3C4D5E6F7;
assert_eq(lie_norm3(0x0011E80EC5E57ACE, _R4L_BY, 0), 0x001921FB54442D18,
  "boundary fixture: sin_half at x_lo is 2*pi * 2^-1024, rounded (precondition)");
assert_eq(lie_norm3(0x0011E80EC5E57ACF, _R4L_BY, 0), 0x001921FB54442D19,
  "boundary fixture: sin_half at x_hi is one ulp above it (precondition)");
var _r4l_lo = su2_log(su2_from_quat(hquat_new(0x0011E80EC5E57ACE, _R4L_BY, 0, _R4L_M1)));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r4l_lo), 0x4011E80EC5E57ACE, "su2_log at x_lo (rescue): x (oracle ...ACF, 1 ulp; 3.3.2: +Inf)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_r4l_lo), 0x4011A2B3C4D5E6F7, "su2_log at x_lo (rescue): y (oracle ...6F8, 1 ulp; 3.3.2: NaN)");
var _r4l_hi = su2_log(su2_from_quat(hquat_new(0x0011E80EC5E57ACF, _R4L_BY, 0, _R4L_M1)));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r4l_hi), 0x4011E80EC5E57ACE, "su2_log at x_hi (sc finite, 3.3.2's line): x (oracle ...ACF)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_r4l_hi), 0x4011A2B3C4D5E6F6, "su2_log at x_hi (sc finite, 3.3.2's line): y (oracle ...6F7)");
# A non-unit element takes the same rescue: (0, 2^-1050, 0, 0) is a half turn about x.
assert_eq(HVec3_x(su2_log(_su2_alloc(0, 0x0000000400000000, 0, 0))), 0x400921FB54442D18,
  "su2_log: (0, 2^-1050, 0, 0) gives exactly pi (3.3.2: +Inf)");
# se3_log over these elements: the R5lie block below, which refuses them.

# === 3.3.3 R5lie: su2_log's rescue over y and z, and at a tiny w ===
# ⛔ 3.3.3 — every rescue fixture above has x as its largest or only component, so a
# rescue that scaled by |x| alone, or forgot y or z in its max, passed the suite while
# returning NaN for a 2*pi turn about y or z. These put the vector part along y, along z,
# and z-largest. Against an 80-digit Decimal oracle the rescue is within 2 ulps.
# [measured: scratch harness and Decimal oracle, not reproducible in-tree, 2026-10-01; cycc 6.6.12]
var _r5l_ry = su2_log(su2_from_quat(hquat_new(0, 0x0010000000000000, 0, _R4L_M1)));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r5l_ry), 0, "su2_log: w = -1, y = DBL_MIN: x is +0 (3.3.2: NaN)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_r5l_ry), _R4L_2PI, "su2_log: w = -1, y = DBL_MIN: y is exactly 2*pi (3.3.2: +Inf)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_r5l_ry), 0, "su2_log: w = -1, y = DBL_MIN: z is +0 (3.3.2: NaN)");
var _r5l_rz = su2_log(su2_from_quat(hquat_new(0, 0, 0x0000000000100000, _R4L_M1)));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r5l_rz) | HVec3_y(_r5l_rz), 0, "su2_log: w = -1, z = 2^-1054: x and y are +0 (3.3.2: NaN)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_r5l_rz), _R4L_2PI, "su2_log: w = -1, z = 2^-1054: z is exactly 2*pi (3.3.2: +Inf)");
var _r5l_t3 = su2_from_quat(hquat_new(0x0001234567890ABD, 0x8004FEDCBA987653, 0x000ABCDEF0123457, _R4L_M1));
assert_eq(_su2_z(_r5l_t3), 0x000ABCDEF0123457, "su2_from_quat keeps the subnormal z (fixture precondition)");
var _r5l_rt = su2_log(_r5l_t3);
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r5l_rt), 0x3FE33A594650D610, "su2_log: z-largest subnormal triple: x (oracle ...611, 1 ulp)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_r5l_rt), 0xC0051B0BFE3A66B5, "su2_log: z-largest subnormal triple: y (oracle ...6B6, 1 ulp)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_r5l_rt), 0x4016AED52CF28030, "su2_log: z-largest subnormal triple: z (oracle ...031, 1 ulp)");
# ⛔ 3.3.3 — the rescue took its angle from sin_half, which `lie_norm3` rounds onto the
# subnormal grid: for (2^-1074, 0, 2^-1074, 2^-1074) sin_half is 2^-1074, not sqrt(2)
# times it, so the angle was atan2(1, 1) where atan(sqrt(2)) is right. White-box (no unit
# element has a subnormal w); 3.3.2 gave (NaN, +Inf, +Inf), and the first rescue 1.11.
var _r5l_rw = su2_log(_su2_alloc(1, 0, 1, 1));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r5l_rw), 0, "su2_log: (2^-1074, 0, 2^-1074, 2^-1074): x is +0");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_r5l_rw), 0x3FF59DC8F2DC2562,
  "su2_log: (2^-1074, 0, 2^-1074, 2^-1074): y is 1.351 (oracle ...563, 1 ulp; 3.3.2: +Inf)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_r5l_rw), 0x3FF59DC8F2DC2562, "su2_log: (2^-1074, 0, 2^-1074, 2^-1074): z as y");

# === 3.3.3 R5lie: se3_log refuses the turns past pi that su2_log rescues ===
# ⛔ 3.3.3 — the rescue made su2_log right on these, a turn of 2*pi, and se3_log then
# inverted a V that is singular there: for w = -1, x = 2^-1054 and t = (1, 2, 3) it gave
# v = (1, -5.1e16, -7.7e16) where the true |v| is 2.2e318, and another v under cycc 6.6.3.
# 3.3.2's omega was non-finite on every element the rescue takes, so se3_log refuses the
# ones past pi, every unit one among them, with NaN in all six slots. Every other element,
# the turns just short of 2*pi included, gives 3.3.2's bits.
# [measured: scratch harness and Decimal oracle, not reproducible in-tree, 2026-10-01; cycc 6.6.12 and 6.6.3]
var _r5l_e = se3_log(se3_new(su2_from_quat(hquat_new(0x0000000000100000, 0, 0, _R4L_M1)), _g7_v123));
var _r5l_ebad = 0;
for (var _r5l_i = 0; _r5l_i < 6; _r5l_i = _r5l_i + 1) {
    if (load64(_r5l_e + _r5l_i * 8) != _R4L_NAN) { _r5l_ebad = _r5l_ebad + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_r5l_ebad, 0,
  "se3_log: w = -1, x = 2^-1054 is 0x7FF8... in all 6 slots (3.3.2: +Inf, NaN, NaN | 1, 2, 3)");
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_twist_omega(_r5l_e)), _R4L_NAN, "se3_log: w = -1, x = 2^-1054: omega x, bit-exact");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_twist_v(_r5l_e)), _R4L_NAN,
  "se3_log: w = -1, x = 2^-1054: v z, bit-exact (3.3.2: 3; the rescue alone: -7.7e16)");
# And se3_exp of the refusal is no motion at all, where the finite twist moved t to ~(1, 0, 0).
var _r5l_ee = se3_exp(_r5l_e);
assert_eq(_g7_nan(_su2_w(_se3_rot(_r5l_ee))), 1, "se3_exp(se3_log(E)): the rotation is NaN");
assert_eq(_g7_nan(HVec3_y(_se3_trans(_r5l_ee))), 1, "se3_exp(se3_log(E)): the translation is NaN");
# ON su2_log's boundary (the x_lo / x_hi pair above): x_lo is rescued and refused, x_hi
# is not rescued and gives 3.3.2's twist.
var _r5l_lo2 = se3_log(se3_new(su2_from_quat(hquat_new(0x0011E80EC5E57ACE, _R4L_BY, 0, _R4L_M1)), _g7_v123));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_twist_omega(_r5l_lo2)), _R4L_NAN, "se3_log at x_lo (rescued): omega x is NaN (3.3.2: +Inf)");
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_twist_v(_r5l_lo2)), _R4L_NAN, "se3_log at x_lo (rescued): v x is NaN (3.3.2: 1)");
var _r5l_hi2 = se3_log(se3_new(su2_from_quat(hquat_new(0x0011E80EC5E57ACF, _R4L_BY, 0, _R4L_M1)), _g7_v123));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_twist_omega(_r5l_hi2)), 0x4011E80EC5E57ACE, "se3_log at x_hi (not rescued): omega x as in 3.3.2");
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_twist_v(_r5l_hi2)), 0x4323FEF03366A464, "se3_log at x_hi (not rescued): v x as in 3.3.2");
# ⚠ A turn whose half-angle rounds to pi is NOT refused unless su2_log rescued it: for some
# of those 3.3.2's v is right. The element su2_from_axis_angle builds from the double
# nearest 2*pi has this v within 1.3e-16 of the oracle, and refusing it would be a loss.
var _r5l_ax = se3_log(se3_new(su2_from_axis_angle(hvec3_new(F64_ONE, 0, 0), F64_TAU), _g7_v123));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_twist_omega(_r5l_ax)), _R4L_2PI, "se3_log, axis-angle (x, 2*pi): omega x as in 3.3.2");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_twist_v(_r5l_ax)), 0xC366C8D414E7F39E,
  "se3_log, axis-angle (x, 2*pi): v y as in 3.3.2, within 1.3e-16 of the oracle");
# su2_log's own sc decides, not 2*pi / sin_half alone: this turn of 5.0 rad (non-unit,
# white-box) has a sin_half for which that quotient overflows, but its sc is finite, so it
# was never rescued, and it keeps 3.3.2's twist, which the oracle confirms.
var _r5l_ttg = _su2_alloc(0x8020000000000000, 0x0018000000000000, 0, 0);
assert_eq(f64_div(F64_TAU, lie_norm3(_su2_x(_r5l_ttg), _su2_y(_r5l_ttg), _su2_z(_r5l_ttg))), _R4L_PINF,
  "sc fixture: 2*pi / sin_half overflows (precondition)");
var _r5l_tt = se3_log(se3_new(_r5l_ttg, _g7_v123));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_twist_omega(_r5l_tt)), 0x4013FC176B7A8560,
  "se3_log: (-2^-1021, 1.5 * 2^-1022, 0, 0): omega x is 5.0, as in 3.3.2 (oracle-close)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_twist_v(_r5l_tt)), 0xC02DFA232137C811,
  "se3_log: (-2^-1021, 1.5 * 2^-1022, 0, 0): v z is -14.99, as in 3.3.2 (oracle-close)");
# A non-unit element rescued past pi is refused as well: (-0.5, 2^-1050, 0, 0) is a turn
# of 2*pi. One rescued at a half turn keeps its twist, which is right there: (0, 2^-1050,
# 0, 0) is a half turn about x, and V(pi) is well-conditioned.
var _r5l_nu = se3_log(se3_new(_su2_alloc(0xBFE0000000000000, 0x0000000400000000, 0, 0), _g7_v123));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_twist_omega(_r5l_nu)), _R4L_NAN, "se3_log: (-0.5, 2^-1050, 0, 0) is refused (3.3.2: +Inf)");
var _r5l_nh = se3_log(se3_new(_su2_alloc(0, 0x0000000400000000, 0, 0), _g7_v123));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_twist_omega(_r5l_nh)), 0x400921FB54442D18,
  "se3_log: (0, 2^-1050, 0, 0): omega x is pi (3.3.2: +Inf)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_twist_v(_r5l_nh)), 0x4012D97C7F3321D2,
  "se3_log: (0, 2^-1050, 0, 0): v y is 3*pi/2, oracle-nearest (3.3.2: 2)");

# --- SE(3) ROUND TRIP, which exercises the V matrix and its inverse together.
var _LT_RUN = 0;
var _LT_BAD = 0;
for (var _lt_e = 0; _lt_e < 500; _lt_e = _lt_e + 1) {
    var _lt_t = (1023 - _lt_e) << 52;
    var _lt_tw = _twist_from(hvec3_new(0, 0, _lt_t), hvec3_new(F64_ONE, 0, 0));
    var _lt_b = se3_log(se3_exp(_lt_tw));
    _LT_RUN = _LT_RUN + 1;
    if (f64_approx_eq(f64_div(HVec3_z(_twist_omega(_lt_b)), _lt_t),
        F64_ONE, LOOSE_TOL_M) != 1) { _LT_BAD = _LT_BAD + 1; }
    if (f64_approx_eq(HVec3_x(_twist_v(_lt_b)), F64_ONE, LOOSE_TOL_M) != 1) {
        _LT_BAD = _LT_BAD + 1;
    }
}
assert_eq(_LT_RUN, 500, "the SE(3) round-trip actually ran 500 angles");
assert_eq(_LT_BAD, 0,
  "se3_log(se3_exp(tw)) recovers both omega and v for angles to 2^-499");

# ⭐ THE CASE THE DEFERRAL WAS ABOUT. At a deeply subnormal angle the old closed
# forms are 0/0: theta^2 and theta^3 underflow to zero at the same time as
# `1 - cos(theta)` and `theta - sin(theta)` do. The series has no theta^2 in it at
# all, so it simply yields its leading term.
var _ls_tiny = 0x0000008000000000;               # 2^-1035, deeply subnormal
var _ls_st = _twist_from(hvec3_new(0, 0, _ls_tiny), hvec3_new(F64_ONE, F64_TWO, 0));
var _ls_se = se3_exp(_ls_st);
var _ls_tr = _se3_trans(_ls_se);
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_ls_tr), F64_ONE,
  "se3_exp: a subnormal angle gives V = I exactly, not a NaN translation");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_ls_tr), F64_TWO,
  "se3_exp: and the second component too");
var _ls_bk = se3_log(_ls_se);
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_twist_v(_ls_bk)), F64_ONE,
  "se3_log: a subnormal angle inverts V exactly, not a NaN velocity");

# ⚠ THAT FIXTURE DID NOT REACH `se3_log`'s COEFFICIENT through 3.3.3, and measuring said
# so: `su2_exp` had its own DBL_MIN guard, so a 2^-1035 rotation came back as the
# exact identity, `su2_log` gave a zero omega and `se3_log` returned early. Since 3.3.4
# `su2_exp` keeps that rotation (audit D086), so the fixture reaches the coefficient as
# well; the one below reaches it for an angle ABOVE DBL_MIN whose SQUARE is still
# zero — and that band is 140 decades wide. 2^-664 sits in it: theta^2 = 2^-1328
# underflows, so the closed form is (1 - 1)/0 = 0/0 = NaN while the series is 1/12.
# ⭐ Built directly rather than through se3_exp, because that is the reachable
# shape: any SE(3) assembled from a rotation and a translation can land here.
var _ls_ta = (1023 - 664) << 52;                 # 2^-664
var _ls_dse = _se3_alloc(su2_from_axis_angle(hvec3_new(0, 0, F64_ONE), _ls_ta),
  hvec3_new(F64_ONE, F64_TWO, 0));
var _ls_dv = _twist_v(se3_log(_ls_dse));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_ls_dv), F64_ONE,
  "se3_log: an angle whose SQUARE underflows still inverts V, not 0/0 = NaN");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_ls_dv), F64_TWO,
  "se3_log: and the second component of that velocity too");

# ⚠ TWO 2.16.0 MUTANTS SURVIVE AND ARE RECORDED, BOTH FOR THE SAME REASON: the
# repairs made each other redundant.
# (a) Disabling `se3_log`'s series and falling back to the closed form passes
#     everything. The 0/0 it was written against is UNREACHABLE: theta there
#     always comes from `su2_log`, which now bottoms out at 2^-537, and theta^2 is
#     2^-1074 — subnormal but non-zero. Above that the closed form's cancellation
#     costs relative accuracy in a term that is itself O(theta^2), so the absolute
#     error stays at the ULP. It is kept for symmetry with `se3_exp`, where the
#     same series IS discriminated, and because a caller assembling an SE(3)
#     directly is not bound by su2_log's floor.
# (b) `F64_TINY` on `se3_exp`'s guard also passes now — and that is the point of
#     the release. In 2.15.0 it was a TRAP: with the old coefficients it produced
#     NaN over the band [DBL_MIN, 2.2e-162]. With c1 written as (sin h / h)^2 / 2
#     and c2 as a series, there is no theta^2 left to underflow, so the guard
#     choice stopped mattering. The exact-zero form is kept because it is what the
#     operation actually requires.
# [measured: 7-mutant run over lie.cyr/lie_ext.cyr, 5 killed / 2 equivalent]

# --- c1 = (1 - cos t)/t^2 -> 1/2, read directly out of the translation. For
# omega = (0,0,t) and v = (1,0,0) the V matrix gives trans = (1 - c2 t^2, c1 t, 0),
# so trans_y / t IS c1 with no cancellation anywhere.
var _LC_RUN = 0;
var _LC_BAD = 0;
for (var _lc_e = 25; _lc_e < 500; _lc_e = _lc_e + 1) {
    var _lc_t = (1023 - _lc_e) << 52;
    var _lc_s = se3_exp(_twist_from(hvec3_new(0, 0, _lc_t), hvec3_new(F64_ONE, 0, 0)));
    _LC_RUN = _LC_RUN + 1;
    # ⚠ FROM 2^-25, AND THE BOUND IS DERIVED. c1 = 1/2 - t^2/24 + ..., so the
    # correction is under the ULP of 1/2 only once t^2/12 < 2^-53, i.e. t < 2^-24.4.
    # An earlier draft started at 2^-20 and failed decades 20..24 against CORRECT
    # output — the correction is real there, not noise.
    if (f64_div(HVec3_y(_se3_trans(_lc_s)), _lc_t) != 0x3FE0000000000000) {
        _LC_BAD = _LC_BAD + 1;
    }
}
assert_eq(_LC_RUN, 475, "the c1 sweep actually ran 475 angles");
assert_eq(_LC_BAD, 0,
  "se3_exp: (1-cos t)/t^2 is exactly 1/2 for angles from 2^-25 down to 2^-499");

# --- AND THE CROSSOVER ITSELF, at three angles that straddle it. The series is
# used below 0.1 and the closed form at or above it, so a wrong series, a wrong
# crossover or a discontinuity all show here. Values computed independently.
var _lx_a = _se3_trans(se3_exp(_twist_from(hvec3_new(0, 0,
        f64_div(F64_ONE, f64_from(20))), hvec3_new(F64_ONE, 0, 0))));       # t = 0.05
var _lx_b = _se3_trans(se3_exp(_twist_from(hvec3_new(0, 0,
        f64_div(F64_ONE, f64_from(10))), hvec3_new(F64_ONE, 0, 0))));       # t = 0.1
var _lx_c = _se3_trans(se3_exp(_twist_from(hvec3_new(0, 0,
        f64_div(F64_ONE, f64_from(5))), hvec3_new(F64_ONE, 0, 0))));        # t = 0.2
assert(f64_approx_eq(HVec3_x(_lx_a), 0x3FEFFC964BBF3D4B, LOOSE_TOL_M) == 1,
  "se3_exp at t = 0.05 (series side): trans_x = 0.9995833854135666");
assert(f64_approx_eq(HVec3_x(_lx_b), 0x3FEFF25A7E6EBDF7, LOOSE_TOL_M) == 1,
  "se3_exp at t = 0.1 (the crossover): trans_x = 0.9983341664682815");
assert(f64_approx_eq(HVec3_x(_lx_c), 0x3FEFC97EEC0D476B, LOOSE_TOL_M) == 1,
  "se3_exp at t = 0.2 (closed-form side): trans_x = 0.9933466539753061");

# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION: the identity of each group must still come back exactly.
assert_eq(HVec3_x(su2_log(su2_identity())), 0,
  "su2_log: the identity still has the zero logarithm");
assert_eq(HVec3_x(so3_log(m3_identity())), 0,
  "so3_log: the identity rotation still has the zero logarithm");
var _ls_z = se3_exp(_twist_from(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), hvec3_new(f64_from(7), 0, 0)));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_se3_trans(_ls_z)), f64_from(7),
  "se3_exp: a pure translation is still exactly itself");
# ⚠ And theta = pi must still take so3_log's eigenvector branch, where the
# antisymmetric part is exactly zero and atan2 alone cannot give the axis.
assert(f64_approx_eq(f64_abs(HVec3_z(so3_log(so3_exp(hvec3_new(0, 0, F64_PI))))),
    F64_PI, LOOSE_TOL_M) == 1,
  "so3_log: a rotation by exactly pi still recovers its axis and angle");

test_group("lie 2.15.0: the exponential maps are scale-covariant");

var _LE_RUN = 0;
var _LE_BAD = 0;
for (var _le_e = 30; _le_e < 530; _le_e = _le_e + 1) {
    var _le_t = (1023 - _le_e) << 52;             # 2^-e, well below the sin cutoff
    var _le_R = so3_exp(hvec3_new(0, 0, _le_t));
    _LE_RUN = _LE_RUN + 1;
    # Rodrigues about z: the (col 0, row 1) entry is sin(theta), and sin(t) == t
    # exactly at these scales, so this is an equality, not an approximation.
    if (m3_get(_le_R, 0, 1) != _le_t) { _LE_BAD = _LE_BAD + 1; }
    # and the mirrored entry is -sin(theta).
    if (m3_get(_le_R, 1, 0) != f64_neg(_le_t)) { _LE_BAD = _LE_BAD + 1; }
}
# ⚠ 530 and not further: `norm` is a sqrt of a sum of SQUARES, so below ~2^-537
# the square underflows and the norm itself returns 0 — a real limitation of the
# norm, not of the guard, and asserting past it would test f64, not this code.
assert_eq(_LE_RUN, 500, "the so3_exp angle sweep actually ran 500 scales");
assert_eq(_LE_BAD, 0,
  "so3_exp: R[1][0] is exactly sin(theta) for theta from 2^-30 down to 2^-529");

# ⭐ THE COVER INVARIANT, at a scale the existing check never reaches. It is
# already asserted at unit scale; below 1e-12 it was vacuously true because BOTH
# sides returned the identity. Repairing only one of the pair breaks it.
var _le_w = hvec3_new(0, 0, (1023 - 60) << 52);   # 2^-60
var _le_c1 = su2_to_rotation_matrix(su2_exp(_le_w));
var _le_c2 = so3_exp(_le_w);
assert_eq(m3_get(_le_c1, 0, 1), m3_get(_le_c2, 0, 1),
  "the SU(2) -> SO(3) cover still holds at 2^-60, where both used to return I");
assert(m3_get(_le_c2, 0, 1) != 0,
  "and it is not vacuous: both sides carry a non-zero rotation there");

# A tiny AXIS with an ordinary angle: the axis length is a unit choice and must
# not change the rotation at all.
var _le_ax = so3_from_axis_angle(hvec3_new(0, 0, (1023 - 200) << 52), F64_ONE);
assert(f64_approx_eq(m3_get(_le_ax, 0, 1), f64_sin(F64_ONE), LOOSE_TOL_M) == 1,
  "so3_from_axis_angle: a 2^-200-long axis still rotates by the given angle");
var _le_lb = lorentz_boost(hvec3_new((1023 - 200) << 52, 0, 0), F64_ONE);
assert(f64_approx_eq(m4_get(_le_lb, 0, 0), ganita_f64_cosh(F64_ONE), LOOSE_TOL_M) == 1,
  "lorentz_boost: a 2^-200-long direction still boosts by the given rapidity");

# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION: an exactly zero axis has no direction and must still return the
# identity, or the repair is a deletion.
assert_eq(m3_get(so3_from_axis_angle(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), F64_ONE), 0, 1), 0,
  "so3_from_axis_angle: a zero axis still returns the identity");
assert_eq(m3_get(so3_exp(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0)), 0, 1), 0,
  "so3_exp: a zero omega still returns the identity");
assert_eq(m4_get(lorentz_boost(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), F64_ONE), 0, 1), 0,
  "lorentz_boost: a zero direction still returns the identity");
var _lz_q = su2_from_axis_angle(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), F64_ONE);
assert_eq(_su2_w(_lz_q), F64_ONE, "su2_from_axis_angle: a zero axis still returns the identity, w = 1 (not cos(1/2))");
assert_eq(_su2_x(_lz_q), 0, "su2_from_axis_angle: a zero axis still returns the identity, x = 0");
var _lz_e = su2_exp(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0));
assert_eq(_su2_w(_lz_e), F64_ONE, "su2_exp: a zero omega still returns the identity, w = 1");
assert_eq(_su2_x(_lz_e), 0, "su2_exp: a zero omega still returns the identity, x = 0");
assert_eq(m4_get(lorentz_rotate(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), F64_ONE), 1, 2), 0,
  "lorentz_rotate: a zero axis still returns the identity");

# ⛔ 3.3.4 — THE 2^-200 AXIS TESTS ABOVE STOP THERE, AND BELOW DBL_MIN EVERY AXIS MAP STILL
# RETURNED THE IDENTITY. `su2_from_axis_angle`, `so3_from_axis_angle`, `lorentz_boost`
# and `lorentz_rotate` guarded `len < F64_TINY`, and `lie_norm3` returns subnormals since
# 2.17.0: (2^-k, 0, 0) gave the identity for 52 of 52 k from 1023 to 1074 (audit D086).
# At the top, a finite axis whose length passes DBL_MAX gave len = +Inf, inv = 0 and a
# zero direction: (DBL_MAX, DBL_MAX, 0) at pi/2 was (cos(pi/4), 0, 0, 0), not a unit.
# [measured: scratch harness and Decimal oracle, not reproducible in-tree, 2026-10-03; cycc 6.6.14]
# ⭐ Each now normalises an exact power-of-two copy of the axis, so the answer is BIT-FOR-
# BIT the answer for the same direction at an ordinary length, which is what is asserted:
# every word of each result against the result for the unit axis.
fn _la_words_differ(a, b, n) {
    var bad = 0;
    for (var i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) {
        if (load64(a + i * 8) != load64(b + i * 8)) { bad = bad + 1; }
    }
    return bad;
}
var _la_x = hvec3_new(F64_ONE, 0, 0);
var _la_q1 = su2_from_axis_angle(_la_x, F64_ONE);
var _la_r1 = so3_from_axis_angle(_la_x, F64_ONE);
var _la_b1 = lorentz_boost(_la_x, F64_ONE);
var _la_t1 = lorentz_rotate(_la_x, F64_ONE);
var _LA_RUN = 0;
var _LA_BAD = 0;
for (var _la_k = 0; _la_k < 52; _la_k = _la_k + 1) {
    var _la_v = hvec3_new(1 << (51 - _la_k), 0, 0);                     # 2^-1023 .. 2^-1074
    _LA_RUN = _LA_RUN + 1;
    _LA_BAD = _LA_BAD + _la_words_differ(su2_from_axis_angle(_la_v, F64_ONE), _la_q1, 4);
    _LA_BAD = _LA_BAD + _la_words_differ(so3_from_axis_angle(_la_v, F64_ONE), _la_r1, 9);
    _LA_BAD = _LA_BAD + _la_words_differ(lorentz_boost(_la_v, F64_ONE), _la_b1, 16);
    _LA_BAD = _LA_BAD + _la_words_differ(lorentz_rotate(_la_v, F64_ONE), _la_t1, 16);
}
assert_eq(_LA_RUN, 52, "the axis-map subnormal sweep ran 52 magnitudes");
assert_eq(_LA_BAD, 0, "su2/so3_from_axis_angle, lorentz_boost/_rotate: a 2^-k axis is the unit axis, every word");
var _la_d = hvec3_new(0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF, 0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF, 0);
var _la_o = hvec3_new(F64_ONE, F64_ONE, 0);
assert_eq(_la_words_differ(su2_from_axis_angle(_la_d, F64_PI_2), su2_from_axis_angle(_la_o, F64_PI_2), 4), 0,
  "su2_from_axis_angle: a (DBL_MAX, DBL_MAX, 0) axis is the (1, 1, 0) axis (3.3.3: x = y = 0)");
assert_eq(_la_words_differ(so3_from_axis_angle(_la_d, F64_PI_2), so3_from_axis_angle(_la_o, F64_PI_2), 9), 0,
  "so3_from_axis_angle: a (DBL_MAX, DBL_MAX, 0) axis is the (1, 1, 0) axis");
assert_eq(_la_words_differ(lorentz_boost(_la_d, F64_ONE), lorentz_boost(_la_o, F64_ONE), 16), 0,
  "lorentz_boost: a (DBL_MAX, DBL_MAX, 0) direction is the (1, 1, 0) direction (3.3.3: no boost in x, y)");
assert_eq(_la_words_differ(lorentz_rotate(_la_d, F64_PI_2), lorentz_rotate(_la_o, F64_PI_2), 16), 0,
  "lorentz_rotate: a (DBL_MAX, DBL_MAX, 0) axis is the (1, 1, 0) axis");
# A full-mantissa subnormal axis (|axis| ~ 2^-1025): all four words are the oracle's nearest.
var _la_f = su2_from_axis_angle(hvec3_new(0x0001ABCDEF012345, 0x80009876543210FF, 0x00004567890ABCDE), F64_ONE);
assert_eq(_su2_w(_la_f), 0x3FEC1528065B7D50, "su2_from_axis_angle, subnormal full-mantissa axis: w");
assert_eq(_su2_x(_la_f), 0x3FDC92295E7A440B, "su2_from_axis_angle, subnormal full-mantissa axis: x (3.3.3: 0)");
assert_eq(_su2_y(_la_f), 0xBFC45D491C9847C3, "su2_from_axis_angle, subnormal full-mantissa axis: y (3.3.3: 0)");
assert_eq(_su2_z(_la_f), 0x3FB28A6C6D18842C, "su2_from_axis_angle, subnormal full-mantissa axis: z (3.3.3: 0)");

# ⛔ 3.3.4 — AND THE TWO EXPONENTIAL MAPS: below DBL_MIN, `su2_exp` and `so3_exp` gave the
# identity for a non-zero omega (audit D086), so a 2^-1060 rotation came back as none.
# ⭐ so3_exp now returns I + [omega]x for a subnormal omega, so the rotation carries
# omega_k off the diagonal, bit-exact; su2_exp returns (1, omega/2) with each half TRUNCATED,
# because the true value lies just under |omega_i| / 2 and omega_i is subnormal. A tie
# (an odd mantissa) is where that differs from f64_mul(omega_i, 1/2): 3 * 2^-1074 halves to
# 1 * 2^-1074, not 2 * 2^-1074. Every word below is the nearest double to the exact value.
# [measured: scratch harness, Fraction and Decimal oracles, not reproducible in-tree, 2026-10-03; cycc 6.6.14]
var _LX_RUN = 0;
var _LX_BAD = 0;
for (var _lx_k = 0; _lx_k < 52; _lx_k = _lx_k + 1) {
    var _lx_s = 1 << (51 - _lx_k);                                      # 2^-1023 .. 2^-1074
    var _lx_R = so3_exp(hvec3_new(_lx_s, 0, 0));
    var _lx_U = su2_exp(hvec3_new(_lx_s, 0, 0));
    _LX_RUN = _LX_RUN + 1;
    if (m3_get(_lx_R, 1, 2) != _lx_s) { _LX_BAD = _LX_BAD + 1; }
    if (m3_get(_lx_R, 2, 1) != f64_neg(_lx_s)) { _LX_BAD = _LX_BAD + 1; }
    if (m3_get(_lx_R, 1, 1) != F64_ONE) { _LX_BAD = _LX_BAD + 1; }
    if (_su2_x(_lx_U) != (_lx_s >> 1)) { _LX_BAD = _LX_BAD + 1; }       # 2^-(k+1); 0 at k = 1074
    if (_su2_w(_lx_U) != F64_ONE) { _LX_BAD = _LX_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_LX_RUN, 52, "the exp-map subnormal sweep ran 52 magnitudes");
assert_eq(_LX_BAD, 0, "so3_exp / su2_exp: a 2^-k omega rotates by 2^-k, bit-exact (3.3.3: the identity)");
var _lx_t = su2_exp(hvec3_new(3, 0x8000000000000007, 0x0001ABCDEF012345));
assert_eq(_su2_x(_lx_t), 1, "su2_exp: 3 * 2^-1074 halves to 1 * 2^-1074, the nearest double (a tie to even gives 2)");
assert_eq(_su2_y(_lx_t), 0x8000000000000003,
  "su2_exp: -7 * 2^-1074 halves to -3 * 2^-1074, sign kept (a tie to even gives -4)");
assert_eq(_su2_z(_lx_t), 0x0000D5E6F78091A2, "su2_exp: a full-mantissa odd component halves down, too");
# Alone, so the norm is the component: the general lines would round norm/2 first and give
# 2 * 2^-1074 for (3, 0, 0) and 3 * 2^-1074 for (5, 5, 0), where 1 and 2 are nearest.
assert_eq(_su2_x(su2_exp(hvec3_new(3, 0, 0))), 1, "su2_exp((3, 0, 0) * 2^-1074): x = 1 * 2^-1074");
assert_eq(_su2_y(su2_exp(hvec3_new(5, 5, 0))), 2, "su2_exp((5, 5, 0) * 2^-1074): y = 2 * 2^-1074");
assert_eq(_su2_x(su2_exp(hvec3_new(0x8000000000000000, 0, 0))), 0,
  "su2_exp((-0, 0, 0)): still the identity, bit for bit (+0, not omega's -0)");
var _lx_W = so3_exp(hvec3_new(0x0001ABCDEF012346, 0x80009876543210FE, 0x00004567890ABCDE));
assert_eq(m3_get(_lx_W, 0, 1), 0x00004567890ABCDE, "so3_exp, full-mantissa subnormal omega: R[1][0] = omega_z");
assert_eq(m3_get(_lx_W, 0, 2), 0x00009876543210FE, "so3_exp, full-mantissa subnormal omega: R[2][0] = -omega_y");
assert_eq(m3_get(_lx_W, 1, 2), 0x0001ABCDEF012346, "so3_exp, full-mantissa subnormal omega: R[2][1] = omega_x");
assert_eq(m3_get(_lx_W, 2, 1), 0x8001ABCDEF012346, "so3_exp, full-mantissa subnormal omega: R[1][2] = -omega_x");
# Routed through so3_from_axis_angle, sin(theta) * n_z rounds twice on the subnormal grid and
# this one comes back as omega_z + 1 ulp.
# [measured: verify-liever scratch harness, not reproducible in-tree, 2026-10-03; cycc 6.6.14]
var _lx_V = so3_exp(hvec3_new(0x00043E285B7A3E62, 0x8000785F8135E277, 0x000BDD39D112E272));
assert_eq(m3_get(_lx_V, 0, 1), 0x000BDD39D112E272, "so3_exp, subnormal omega: R[1][0] = omega_z, not omega_z + 1 ulp");
assert_eq(m3_get(_lx_V, 1, 0), 0x800BDD39D112E272, "so3_exp, subnormal omega: R[0][1] = -omega_z");
# The SU(2) -> SO(3) cover below DBL_MIN, where both sides were the identity through 3.3.3.
var _lx_w = hvec3_new(0, 0, 0x0000000000400000);                       # 2^-1052, even mantissa
assert_eq(m3_get(su2_to_rotation_matrix(su2_exp(_lx_w)), 0, 1), m3_get(so3_exp(_lx_w), 0, 1),
  "the SU(2) -> SO(3) cover holds at 2^-1052");
assert_eq(m3_get(so3_exp(_lx_w), 0, 1), 0x0000000000400000, "and it is not vacuous: R[1][0] is 2^-1052 there");

# ⛔ 2.15.0 LEFT TWO SITES ALONE, WITH THE REASON MEASURED; 2.16.0 REPAIRED THEM.
# `se3_exp` and `se3_log` (lie_ext.cyr) divided by theta^2 and theta^3.
# Lowering their guard to DBL_MIN as the census recommended would have introduced
# a NEW defect: at theta <= 2.2e-162 both `theta*theta` and `1 - cos(theta)` are
# exactly 0, so `c1 = (1-cos)/theta^2` is 0/0 = NaN and the whole V matrix becomes
# NaN. They needed a small-angle series, a formula change rather than a threshold
# change — the same reason `so3_log` was excluded. 2.16.0 shipped it: a half-angle
# c1, series below `_LIE_SERIES_CUT`, and `atan2` in the log maps; the "2.16.0 —
# the small-angle series" block above tests them.
# [measured: 1-cos and theta^2 both underflow to 0 at theta = 1e-162]

test_group("complex 2.15.0: cx_div / cx_inv are scale-covariant");

fn _cx_relbad(got, want) {
    # Relative, not absolute: the expected value spans 400 decades here, so an
    # absolute tolerance would be vacuous at one end and impossible at the other.
    if (f64_eq(want, 0) == 1) {
        if (f64_eq(got, 0) == 1) { return 0; }
        return 1;
    }
    if (f64_lt(f64_abs(f64_sub(got, want)),
        f64_mul(f64_div(F64_ONE, f64_from(1000000000)), f64_abs(want))) == 1) {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

var _CX_RUN = 0;
var _CX_BAD = 0;
var _cx_e = 0 - 200;
while (_cx_e <= 200) {
    # k = 10^e, rebuilt from 1 each iteration so rounding does not accumulate.
    var _cx_k = F64_ONE;
    var _cx_i = 0;
    if (_cx_e >= 0) {
        while (_cx_i < _cx_e) { _cx_k = f64_mul(_cx_k, f64_from(10)); _cx_i = _cx_i + 1; }
    }
    if (_cx_e < 0) {
        while (_cx_i < (0 - _cx_e)) { _cx_k = f64_div(_cx_k, f64_from(10)); _cx_i = _cx_i + 1; }
    }
    var _cx_a = cx_new(_cx_k, f64_mul(_cx_k, F64_TWO));
    var _cx_b = cx_new(f64_mul(_cx_k, f64_from(3)), f64_mul(_cx_k, f64_from(4)));
    _CX_RUN = _CX_RUN + 1;
    # (1+2i)/(3+4i) = 0.44 + 0.08i at EVERY common scale k.
    var _cx_q = cx_div(_cx_a, _cx_b);
    _CX_BAD = _CX_BAD + _cx_relbad(HComplex_re(_cx_q), f64_div(f64_from(44), f64_from(100)));
    _CX_BAD = _CX_BAD + _cx_relbad(HComplex_im(_cx_q), f64_div(f64_from(8), f64_from(100)));
    # 1/(k(3+4i)) = (3 - 4i)/(25k) — this one DOES carry k, linearly.
    # [measured: docs/audit/2026-09-09-epsilon-open.json]
    var _cx_v = cx_inv(_cx_b);
    _CX_BAD = _CX_BAD + _cx_relbad(HComplex_re(_cx_v),
      f64_div(f64_from(3), f64_mul(f64_from(25), _cx_k)));
    _CX_BAD = _CX_BAD + _cx_relbad(HComplex_im(_cx_v),
      f64_neg(f64_div(f64_from(4), f64_mul(f64_from(25), _cx_k))));
    _cx_e = _cx_e + 1;
}
# ⚠ `cx_powf` needs its OWN sweep and the first draft of this block had none —
# its mutant SURVIVED. In 2.15.0 its guard was a threshold on |a|², argued to be
# the right shape because cx_abs was then `sqrt(cx_norm_sq)`, so the value was
# already gone once |a|² was subnormal. 2.17.0 refuted that premise: cx_abs is
# exact at every scale now, and cx_powf guards the modulus with an exact-zero test
# (`f64_eq(m, 0)`), so there is no scale left to choose. This sweep pins the
# result scale-free; the "complex 2.17.0: the modulus is scale-free" group below
# covers the band the old |a|² guard fabricated a zero over.
# ⚠ Narrower range than the sweep above, and deliberately: this squares, so k²
# must stay representable — at k = 1e±150 the result is ~1e±300, still normal.
var _CP_RUN = 0;
var _CP_BAD = 0;
var _cp_e = 0 - 150;
while (_cp_e <= 150) {
    var _cp_k = F64_ONE;
    var _cp_i = 0;
    if (_cp_e >= 0) {
        while (_cp_i < _cp_e) { _cp_k = f64_mul(_cp_k, f64_from(10)); _cp_i = _cp_i + 1; }
    }
    if (_cp_e < 0) {
        while (_cp_i < (0 - _cp_e)) { _cp_k = f64_div(_cp_k, f64_from(10)); _cp_i = _cp_i + 1; }
    }
    # (k(3+4i))² = k²(-7 + 24i) at every scale.
    var _cp_r = cx_powf(cx_new(f64_mul(_cp_k, f64_from(3)),
        f64_mul(_cp_k, f64_from(4))), F64_TWO);
    var _cp_k2 = f64_mul(_cp_k, _cp_k);
    _CP_RUN = _CP_RUN + 1;
    _CP_BAD = _CP_BAD + _cx_relbad(HComplex_re(_cp_r),
      f64_mul(_cp_k2, f64_neg(f64_from(7))));
    _CP_BAD = _CP_BAD + _cx_relbad(HComplex_im(_cp_r), f64_mul(_cp_k2, f64_from(24)));
    _cp_e = _cp_e + 1;
}
assert_eq(_CP_RUN, 301, "the cx_powf scale sweep actually ran 301 decades");
assert_eq(_CP_BAD, 0,
  "cx_powf: (k(3+4i))^2 = k^2(-7+24i) at every scale from 1e-150 to 1e+150");

assert_eq(_CX_RUN, 401, "the complex scale sweep actually ran 401 decades");
assert_eq(_CX_BAD, 0,
  "cx_div / cx_inv: exact at every common scale from 1e-200 to 1e+200");

# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION. The pole is documented and must keep its sentinel, or the
# repair is a deletion: both components of the divisor exactly zero.
# ⚠ Bit-exact, not `cx_is_zero` — that takes a TOLERANCE, and a tolerance-based
# check would pass on a near-zero fabrication, which is the very thing under test.
var _cx_p0 = cx_div(cx_new(F64_ONE, F64_TWO), cx_new(0, 0));
assert_eq(HComplex_re(_cx_p0), 0,
  "cx_div: an exactly zero divisor still returns the zero sentinel (re)");
assert_eq(HComplex_im(_cx_p0), 0,
  "cx_div: an exactly zero divisor still returns the zero sentinel (im)");
var _cx_p1 = cx_inv(cx_new(0, 0));
assert_eq(HComplex_re(_cx_p1), 0,
  "cx_inv: inverting exactly zero still returns the zero sentinel (re)");
assert_eq(HComplex_im(_cx_p1), 0,
  "cx_inv: inverting exactly zero still returns the zero sentinel (im)");
# ⚠ And a purely-imaginary divisor exercises the OTHER branch of Smith's, which
# an all-real fixture would never reach: (1+2i)/(2i) = 1 - 0.5i.
var _cx_pi = cx_div(cx_new(F64_ONE, F64_TWO), cx_new(0, F64_TWO));
assert_eq(HComplex_re(_cx_pi), 0x3FF0000000000000,
  "cx_div: a purely imaginary divisor takes the second branch and gives re = 1");
assert_eq(HComplex_im(_cx_pi), 0xBFE0000000000000,
  "cx_div: a purely imaginary divisor gives im = -0.5");

test_group("complex 2.17.0: the modulus is scale-free");

# ⛔ `cx_abs` SQUARED BEFORE SUMMING, and it is the modulus that `cx_ln`,
# `cx_sqrt`, `cx_powf` and `cmat_exp` are every one of them built on — so ONE
# defect reached four public functions. Below 2^-537 `re*re` underflowed to zero
# and above 2^511 it overflowed, so |3k + 4ki| came back as exactly 0 or exactly
# +Inf where 5k is perfectly representable. Measured on the 2.16.0 tree: **994 of
# these 2041 scales were wrong**, 483 below 1 and 511 at or above it.
# ⭐ (3k, 4k) -> 5k is the fixture because all three are EXACT at every power of
# two, so this compares bit for bit with no tolerance to hide behind.
# ⚠ BOTH BANDS IN ONE LOOP. A one-sided sweep — the shape every earlier scale
# sweep in this file used — would have left the overflow half untested, and that
# half is the majority of the failures.
# ⚠ What this sweep does NOT discriminate, said out loud: WIDENING the fast-path
# trigger is a pure pessimisation, not a defect, because the rescue is exact at
# every scale. Only narrowing it (to `== 0`, which leaves the subnormal band up to
# 13.1% wrong) or deleting it changes an answer, and the sweep kills both — the
# subnormal band is inside its range, at e = -539.
# [measured: scratch two-sided binade probe on the 2.16.0 tree, 2026-09-10]
# [measured: 10-mutant run over complex.cyr + vec2.cyr — 9 killed, 2026-09-10]
var _CA_RUN = 0;
var _CA_BAD = 0;
for (var _ca_e = 0 - 1020; _ca_e <= 1020; _ca_e = _ca_e + 1) {
    var _ca_k = _gp2(_ca_e);
    _CA_RUN = _CA_RUN + 1;
    if (cx_abs(cx_new(f64_mul(f64_from(3), _ca_k), f64_mul(f64_from(4), _ca_k)))
      != f64_mul(f64_from(5), _ca_k)) { _CA_BAD = _CA_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_CA_RUN, 2041, "the cx_abs binade sweep actually ran 2041 scales");
assert_eq(_CA_BAD, 0,
  "cx_abs: |3k+4ki| = 5k bit-exact for k = 2^-1020 .. 2^1020");

# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION and the boundary contracts, none of which the sweep reaches.
assert_eq(cx_abs(cx_zero()), 0, "cx_abs: an exactly zero argument still has modulus 0");
assert_eq(cx_abs(cx_new(F64_POS_INF, 0)), F64_POS_INF, "cx_abs: +Inf in, +Inf out");
assert_eq(cx_abs(cx_new(0, f64_neg(F64_POS_INF))), F64_POS_INF,
  "cx_abs: a negative infinite leg still gives +Inf");
# NaN is the only value not equal to itself, which is the portable test here.
var _ca_nan = cx_abs(cx_new(0x7FF8000000000000, 0));
assert_eq(f64_eq(_ca_nan, _ca_nan), 0, "cx_abs: NaN propagates, it is not zeroed");

# ⭐ `cx_ln` AND `cx_sqrt` ARE REPAIRED WITH NO CHANGE AT THEIR OWN LINES, and
# these are the assertions that say so. Through 2.16.0 `cx_ln(2^-600 + 0i).re` was
# `ln(0)` = -Inf and `cx_sqrt(2^-600 + 0i).re` was 0; both now agree bit for bit
# with the real-valued stdlib call over the whole band the defect had eaten.
# ⚠ Asserted against `f64_ln`/`f64_sqrt` rather than a hand-written constant so
# the check cannot drift from the stdlib it is built on.
var _CL_RUN = 0;
var _CL_BAD = 0;
for (var _cl_e = 0 - 1000; _cl_e <= 0 - 512; _cl_e = _cl_e + 1) {
    var _cl_k = _gp2(_cl_e);
    _CL_RUN = _CL_RUN + 1;
    if (HComplex_re(cx_ln(cx_new(_cl_k, 0))) != f64_ln(_cl_k)) { _CL_BAD = _CL_BAD + 1; }
    if (HComplex_re(cx_sqrt(cx_new(_cl_k, 0))) != f64_sqrt(_cl_k)) { _CL_BAD = _CL_BAD + 1; }
    # cx_powf(k + 0i, 1) is k, and this is the band the old |a|^2 guard fabricated
    # a zero over: 2^-600 came back as exactly 0.
    if (HComplex_re(cx_powf(cx_new(_cl_k, 0), F64_ONE)) != _cl_k) { _CL_BAD = _CL_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_CL_RUN, 489, "the cx_ln / cx_sqrt / cx_powf inheritance sweep ran 489 scales");
assert_eq(_CL_BAD, 0,
  "cx_ln / cx_sqrt / cx_powf: exact from 2^-1000 to 2^-512, where all three were dead");

# ⚠ AND A SUBNORMAL MODULUS, WHICH THE SWEEP ABOVE CANNOT REACH. `_gp2` encodes
# only NORMAL powers of two, so the loop bottoms out at DBL_MIN however deep it
# runs — and a repair that guards the modulus at `F64_TINY` instead of at zero
# agrees with the real guard everywhere the sweep looks. Measured: with the sweep
# alone that mutant SURVIVES; this one assertion kills it. There is no scale to
# choose here, and this is the assertion that says so.
# [measured: 10-mutant run over complex.cyr and vec2.cyr, 2026-09-10]
var _cp_sub = 1 << 24;                            # 2^-1050, subnormal
assert_eq(HComplex_re(cx_powf(cx_new(_cp_sub, 0), F64_ONE)), _cp_sub,
  "cx_powf: a subnormal modulus survives — only an exactly zero base is guarded");

# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION for cx_powf: the zero sentinel and the documented 0^0 stay.
assert_eq(HComplex_re(cx_powf(cx_zero(), F64_ONE)), 0,
  "cx_powf: an exactly zero base still returns the zero sentinel");
assert_eq(HComplex_re(cx_powf(cx_zero(), 0)), 0,
  "cx_powf: 0^0 is still the documented zero, not ganita's 1.0");

# ⛔ `cx_is_zero` SQUARED BOTH SIDES — the value AND the tolerance the caller
# passed. It is dimensionally consistent, which is why it survived every earlier
# pass over this module, and it is still wrong at both ends: below ~1.5e-154 both
# squares flush to exactly 0 and `0 < 0` is false, above ~1.3e154 both become +Inf
# and `+Inf < +Inf` is false. Either way the answer is a confident 0.
# ⚠ These four are DIRECTIONAL PAIRS. Asserting only the `== 1` cases would pass
# on a `return 1` stub; asserting only the `== 0` cases passes on the defect
# itself, which answered 0 everywhere it failed.
assert_eq(cx_is_zero(cx_new(_gp2(0 - 700), 0), _gp2(0 - 600)), 1,
  "cx_is_zero: 2^-700 is below a 2^-600 tolerance (both squares used to flush)");
assert_eq(cx_is_zero(cx_new(_gp2(0 - 600), 0), _gp2(0 - 700)), 0,
  "cx_is_zero: 2^-600 is NOT below a 2^-700 tolerance");
assert_eq(cx_is_zero(cx_new(_gp2(600), 0), _gp2(700)), 1,
  "cx_is_zero: 2^600 is below a 2^700 tolerance (both squares used to overflow)");
assert_eq(cx_is_zero(cx_new(_gp2(700), 0), _gp2(600)), 0,
  "cx_is_zero: 2^700 is NOT below a 2^600 tolerance");
# The comparison is strict, and an exact zero is below ANY positive tolerance.
assert_eq(cx_is_zero(cx_new(_gp2(0 - 600), 0), _gp2(0 - 600)), 0,
  "cx_is_zero: a modulus exactly equal to the tolerance is not zero");
assert_eq(cx_is_zero(cx_zero(), _gp2(0 - 1000)), 1,
  "cx_is_zero: exact zero is below even a 2^-1000 tolerance");

test_group("2.17.0: the last two norm sites");

# ⛔ `cga_norm` runs a GEOMETRIC PRODUCT, so every term is a product of two
# coefficients and the whole class applies. `cga_norm(k*e1)` — whose answer is
# exactly k — was wrong on **992 of 2041 binades**, returning 0 at 2^-600 and
# overflowing above 2^511.
# ⭐ THE RESCALE IS BY A POWER OF TWO AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING. An arbitrary
# divisor would perturb the exact cancellation that makes a null blade read
# bit-exactly 0, and four assertions in tests/hisab.tcyr depend on it.
# [measured: 2041-binade two-sided sweep before and after, 2026-09-10]
var _CN_RUN = 0;
var _CN_BAD = 0;
for (var _cn_e = 0 - 1020; _cn_e <= 1020; _cn_e = _cn_e + 1) {
    var _cn_k = (1023 + _cn_e) << 52;
    var _cn_mv = cga_mv_zero();
    cga_mv_set(_cn_mv, 1, _cn_k);
    _CN_RUN = _CN_RUN + 1;
    if (cga_norm(_cn_mv) != _cn_k) { _CN_BAD = _CN_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_CN_RUN, 2041, "the cga_norm binade sweep actually ran 2041 scales");
assert_eq(_CN_BAD, 0, "cga_norm: |k*e1| = k bit-exact from 2^-1020 to 2^1020");

# ⚠ AND A SUBNORMAL COEFFICIENT, which the sweep above cannot reach — the
# power-of-two floor that `linalg_precision.cyr` uses reads the EXPONENT FIELD,
# which is 0 for every subnormal, so it would return 1.0 and rescale by nothing.
# The version here falls back to the mantissa's highest set bit. ⚠ And the scaled
# copy must DIVIDE: three reviewers measured the `1/s` form returning NaN across
# exactly this band, because `1/s` is +Inf and every zero slot becomes `0 * Inf`.
# [measured: 2026-09-09 norm census verifiers; 8-mutant run, 2026-09-10]
var _cn_sub = cga_mv_zero();
cga_mv_set(_cn_sub, 1, 1 << 44);                  # 2^-1030, subnormal
assert_eq(cga_norm(_cn_sub), 1 << 44,
  "cga_norm: a subnormal coefficient survives, and does not become 0 * Inf = NaN");

# ⭐ AND THE NULL BLADE AT EXTREME SCALE — the assertion the power-of-two choice
# exists for, and the one the four unit-scale null-blade checks in
# tests/hisab.tcyr cannot make, because at unit scale the rescue never runs at
# all. `cga_point(3,4,0)` has q = 25, which is exactly representable, so its norm
# is exactly 0 and scaling by a power of two keeps it there — and now the rescue
# path IS taken. ⚠ Exactly 0 because q is EXACT here, not because every conformal
# point is bit-exactly null; 2.21.0's claim is a sub-ulp residual, not a hard zero
# (see `_cga_scalar_of_geo`). Measured: with an
# arbitrary `mx` divisor instead of a power of two, the exact cancellation is
# perturbed and the norm comes back NON-ZERO at every one of these scales — the
# mutant survived until this block existed.
# [measured: 3-scale null-point probe with the mx divisor installed, 2026-09-10]
var _cn_np = cga_point(f64_from(3), f64_from(4), 0);
assert_eq(cga_norm(cga_mv_scale(_cn_np, (1023 - 600) << 52)), 0,
  "cga_norm: a null point scaled to 2^-600 still reads exactly 0");
assert_eq(cga_norm(cga_mv_scale(_cn_np, (1023 - 900) << 52)), 0,
  "cga_norm: and at 2^-900");
assert_eq(cga_norm(cga_mv_scale(_cn_np, (1023 + 700) << 52)), 0,
  "cga_norm: and at 2^700, where the rescue runs on the overflow side");

# ⛔ 2.18.0 — AND `cga_norm_sq` DESTROYED ITS OWN ANSWER BY THE ORDER IT ADDED IN.
# A translator is a UNIT versor — `e_inf² = 0`, so `T * rev(T) = 1` identically —
# and its scalar part accumulates `1 + t²/4 - t²/4`. Once `t²/4` passes 2^53 the
# leading 1 is rounded away and the answer comes back **0 instead of 1**:
# measured, `cga_norm(cga_translator(t,0,0))` stopped being 1 at **t = 2^28**, an
# entirely ordinary translation distance, and 993 of 2041 scales were wrong.
# ⚠ NOT the norm class, and 2.17.0's power-of-two rescale provably cannot help —
# it is exact, so it moves every term by the same factor and the cancellation is
# untouched. Measured identical before and after that repair.
# ⭐ NEUMAIER, NOT KAHAN, and the distinction is measurable: Kahan folds its
# correction into the next ADDEND, and with `sum = 1` and addend `2^54` the
# correction 1 is below the ulp of 2^54 and is simply lost. Neumaier picks its
# branch by which operand is larger and keeps the correction to the end.
# ⭐ The scalar part needed only the 32 DIAGONAL blade pairs, so this stopped
# building a whole multivector to read one number out of it.
# ⛔ 2.21.0 — THAT SENTENCE USED TO END "and every one has i == j", WHICH THE NULL
# BASIS MADE FALSE. Still 32 pairs of 1024 produce blade 0, but now only **16 are
# diagonal**: the other 16 are the eight complementary (n0-side, ninf-side) blade
# pairs in both orders, because `n0*ninf` has a scalar part where an orthonormal
# basis has none. `_cga_scalar_of_geo` sums all pairs for exactly this reason, and
# leaving the loop diagonal-only made every sampled point non-null.
# [measured: 32x32 table scan of _cga_geo_blades for blade 0, 2026-09-11]
# ⚠ **First failure moves 2^28 -> 2^538, and the residue is inherent.** Beyond
# that the rescue's own arithmetic runs out: scaling by `s` makes the scalar term
# `1/s`, whose square `1/s²` underflows once `s > 2^537`. A translator at 2^539
# has coefficients spanning 538 binades and their SQUARES span 2^1076, which is
# wider than the double range — no single scale keeps both ends.
# [measured: 2041-binade translator sweep before and after, 2026-09-10]
var _CT_RUN = 0;
var _CT_BAD = 0;
for (var _ct_e = 0 - 1020; _ct_e <= 538; _ct_e = _ct_e + 1) {
    var _ct_t = cga_translator((1023 + _ct_e) << 52, 0, 0);
    _CT_RUN = _CT_RUN + 1;
    if (cga_norm(_ct_t) != F64_ONE) { _CT_BAD = _CT_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_CT_RUN, 1559, "the translator sweep actually ran 1559 scales");
assert_eq(_CT_BAD, 0,
  "cga_norm: a translator is a UNIT versor at every distance from 2^-1020 to 2^538");

# ⚠ AND THE HONEST CONSEQUENCE FOR CONFORMAL POINTS, recorded rather than hidden,
# because this is a BEHAVIOUR CHANGE on a public function. (2.18.0, in the ep/em
# basis; 2.21.0's null basis replaced the representation this paragraph and the
# bound below describe.) A point is null, and the old accumulation returned
# exactly 0 for 1010 of 1101 positions — but that 0 was ROUNDING LUCK, not a
# property of the stored multivector: `cga_point` added `x²/2` to `±0.5`, and for
# small x that term rounded away entirely, so the point it actually stored had
# `norm_sq = x²`. The compensated sum reports that truth, and exact zeros drop
# 1010 -> 53.
# ⭐ Judged against the point's OWN scale — its largest coefficient, which is the
# unit-weight e_o part, not x — nullity is essentially unchanged: **1010 -> 970**
# within 1e-14, and the WORST relative nullity defect is identical either way
# (5e-9, at x = 2^-27). The residue was `cga_point`'s representation, not this
# function's arithmetic: the ep/em basis cannot hold `x²/2 ± 1/2` for small x.
# [measured: 1101-position sweep, exact and relative, before and after]
var _CP_RUN2 = 0;
var _CP_BAD2 = 0;
for (var _cp_e2 = 0 - 500; _cp_e2 <= 500; _cp_e2 = _cp_e2 + 1) {
    var _cp_pt = cga_point((1023 + _cp_e2) << 52, 0, 0);
    var _cp_mx = f64_from(0);
    for (var _cp_q = 0; _cp_q < 32; _cp_q = _cp_q + 1) {
        var _cp_a = f64_abs(cga_mv_get(_cp_pt, _cp_q));
        if (f64_gt(_cp_a, _cp_mx) == 1) { _cp_mx = _cp_a; }
    }
    _CP_RUN2 = _CP_RUN2 + 1;
    # ⚠ 2.21.0 — THIS BOUND WAS DERIVED FOR THE ep/em BASIS, AND IT NO LONGER
    # DISCRIMINATES. In that basis `cga_point` stored `x²/2 ± 1/2`, and ulp(0.5) is
    # 2^-53, so the whole `x²/2` term rounded away once it fell below half of that
    # — at x ~ 2^-26.5. The residue was then the point's own `x`, i.e.
    # `x / 0.5 = 2x` relative to its largest coefficient, and the worst case sat
    # right at the crossover: measured **exactly 2^-26, at x = 2^-27**.
    # [measured: 1001-position relative-nullity sweep, 2026-09-10]
    #
    # The null basis stores no such sum, and every on-axis point this loop builds
    # now reads EXACTLY 0: 0 of 1001 non-zero, and 0 of 1001 with a full mantissa,
    # so the 2^-26 bound sits far above anything these on-axis points produce.
    # [measured: scratch on-axis nullity probe, not reproducible in-tree, 2026-09-30; cycc 6.6.12]
    # It still catches a gross defect: a `cga_point` that stores q instead of q/2
    # on ninf fails 51 of these 1001 positions. But it passes any residue up to
    # 2^-26, the old ep/em one included, so a basis regression would get through.
    # [measured: scratch mutation, ninf = q instead of q/2, not reproducible in-tree, 2026-10-01; cycc 6.6.12]
    # Replacing the sweep with multi-coordinate full-mantissa points and a bound
    # derived on the null basis is roadmap [3.3.5], audit D047.
    if (f64_gt(f64_div(cga_norm(_cp_pt), _cp_mx), (1023 - 26) << 52) == 1) {
        _CP_BAD2 = _CP_BAD2 + 1;
    }
}
assert_eq(_CP_RUN2, 1001, "the conformal-point nullity sweep actually ran 1001 positions");
assert_eq(_CP_BAD2, 0,
  "cga_point: every point is null to 2^-26 of its own largest coefficient, from 2^-500 to 2^500");

# === 3.3.4 D048: q/2 and r^2/2 at both ends of the range ===
# Through 3.3.3 cga_point stored 0.5 * (x*x + y*y + z*z): +Inf once |p| passed ~2^512,
# although q/2 is finite up to 2^512.5, and one subnormal step off below 2^-510.5,
# where the squares and then the halving each rounded. cga_sphere's 0.5 * (r * r)
# had both faults too. Each expected value is the correctly rounded q/2 (or -r^2/2)
# from an exact rational oracle, except the covariance sweep's _d48_h0 below, which
# is hisab's own; where 3.3.3 differed, its value is in the message.
# [measured: D048 fixtures vs a fractions oracle, 3.3.4 base and this tree, 2026-10-03; cycc 6.6.14]
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_point(0x5FF3A5C91E2B7D41, 0, 0), 5), 0x7FE82073759590AF,
  "cga_point: q/2 at |p| = 1.23 * 2^512 is finite (3.3.3: +Inf)");
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_point(0x5FE4F1D2C3B4A597, 0x5FE5E0F1A2B3C4D5, 0x5FE3C2B1A0F9E8D7), 5),
  0x7FE46F5464836679, "cga_point: three coordinates near 1.3 * 2^511 whose squares sum past 2^1024");
# ON the overflow boundary: the largest x whose x^2/2 rounds to a finite double, and
# the next double up, whose x^2/2 rounds to +Inf.
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_point(0x5FF6A09E667F3BCC, 0, 0), 5), 0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFE,
  "cga_point: the largest x with a finite q/2 stores it (3.3.3: +Inf)");
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_point(0x5FF6A09E667F3BCD, 0, 0), 5), F64_POS_INF,
  "cga_point: one double further, q/2 itself overflows to +Inf");
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_point(0x1F051E03FC260FF5, 0, 0), 5), 0x00000000000DEF81,
  "cga_point: a subnormal q/2 from the scaled sum, correctly rounded here (3.3.3: 0xDEF82, a step off)");
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_point(0x1FAA3B4856AB8CEF, 0x1FAA516B808B7DE5, 0x1F8D94AA6A34E681), 5),
  0x000002CDB63B3869, "cga_point: three coordinates, subnormal q/2 (3.3.3: 0x2CDB63B386A)");
# The scaled form's lower edge is 2^-484, not 2^-511, because between the two a square
# below DBL_MIN can still move the plain sum's last bit. Built to sit on that tie:
# x^2 = 2^-1000 (1 + 2^-19 + 2^-40) is exact and even in its last place, and y^2 is just
# above 2^-1053, half an ulp of x^2. Plain, y^2 rounds onto the subnormal grid at exactly
# 2^-1053 and the sum ties down to x^2; scaled, it stays above the tie and rounds up.
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_point(0x20B0000100000000, 0x1F06A09E667F3BCD, 0), 5), 0x0160000200001001,
  "cga_point: a subnormal square below the lower edge rounds up past the tie (3.3.3: one ulp low)");
# A subnormal coordinate: the scale is subnormal too, and dividing by it is exact
# where multiplying by its reciprocal (+Inf) would make q/2 NaN.
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_point(0x0000000001234567, 0, 0), 5), 0,
  "cga_point: a subnormal coordinate gives q/2 = +0, not NaN");
# ON the plain form's own overflow edge, 2^512 / sqrt(3): two ulps above the smallest
# equal coordinates whose x*x + y*y + z*z overflows (0x5FE279A74590331C). The window's
# upper edge, 2^511, sits below it; an edge above it would take the plain form here.
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_point(0x5FE279A74590331E, 0x5FE279A74590331C, 0x5FE279A74590331C), 5),
  0x7FE0000000000001, "cga_point: three coordinates at 2^512 / sqrt(3), where the plain sum overflows");
# ON the lower edge's derived boundary: the tie above, rebuilt at 2^-484.51, just below
# 2^-484.5, the last point where the sum's ulp is 2^-1022 and a subnormal square can still
# move its last bit (x = 2^-485 (1 + 2^-2 + 2^-3 + 2^-5 + 2^-20), x^2 exact and even).
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_point(0x21A6800100000000, 0x1FF6A09E667F3BCD, 0), 5), 0x034FA402D0001001,
  "cga_point: the subnormal-square tie at 2^-484.51 rounds up (the plain sum is one ulp low)");
# An infinite coordinate keeps the plain form: q/2 is +Inf, not Inf / Inf = NaN.
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_point(F64_POS_INF, 0, 0), 5), F64_POS_INF,
  "cga_point: an infinite coordinate gives q/2 = +Inf");
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_sphere(0, 0, 0, 0x5FF1B7E3C5A9D2F1), 5), 0xFFE39F057AFCEF36,
  "cga_sphere: -r^2/2 at r = 1.11 * 2^512 is finite (3.3.3: -Inf)");
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_sphere(0, 0, 0, 0x1EAF748741104CFD), 5), 0x80000000000001EF,
  "cga_sphere: a subnormal r^2/2 is rounded once (3.3.3: -0x1EE units, rounded twice)");
# Scale covariance: q/2 of 2^k p is 4^k times q/2 of p, rounded once, for every k
# from 2^-1020 up to 2^512, where 3.3.3 overflowed. p's largest coordinate is in [1, 2)
# and its q/2 in [0.5, 1), so every k from -1020 to 512 has a finite answer. _d48_h0
# is hisab's unit-scale value, one ulp below the correctly rounded ...A440; the
# sweep compares every scale against it, not against the oracle.
# [measured: this sweep; the 3.3.4 base tree fails 9 of its 1533 scales, scratch, 2026-10-03; cycc 6.6.14]
var _d48_x = 0x3FF17FFCAAA8987B;
var _d48_y = 0x3FD3F1E031603081;
var _d48_z = 0xBFC8CD3414275CEC;
var _d48_h0 = cga_mv_get(cga_point(_d48_x, _d48_y, _d48_z), 5);
assert_eq(_d48_h0, 0x3FE54B8C1495A43F, "pinned: hisab's q/2 of the covariance sweep's unit-scale point");
var _D48_RUN = 0;
var _D48_BAD = 0;
for (var _d48_k = 0 - 1020; _d48_k <= 512; _d48_k = _d48_k + 1) {
    var _d48_s = (1023 + _d48_k) << 52;
    var _d48_p = cga_point(f64_mul(_d48_x, _d48_s), f64_mul(_d48_y, _d48_s), f64_mul(_d48_z, _d48_s));
    _D48_RUN = _D48_RUN + 1;
    if (cga_mv_get(_d48_p, 5) != f64_mul(f64_mul(_d48_h0, _d48_s), _d48_s)) { _D48_BAD = _D48_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_D48_RUN, 1533, "the cga_point scale sweep actually ran 1533 scales");
assert_eq(_D48_BAD, 0, "cga_point: q/2 scales by exactly 4^k from 2^-1020 to 2^512 (3.3.3: 9 wrong)");

# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION: the null blades must still read exactly 0, which is what the
# power-of-two choice protects. ⚠ And an all-zero multivector, and NaN.
var _cn_z = cga_mv_zero();
assert_eq(cga_norm(_cn_z), 0, "cga_norm: the zero multivector still has norm 0");
var _cn_i = cga_mv_zero();
cga_mv_set(_cn_i, 1, F64_POS_INF);
assert_eq(cga_norm(_cn_i), F64_POS_INF, "cga_norm: +Inf in, +Inf out");
var _cn_n = cga_mv_zero();
cga_mv_set(_cn_n, 1, 0x7FF8000000000000);
var _cn_nv = cga_norm(_cn_n);
assert_eq(f64_eq(_cn_nv, _cn_nv), 0, "cga_norm: NaN propagates");

# ⛔ `calc_monotone_cubic` PROJECTS (alpha, beta) ONTO THE FRITSCH-CARLSON CIRCLE
# of radius 3, which is a norm-scaled projection — and `alpha² + beta²` overflowed
# to +Inf at hypot >= 2^512, making tau exactly +0 and FLATTENING the segment.
# The result stays finite and monotone, so the monotonicity oracle this function
# already has still passed while the value was **20% wrong**.
# ⚠ THE DISCRIMINATING VARIABLE IS THE RATIO BETWEEN ADJACENT SECANTS. alpha and
# beta are tangent-over-secant ratios, so the uniform x-axis sweep that already
# covers this function is bit-exactly invariant and could never have seen it —
# the 2.15.0 "a uniform units change is vacuous" lesson, third instance.
# ⭐ The grid is xs = (0, 2^-q, 1) with ys = (0, 1, 2): the two secants differ by
# 2^q, so the sweep IS the ratio. Above q = 60 the spline has reached its limit
# shape and f(0.5) is exactly 1.875 at every ratio.
# [measured: 941-step secant-ratio sweep — 488 wrong before, 0 after, 2026-09-10]
var _MC_RUN = 0;
var _MC_BAD = 0;
for (var _mc_q = 60; _mc_q <= 1000; _mc_q = _mc_q + 1) {
    var _mc_xs = alloc(24);
    store64(_mc_xs, 0);
    store64(_mc_xs + 8, (1023 - _mc_q) << 52);
    store64(_mc_xs + 16, F64_ONE);
    var _mc_ys = alloc(24);
    store64(_mc_ys, 0);
    store64(_mc_ys + 8, F64_ONE);
    store64(_mc_ys + 16, F64_TWO);
    _MC_RUN = _MC_RUN + 1;
    if (calc_monotone_cubic(_mc_xs, _mc_ys, 3, F64_HALF) != 0x3FFE000000000000) {
        _MC_BAD = _MC_BAD + 1;
    }
}
assert_eq(_MC_RUN, 941, "the secant-ratio sweep actually ran 941 ratios");
assert_eq(_MC_BAD, 0,
  "calc_monotone_cubic: f(0.5) = 1.875 for secant ratios 2^60 .. 2^1000 (was 1.5 above 2^512)");

# ⚠ ONE MUTANT SURVIVES AND IT IS A CORRECTNESS EQUIVALENCE, recorded so the next
# reader does not go looking for a missing assertion: replacing the fast path with
# an UNCONDITIONAL `ganita_f64_hypot` is right at every ratio. The fast path is
# there for cost, not correctness — `calc_monotone_cubic` rebuilds every tangent
# on EVERY evaluation, so a spline sampled at m points is O(n*m), and vec2.cyr
# measured the same choice at +119% unconditional against +12% guarded. ⚠ No
# tracked benchmark touches calc_ext, so that figure is carried from vec2's
# measurement rather than re-measured here, and no performance claim is made.
# [measured: 8-mutant run over geo_advanced.cyr + calc_ext.cyr, 7 killed]

test_group("geo 2.14.0: ray/geometry guards are scale-free");

# --- 2.17.0: geo_triangle_unit_normal / geo_segment_direction. Both had an
# EXACT guard by 2.10.2 and both still fabricated, because the quantity the exact
# guard tested — `hvec3_length_sq` — squared before summing and reached zero
# while the geometry was perfectly ordinary. An exact test on a destroyed value
# is still wrong; the value has to survive to the test.
# ⚠ A (3k, 4k, 0) SEGMENT, NOT AN AXIS-ALIGNED ONE. The first probe used a
# segment along +x, where the fabricated `unit_x` fallback IS the right answer, so
# it reported the entire low band clean. The diagonal is what discriminates, and
# this is the second time in this release that a fixture returned the right answer
# for the wrong reason. [measured: axis-aligned 509 bad of 2041, diagonal 994]
var _GN_RUN = 0;
var _GN_BAD = 0;
for (var _gn_e = 0 - 1020; _gn_e <= 1020; _gn_e = _gn_e + 1) {
    var _gn_k = (1023 + _gn_e) << 52;
    var _gn_d = geo_segment_direction(geo_segment_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0),
        hvec3_new(f64_mul(f64_from(3), _gn_k), f64_mul(f64_from(4), _gn_k), 0)));
    _GN_RUN = _GN_RUN + 1;
    # 3/5 and 4/5 -- not bit-exact through a division, so compare relatively.
    if (f64_gt(f64_abs(f64_sub(HVec3_x(_gn_d), 0x3FE3333333333333)), ULP_TOL_M) == 1) {
        _GN_BAD = _GN_BAD + 1;
    }
    if (f64_gt(f64_abs(f64_sub(HVec3_y(_gn_d), 0x3FE999999999999A)), ULP_TOL_M) == 1) {
        _GN_BAD = _GN_BAD + 1;
    }
}
assert_eq(_GN_RUN, 2041, "the segment-direction binade sweep actually ran 2041 scales");
assert_eq(_GN_BAD, 0,
  "geo_segment_direction: (3k,4k,0) -> (0.6,0.8,0) for k from 2^-1020 to 2^1020");

# --- 3.3.4 D087: geo_segment_direction below DBL_MIN. The sweep above stops at 2^-1020
# and (3k, 4k, 0) is a Pythagorean shape whose length is exact on any grid, so it
# could not see either half of this. Every non-zero integer direction (i, j, l) with
# |i|, |j| <= 6 and l in {0, 1, 3}, put on the subnormal grid as (i, j, l) * 2^-1074,
# must give the unit-size answer bit for bit: a power of two does not change a
# direction. 3.3.3 returned unit_x for 500 of the 506 (`1 / len` overflowed), and
# dividing by the rounded subnormal length instead is wrong for 452 (see src/geo.cyr).
# [measured: georay segment sweep, exact Fraction/Decimal oracle, 3.3.3 geo.cyr vs repaired, 2026-10-03; cycc 6.6.14]
var _SD_RUN = 0;
var _SD_BAD = 0;
var _sd_o = hvec3_new(0, 0, 0);
var _sd_t = 0x0000000000000001;   # 2^-1074, the least subnormal
for (var _sd_i = 0 - 6; _sd_i <= 6; _sd_i = _sd_i + 1) {
    for (var _sd_j = 0 - 6; _sd_j <= 6; _sd_j = _sd_j + 1) {
        for (var _sd_q = 0; _sd_q < 3; _sd_q = _sd_q + 1) {
            var _sd_l = _sd_q;
            if (_sd_q == 2) { _sd_l = 3; }
            if (_sd_i != 0 || _sd_j != 0 || _sd_l != 0) {
                var _sd_x = f64_from(_sd_i);
                var _sd_y = f64_from(_sd_j);
                var _sd_z = f64_from(_sd_l);
                var _sd_u = geo_segment_direction(geo_segment_new(_sd_o, hvec3_new(_sd_x, _sd_y, _sd_z)));
                # n * 2^-1074 is exact for every small integer n
                var _sd_d = geo_segment_direction(geo_segment_new(_sd_o,
                    hvec3_new(f64_mul(_sd_x, _sd_t), f64_mul(_sd_y, _sd_t), f64_mul(_sd_z, _sd_t))));
                _SD_RUN = _SD_RUN + 1;
                if (HVec3_x(_sd_d) != HVec3_x(_sd_u)) { _SD_BAD = _SD_BAD + 1; }
                if (HVec3_y(_sd_d) != HVec3_y(_sd_u)) { _SD_BAD = _SD_BAD + 1; }
                if (HVec3_z(_sd_d) != HVec3_z(_sd_u)) { _SD_BAD = _SD_BAD + 1; }
            }
        }
    }
}
assert_eq(_SD_RUN, 506, "the subnormal segment-direction grid actually ran 506 directions");
assert_eq(_SD_BAD, 0,
  "geo_segment_direction: (i,j,l) * 2^-1074 is the unit-size direction, bit for bit (3.3.3: 500 of 506 unit_x)");
# One in bits, so a change at unit size cannot hide behind the comparison: (-6,-6,0) is
# (-1/sqrt2, -1/sqrt2, 0), correctly rounded, where dividing by the rounded subnormal
# length, 8 * 2^-1074, gives (-0.75, -0.75, 0) and 3.3.3 gave unit_x.
var _sd_p = geo_segment_direction(geo_segment_new(_sd_o,
    hvec3_new(0x8000000000000006, 0x8000000000000006, 0)));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_sd_p), 0xBFE6A09E667F3BCD, "geo_segment_direction: (-6,-6,0) * 2^-1074 -> x = -1/sqrt2 (bits)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_sd_p), 0xBFE6A09E667F3BCD, "geo_segment_direction: ... y = -1/sqrt2 (bits)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_sd_p), 0, "geo_segment_direction: ... z = +0 (bits)");
# At unit size the shipped arithmetic is kept bit for bit: (3,4,0) is 3 and 4 times the
# rounded 1/5, so x is 0x3FE3333333333334, 1 ulp above 0.6. Dividing by the length would
# round x to 0.6 exactly here, and move the last bit of ordinary directions elsewhere.
var _sd_u34 = geo_segment_direction(geo_segment_new(_sd_o, hvec3_new(f64_from(3), f64_from(4), 0)));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_sd_u34), 0x3FE3333333333334, "geo_segment_direction: (3,4,0) -> x = 3 * (1/5), 3.3.3's bits");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_sd_u34), 0x3FE999999999999A, "geo_segment_direction: ... y = 4 * (1/5), 3.3.3's bits");
# And the overflowing end: (DBL_MAX, DBL_MAX, 0) has a length above DBL_MAX, which 3.3.3
# turned into the direction (0, 0, 0) (`1 / Inf` = 0). 0x3FE6A09E667F3BCC is 1 ulp under
# the nearest double to 1/sqrt2, and it is what (2 - 2^-52, 2 - 2^-52, 0), the same
# mantissa at unit size, gives on 3.3.3 too.
var _sd_m = geo_segment_direction(geo_segment_new(_sd_o,
    hvec3_new(0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF, 0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF, 0)));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_sd_m), 0x3FE6A09E667F3BCC,
  "geo_segment_direction: (DBL_MAX,DBL_MAX,0) -> x = 1/sqrt2 - 1 ulp (bits)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_sd_m), 0x3FE6A09E667F3BCC, "geo_segment_direction: ... y the same (bits), not 3.3.3's 0");

# ⚠ THE TRIANGLE SWEEP IS SHALLOWER THAN THE SEGMENT ONE, AND THE DEPTH IS
# DERIVED. A cross product is degree TWO, so legs of size s give a normal of size
# s², and `hvec3_cross` ITSELF underflows for s below 2^-512 and overflows at or
# above 2^512 — before any norm runs. That is upstream of this function and the
# norm repair cannot reach it, so the sweep runs to where the repaired code is
# actually right rather than pretending otherwise. Measured: the band moved from
# legs of 2^-269 / 2^256 to 2^-512 / 2^512, i.e. 243 and 256 binades bought, and
# 378 of 1401 remain — all of them the cross product's own limit.
# [measured: 1401-binade two-sided sweep before and after, 2026-09-10]
var _GT_RUN = 0;
var _GT_BAD = 0;
for (var _gt_e = 0 - 511; _gt_e <= 511; _gt_e = _gt_e + 1) {
    var _gt_k = (1023 + _gt_e) << 52;
    # a right triangle in the xy plane: the unit normal is +z at every leg length
    var _gt_n = geo_triangle_unit_normal(geo_triangle_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0),
        hvec3_new(_gt_k, 0, 0), hvec3_new(0, _gt_k, 0)));
    _GT_RUN = _GT_RUN + 1;
    if (HVec3_z(_gt_n) != 0x3FF0000000000000) { _GT_BAD = _GT_BAD + 1; }
    # ⚠ z alone does not discriminate: the fabricated fallback is unit_y, whose z
    # is 0 — but a HALF repair could leave y non-zero, so pin that too.
    if (HVec3_y(_gt_n) != 0) { _GT_BAD = _GT_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_GT_RUN, 1023, "the triangle-normal binade sweep actually ran 1023 leg scales");
assert_eq(_GT_BAD, 0,
  "geo_triangle_unit_normal: +z for legs from 2^-511 to 2^511 (was unit_y below 2^-269)");

# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION: the degenerate fallbacks must survive, or the repair is a
# deletion. ⚠ And the NaN contract, which 3.3.3 REVERSED: 2.10.2 folded a NaN
# into the fallback (its guards were `f64_gt(len, 0) == 0`, true for NaN) and
# these two pins said so. That fold was the fabricated-answer class — a NaN
# vertex came back as a valid unit normal — so a NaN now propagates, as it does
# through hvec3_normalize. The true value was settled on that second code path:
# `hvec3_normalize(geo_triangle_normal(tri))` and `hvec3_normalize(end - start)`
# are NaN on these inputs (the oracle controls in the "3.3.3 G6: the same four
# guards at a NaN input" block, after the 2.10.2 guard interrogation).
assert_eq(HVec3_y(geo_triangle_unit_normal(geo_triangle_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0),
        hvec3_new(F64_ONE, 0, 0), hvec3_new(F64_TWO, 0, 0)))), 0x3FF0000000000000,
  "geo_triangle_unit_normal: a colinear triangle still returns unit_y");
assert_eq(HVec3_x(geo_segment_direction(geo_segment_new(hvec3_new(F64_ONE, 0, 0),
        hvec3_new(F64_ONE, 0, 0)))), 0x3FF0000000000000,
  "geo_segment_direction: a zero-length segment still returns unit_x");
var _g6_pin_tn = geo_triangle_unit_normal(geo_triangle_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0),
    hvec3_new(0x7FF8000000000000, 0, 0), hvec3_new(0, F64_ONE, 0)));
assert_eq(f64_eq(HVec3_y(_g6_pin_tn), HVec3_y(_g6_pin_tn)), 0,
  "geo_triangle_unit_normal: a NaN triangle is NaN out, not unit_y (2.10.2's fold, reversed 3.3.3)");
var _g6_pin_sd = geo_segment_direction(geo_segment_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0),
    hvec3_new(0x7FF8000000000000, 0, 0)));
assert_eq(f64_eq(HVec3_x(_g6_pin_sd), HVec3_x(_g6_pin_sd)), 0,
  "geo_segment_direction: a NaN segment is NaN out, not unit_x (reversed 3.3.3)");
# 3.3.4: a non-finite input skips the power-of-two pre-scaling and meets 3.3.3's
# arithmetic, so its answer keeps its bits, down to the sign of a zero beside the NaN:
# (0,0,0), (1,1,3), (Inf,1,1) has the cross product (-2, Inf, -Inf), length Inf, and
# (-2 * 0, NaN, NaN). Scaled first, -2 would flush to +0 - +0 = +0.
var _g6_pin_ti = geo_triangle_unit_normal(geo_triangle_new(hvec3_zero(),
    hvec3_new(F64_ONE, F64_ONE, f64_from(3)), hvec3_new(0x7FF0000000000000, F64_ONE, F64_ONE)));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_g6_pin_ti), 0x8000000000000000,
  "geo_triangle_unit_normal: an infinite vertex keeps 3.3.3's arithmetic, x = -0 (bits)");
assert_eq(_g6_nan3(_g6_pin_ti), 2, "geo_triangle_unit_normal: ... and y, z NaN");

# === 3.3.3 R4geo: geo_triangle_unit_normal past the cross product's overflow ===
# The _GT sweep above stops at legs of 2^511 because `hvec3_cross` overflows from 2^512,
# and its right triangle cannot show what that overflow does to a GENERIC triangle: its
# cross product is (0, 0, Inf), with no `Inf - Inf`. A generic one gets `Inf - Inf` = NaN
# in a component, a NaN length, and through 3.3.2 the NaN-true fold returned unit_y for it,
# a finite input with a wrong answer here (and a right one only by coincidence where the
# true normal is +y; see src/geo.cyr). Since 3.3.3 it is NaN in all three, the loud
# answer until the edges are pre-scaled (roadmap [3.3.4] D037). THE TRUE VALUE is
# (0.7071, -0.7071, 0), what the same triangle at 1e150 returns (the control below); NaN
# is pinned as the contract, settled on the second path `hvec3_normalize` of the same
# cross product, which is NaN in all three too.
# [measured: R4geo unit-normal probe, 3.3.2 and repaired bundles, not reproducible in-tree, 2026-10-01; cycc 6.6.12]
# ⛔ 3.3.4 — D037 pre-scales the edges, and the true value comes back. The exact cross
# product is (1e300, -1e300, 0), so the exact unit normal is (1/sqrt2, -1/sqrt2, 0); the
# two doubles either side of 1/sqrt2 are ...3BCC and ...3BCD (nearest), and this
# triangle's (x, -x, 0) at x = 1e300's mantissa rounds to the lower, 1 ulp from exact.
# The pin is that value, and the same triangle multiplied by 2^-700, exactly, must give
# the same bits: a power of two does not change a normal.
var _r4g_e300 = 0x7E37E43C8800759C;   # 1e300
var _r4g_t300 = geo_triangle_new(hvec3_zero(), hvec3_new(_r4g_e300, _r4g_e300, 0),
  hvec3_new(_r4g_e300, _r4g_e300, F64_ONE));
assert_eq(_g6_nan3(hvec3_normalize(geo_triangle_normal(_r4g_t300))), 3,
  "oracle: hvec3_normalize of the 1e300 triangle's UNSCALED cross product is NaN in all three");
var _r4g_n300 = geo_triangle_unit_normal(_r4g_t300);
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r4g_n300), 0x3FE6A09E667F3BCC,
  "geo_triangle_unit_normal: the finite 1e300 triangle -> x = 1/sqrt2 - 1 ulp (bits; 3.3.3 NaN, 3.3.2 unit_y)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_r4g_n300), 0xBFE6A09E667F3BCC, "geo_triangle_unit_normal: ... y = -x (bits)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_r4g_n300), 0, "geo_triangle_unit_normal: ... z = +0 (bits)");
var _r4g_m700 = (1023 - 700) << 52;   # 2^-700
var _r4g_n300s = geo_triangle_unit_normal(geo_triangle_new(hvec3_zero(),
    hvec3_new(f64_mul(_r4g_e300, _r4g_m700), f64_mul(_r4g_e300, _r4g_m700), 0),
    hvec3_new(f64_mul(_r4g_e300, _r4g_m700), f64_mul(_r4g_e300, _r4g_m700), _r4g_m700)));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r4g_n300s), HVec3_x(_r4g_n300),
  "geo_triangle_unit_normal: the 1e300 triangle * 2^-700, x the same bits");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_r4g_n300s), HVec3_y(_r4g_n300), "geo_triangle_unit_normal: ... y the same bits");
var _r4g_e150 = 0x5F138D352E5096AF;   # 1e150
var _r4g_n150 = geo_triangle_unit_normal(geo_triangle_new(hvec3_zero(), hvec3_new(_r4g_e150, _r4g_e150, 0),
    hvec3_new(_r4g_e150, _r4g_e150, F64_ONE)));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_r4g_n150), 0x3FE6A09E667F3BCD, "control: the same triangle at 1e150 -> x = 0.7071 (bits)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_r4g_n150), 0xBFE6A09E667F3BCD, "control: ... y = -0.7071 (bits)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_r4g_n150), 0, "control: ... z = +0 (bits)");

# === 3.3.4 D037 / D038 / closest point: one full-mantissa triangle at every scale ===
# geo_triangle_unit_normal, geo_barycentric_coords and geo_closest_point_on_triangle now
# pre-scale by a power of two, so multiplying the whole scene by 2^e must change nothing
# but the closest point, which scales with it: bit-exact against the unit-size answers.
# Full mantissas, coordinates of magnitude 1 to 8 (no power-of-two or Pythagorean shape,
# whose products come out exact), and one query in each of the seven Voronoi regions (A,
# AB, AC, B, BC, C, interior, settled by an exact rational closest point). The range is
# derived: below 2^-1021 an edge or an answer component of this fixture is subnormal, and
# above 2^1021 a coordinate (up to 8) overflows, so outside it the scaled input is not the
# same triangle. From 2^1020 an edge can pass DBL_MAX, which the routines meet by halving
# every input first. The unit-size answers are 3.3.3's own, within 0.88 * 2^-53 (normal),
# 12.6 * 2^-53 (weights) and 0.70 ulp of the largest coordinate (closest point) of the
# exact values. 3.3.3 changed the normal at 1021 of these 2043 scales, and missed 32175
# weight components and 21227 closest-point components of the 7 * 3 * 2043.
# [measured: georay fixture check, exact Fraction/Decimal oracle, 3.3.3 geo.cyr vs repaired, 2026-10-03; cycc 6.6.14]
var _ST_A = hvec3_new(0xC01236DE575A4247, 0x401A35070D87AB79, 0x400EDF52C27567E0);
var _ST_B = hvec3_new(0x4008C8F128B23A7D, 0x40039DF6F323D23C, 0xC00DF44B7747B58F);
var _ST_C = hvec3_new(0x400A0437E0105A8E, 0x401513CD911CA427, 0x40050E9745DA27B6);
var _st_q = alloc(56);
store64(_st_q, hvec3_new(0xC014AE7CCB6C2A34, 0x4019BC5ADE0E2224, 0x401351655FD46D9E));        # A
store64(_st_q + 8, hvec3_new(0xC00B1BFFE46CFB80, 0xC01460147AB4D418, 0xC017B9DDAB7BCC20));    # AB
store64(_st_q + 16, hvec3_new(0x3FF43A26696A867C, 0xC012D43445CC7F10, 0x401DEEF8B2796721));   # AC
store64(_st_q + 24, hvec3_new(0x401FC3639F034855, 0xC011A6A8070D0179, 0xBFF0B11A64B1A0C2));   # B
store64(_st_q + 32, hvec3_new(0x4019C165BB4C684D, 0xC0056926EBFAAEA1, 0x4005BE4DC3C82B1C));   # BC
store64(_st_q + 40, hvec3_new(0x4013175D60F31DDA, 0x3FF506EA7997891D, 0x4018DBC15E72E569));   # C
store64(_st_q + 48, hvec3_new(0xC00809AF8F2B56D6, 0xC012C91577735992, 0x401F3B736E59FC7D));   # interior
var _st_t0 = geo_triangle_new(_ST_A, _ST_B, _ST_C);
var _st_n0 = geo_triangle_unit_normal(_st_t0);
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_st_n0), 0xBFB606A57A620C1F, "geo_triangle_unit_normal: the fixture at unit size, x (bits)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_st_n0), 0xBFED23244F754DD0, "geo_triangle_unit_normal: ... y (bits)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_st_n0), 0x3FD9E1304019EFEB, "geo_triangle_unit_normal: ... z (bits)");
var _st_b0 = alloc(56);
var _st_c0 = alloc(56);
for (var _st_k = 0; _st_k < 7; _st_k = _st_k + 1) {
    store64(_st_b0 + _st_k * 8, geo_barycentric_coords(_st_t0, load64(_st_q + _st_k * 8)));
    store64(_st_c0 + _st_k * 8, geo_closest_point_on_triangle(_st_t0, load64(_st_q + _st_k * 8)));
}
var _st_ci = load64(_st_c0 + 48);
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_st_ci), 0xBFFFF62D133EA9BC, "geo_closest_point_on_triangle: interior query at unit size, x (bits)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_st_ci), 0x4017D90806CFEA98, "geo_closest_point_on_triangle: ... y (bits)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_st_ci), 0x400899040E7EEC2D, "geo_closest_point_on_triangle: ... z (bits)");
var _st_bi = load64(_st_b0 + 48);
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_st_bi), 0x3FE57BD92537CF25, "geo_barycentric_coords: interior query at unit size, u (bits)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_st_bi), 0x3FAE9A5081CCCD75, "geo_barycentric_coords: ... v (bits)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_st_bi), 0x3FD13503A556C807, "geo_barycentric_coords: ... w (bits)");
var _ST_RUN = 0;
var _ST_BAD_N = 0;
var _ST_BAD_W = 0;
var _ST_BAD_P = 0;
for (var _st_e = 0 - 1021; _st_e <= 1021; _st_e = _st_e + 1) {
    var _st_s = (1023 + _st_e) << 52;
    var _st_t = geo_triangle_new(hvec3_scale(_ST_A, _st_s), hvec3_scale(_ST_B, _st_s),
      hvec3_scale(_ST_C, _st_s));
    var _st_n = geo_triangle_unit_normal(_st_t);
    if (HVec3_x(_st_n) != HVec3_x(_st_n0)) { _ST_BAD_N = _ST_BAD_N + 1; }
    if (HVec3_y(_st_n) != HVec3_y(_st_n0)) { _ST_BAD_N = _ST_BAD_N + 1; }
    if (HVec3_z(_st_n) != HVec3_z(_st_n0)) { _ST_BAD_N = _ST_BAD_N + 1; }
    for (var _st_j = 0; _st_j < 7; _st_j = _st_j + 1) {
        var _st_p = hvec3_scale(load64(_st_q + _st_j * 8), _st_s);
        var _st_w = geo_barycentric_coords(_st_t, _st_p);
        var _st_w0 = load64(_st_b0 + _st_j * 8);
        if (HVec3_x(_st_w) != HVec3_x(_st_w0)) { _ST_BAD_W = _ST_BAD_W + 1; }
        if (HVec3_y(_st_w) != HVec3_y(_st_w0)) { _ST_BAD_W = _ST_BAD_W + 1; }
        if (HVec3_z(_st_w) != HVec3_z(_st_w0)) { _ST_BAD_W = _ST_BAD_W + 1; }
        var _st_c = geo_closest_point_on_triangle(_st_t, _st_p);
        var _st_cw = hvec3_scale(load64(_st_c0 + _st_j * 8), _st_s);   # exact: a power of two
        if (HVec3_x(_st_c) != HVec3_x(_st_cw)) { _ST_BAD_P = _ST_BAD_P + 1; }
        if (HVec3_y(_st_c) != HVec3_y(_st_cw)) { _ST_BAD_P = _ST_BAD_P + 1; }
        if (HVec3_z(_st_c) != HVec3_z(_st_cw)) { _ST_BAD_P = _ST_BAD_P + 1; }
    }
    _ST_RUN = _ST_RUN + 1;
}
assert_eq(_ST_RUN, 2043, "the full-mantissa triangle scale sweep actually ran 2043 scales");
assert_eq(_ST_BAD_N, 0,
  "geo_triangle_unit_normal: the same bits at every scale 2^-1021..2^1021 (3.3.3: changed at 1021)");
assert_eq(_ST_BAD_W, 0,
  "geo_barycentric_coords: the same bits at every scale, 7 queries (3.3.3: 32175 components missed)");
assert_eq(_ST_BAD_P, 0,
  "geo_closest_point_on_triangle: 2^e times the unit answer, every region (3.3.3: 21227 missed)");
# The closest point's scale comes from the edges, not the query. A query 2^-600 of the
# edges from vertex A must not set it, or the degree-four va = d3 * d6 - d5 * d4, all
# edge, overflows. Triangle A = 0, B and C in z = 0, query (B + C) / 4 * 2^-600 inside
# it: the answer is 3.3.3's, bit for bit, 12 ulp of q from q itself (2^-650 of the
# triangle).
var _nv_b = hvec3_new(0x4008C8F128B23A7D, 0x40039DF6F323D23C, 0);
var _nv_c = hvec3_new(0x400A0437E0105A8E, 0x401513CD911CA427, 0);
var _nv_p = geo_closest_point_on_triangle(geo_triangle_new(hvec3_zero(), _nv_b, _nv_c),
  hvec3_new(0x1A79669484614A86, 0x1A7EE2C90AAE8D45, 0));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_nv_p), 0x1A79669484614A7A, "geo_closest_point_on_triangle: query 2^-600 from A, x (bits)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_nv_p), 0x1A7EE2C90AAE8D3C, "geo_closest_point_on_triangle: ... y (bits)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_nv_p), 0, "geo_closest_point_on_triangle: ... z = +0");
# A query far OFF the plane must not set it either: the same triangle at 2^-300, the
# query (B + C) / 4 * 2^-300 raised 1.37 * 2^900 out of z = 0. Its offset along the
# edges is that of the in-plane point, so the answer is (B + C) / 4 * 2^-300, as the
# unit-size triangle with the query 2^600 above it gives (3.3.3 too, there): x is q's
# x exactly and y is 2 ulp under. Scaled by the edges alone, the offset (2^1200)
# overflows and the answer is (0,0,0); s is held so the offset stays below 2^1018.
# 3.3.3 answered another point of the plane, 0x2D3EA5B797DBE14A in x.
var _fp_s = (1023 - 300) << 52;
var _fp_p = geo_closest_point_on_triangle(geo_triangle_new(hvec3_zero(), hvec3_scale(_nv_b, _fp_s),
    hvec3_scale(_nv_c, _fp_s)), hvec3_new(0x2D39669484614A86, 0x2D3EE2C90AAE8D45, f64_mul(0x3FF5EB851EB851EC,
      (1023 + 900) << 52)));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_fp_p), 0x2D39669484614A86,
  "geo_closest_point_on_triangle: query 2^900 off a 2^-300 triangle, x (bits)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_fp_p), 0x2D3EE2C90AAE8D43, "geo_closest_point_on_triangle: ... y (bits)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_fp_p), 0, "geo_closest_point_on_triangle: ... z = +0");
# The scale is the LARGER edge's: a triangle with AB 2^-600 of AC, the query near edge
# AC. Scaled by AB alone, AC and the offset would be 2^600 and d2 * d6 overflow (NaN).
# The answer is the exact closest point, correctly rounded, as on 3.3.3.
var _th_p = geo_closest_point_on_triangle(geo_triangle_new(hvec3_zero(),
    hvec3_new(0x1A88C8F128B23A7D, 0x1A839DF6F323D23C, 0), _nv_c), hvec3_new(0x3FFB9DD179A9F428, 0x40044700C44FD75A, 0));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_th_p), 0x3FF9BE22AB1EAC6D, "geo_closest_point_on_triangle: thin triangle, query near AC, x (bits)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_th_p), 0x4004DB066CC9C6A9, "geo_closest_point_on_triangle: ... y (bits)");

# The right triangle with legs 2^e over the WHOLE range, subnormal legs included: the
# 2.17.0 sweep above stops at 2^-511 / 2^511 because the unscaled cross product did.
# 3.3.3 was wrong at 1075 of these 2098 (unit_y at or below 2^-512, NaN from 2^512).
# [measured: georay triangle sweeps, exact Fraction/Decimal oracle, 3.3.3 geo.cyr vs repaired, 2026-10-03; cycc 6.6.14]
var _SR_RUN = 0;
var _SR_BAD = 0;
for (var _sr_e = 0 - 1074; _sr_e <= 1023; _sr_e = _sr_e + 1) {
    var _sr_k = (1023 + _sr_e) << 52;
    if (_sr_e < 0 - 1022) { _sr_k = 1 << (1074 + _sr_e); }   # subnormal 2^e: shift <= 51
    var _sr_n = geo_triangle_unit_normal(geo_triangle_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0),
        hvec3_new(_sr_k, 0, 0), hvec3_new(0, _sr_k, 0)));
    _SR_RUN = _SR_RUN + 1;
    if (HVec3_z(_sr_n) != 0x3FF0000000000000) { _SR_BAD = _SR_BAD + 1; }
    if (HVec3_y(_sr_n) != 0) { _SR_BAD = _SR_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_SR_RUN, 2098, "the full-range right-triangle sweep actually ran 2098 leg scales");
assert_eq(_SR_BAD, 0, "geo_triangle_unit_normal: +z for legs 2^-1074 to 2^1023 (3.3.3: 1075 wrong)");
# the larger edge sets the scale: legs 2^-1050 and 3. Scaled by the subnormal leg alone
# (2^1023), the other would overflow and the normal be NaN.
var _sr_t = geo_triangle_unit_normal(geo_triangle_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), hvec3_new(0x0000000001000000, 0, 0),
    hvec3_new(0, f64_from(3), 0)));
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_sr_t), 0x3FF0000000000000, "geo_triangle_unit_normal: legs 2^-1050 and 3 -> +z (3.3.3: unit_y)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_sr_t), 0, "geo_triangle_unit_normal: ... y = +0");

# Vertices MORE than DBL_MAX apart: the edge b - a itself overflows before any scaling,
# so the four routines halve every input first when one is in the top binade (exact
# there). Triangle (-h,0,0), (h,0,0), (0,h,0) with h = 1.5 * 2^1023: its edges are 3h/2
# and its answers are exact dyadic numbers. 3.3.3 answered NaN for each but the closest
# point to (h, h, 0), which it put at vertex B, (h, 0, 0).
var _tb_h = 0x7FE8000000000000;   # 1.5 * 2^1023
var _tb_t = geo_triangle_new(hvec3_new(f64_neg(_tb_h), 0, 0), hvec3_new(_tb_h, 0, 0), hvec3_new(0, _tb_h, 0));
var _tb_n = geo_triangle_unit_normal(_tb_t);
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_tb_n), 0x3FF0000000000000, "geo_triangle_unit_normal: edges past DBL_MAX -> +z (3.3.3: NaN)");
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_tb_n), 0, "geo_triangle_unit_normal: ... x = +0 (bits)");
var _tb_w = geo_barycentric_coords(_tb_t, hvec3_new(0, 0x7FC8000000000000, 0));   # (0, h/4, 0)
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_tb_w), 0x3FD8000000000000, "geo_barycentric_coords: edges past DBL_MAX, u = 3/8 (3.3.3: NaN)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_tb_w), 0x3FD8000000000000, "geo_barycentric_coords: ... v = 3/8");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_tb_w), 0x3FD0000000000000, "geo_barycentric_coords: ... w = 1/4");
var _tb_c = geo_closest_point_on_triangle(_tb_t, hvec3_new(_tb_h, _tb_h, 0));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_tb_c), 0x7FD8000000000000,
  "geo_closest_point_on_triangle: (h,h,0) -> (h/2,h/2,0), x (3.3.3: vertex B, h)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_tb_c), 0x7FD8000000000000, "geo_closest_point_on_triangle: ... y = h/2 (3.3.3: 0)");
var _tb_ci = geo_closest_point_on_triangle(_tb_t, hvec3_new(0, 0x7FC8000000000000, _tb_h));
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_tb_ci), 0x7FC8000000000000, "geo_closest_point_on_triangle: (0,h/4,h) -> (0,h/4,0), y (3.3.3: NaN)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_tb_ci), 0, "geo_closest_point_on_triangle: ... z = +0");
var _tb_d = geo_segment_direction(geo_segment_new(hvec3_new(f64_neg(_tb_h), f64_neg(_tb_h), 0),
    hvec3_new(_tb_h, _tb_h, 0)));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_tb_d), 0x3FE6A09E667F3BCD, "geo_segment_direction: ends past DBL_MAX apart -> 1/sqrt2 (3.3.3: NaN)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_tb_d), 0x3FE6A09E667F3BCD, "geo_segment_direction: ... y = 1/sqrt2 (bits)");
# The query alone in the top binade: the triangle (-g,0,0), (0,g,0), (-g,g,0) with
# g = 1.5 * 2^1022 is not, but the query's offset from A passes DBL_MAX. 3.3.3: NaN
# weights, and vertex A for the closest point.
var _tb_g = 0x7FD8000000000000;   # 1.5 * 2^1022
var _tb_t2 = geo_triangle_new(hvec3_new(f64_neg(_tb_g), 0, 0), hvec3_new(0, _tb_g, 0),
  hvec3_new(f64_neg(_tb_g), _tb_g, 0));
var _tb_w2 = geo_barycentric_coords(_tb_t2, hvec3_new(_tb_h, 0, 0));   # (2g,0,0) = A + 3B - 3C
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_tb_w2), 0x3FF0000000000000, "geo_barycentric_coords: query alone past 2^1023, u = 1 (3.3.3: NaN)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_tb_w2), 0x4008000000000000, "geo_barycentric_coords: ... v = 3");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_tb_w2), 0xC008000000000000, "geo_barycentric_coords: ... w = -3");
# beyond edge AB at its midpoint M = (-g/2, g/2, 0), along the outward normal: M exactly
var _tb_c2 = geo_closest_point_on_triangle(_tb_t2, hvec3_new(0x7FE6000000000000, 0xFFE6000000000000, 0));
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_tb_c2), 0xFFC8000000000000,
  "geo_closest_point_on_triangle: query alone past 2^1023 -> M, x = -g/2 (3.3.3: A)");
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_tb_c2), 0x7FC8000000000000, "geo_closest_point_on_triangle: ... y = g/2");
# and each vertex alone in the top binade: B, then C, at h; the normals are +z and -z.
assert_eq(HVec3_z(geo_triangle_unit_normal(geo_triangle_new(hvec3_new(f64_neg(_tb_g), 0, 0),
        hvec3_new(_tb_h, 0, 0), hvec3_new(0, _tb_g, 0)))), 0x3FF0000000000000,
  "geo_triangle_unit_normal: B alone past 2^1023 -> +z (3.3.3: NaN)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(geo_triangle_unit_normal(geo_triangle_new(hvec3_new(f64_neg(_tb_g), 0, 0),
        hvec3_new(0, _tb_g, 0), hvec3_new(_tb_h, 0, 0)))), 0xBFF0000000000000,
  "geo_triangle_unit_normal: C alone past 2^1023 -> -z (3.3.3: NaN)");
var _tb_wa = geo_barycentric_coords(geo_triangle_new(hvec3_new(_tb_h, 0, 0), hvec3_new(0, _tb_g, 0),
    hvec3_new(f64_neg(_tb_g), 0, 0)), hvec3_new(0, _tb_g, 0));
# exact (0, 1, 0); the Gram form rounds v 1 ulp low, at any size (h and g scaled to 3 and
# 1.5 give the same bits), and u = 1 - v
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_tb_wa), 0x3FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF,
  "geo_barycentric_coords: A alone past 2^1023, query at B, v = 1 - 2^-53 (3.3.3: NaN)");
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_tb_wa), 0x3CA0000000000000, "geo_barycentric_coords: ... u = 2^-53");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_tb_wa), 0, "geo_barycentric_coords: ... w = 0");
# The halving starts ON 2^1023: ends at 0x7FDFFFFFFFFFFFFF, the double below it, are not
# halved, so a difference of 2^-1074 in y survives and the direction is (0, -1, 0).
# Halving one binade early (`>= 2045` for `== 2046`) rounds that bit away, and the
# zero segment's unit_x comes back, as it does at 2^1023 itself (_geo_top_binade).
var _tb_lo = 0x7FDFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
var _tb_db = geo_segment_direction(geo_segment_new(hvec3_new(_tb_lo, 1, 0), hvec3_new(_tb_lo, 0, 0)));
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_tb_db), 0xBFF0000000000000,
  "geo_segment_direction: ends just below 2^1023, 2^-1074 apart in y -> y = -1 (not halved)");
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_tb_db), 0, "geo_segment_direction: ... x = +0 (bits; not unit_x)");

# --- J. THE SIX JETS. Each guarded `den` (or dk/fk) against an absolute 1e-12
# while the thing that fails is `1 / den` on the next line, so an ordinary scene
# in small units was reported as GRAZING — and `_geo_jet_miss()` is exactly what
# a genuine tangency returns, so the caller cannot tell them apart.
# ⚠ The direction magnitude is what these carry, so the ray must NOT be built
# through geo_ray_new. ⭐ geo_jet_plane was not on the triage list; it is here
# because its primal geo_ray_plane is repaired in this same release, and a jet
# that rejects rays its own primal accepts is a NEW defect, not a leftover.
var _GJ_RUN = 0;
var _GJ_BAD = 0;
var _gj_pl = geo_plane_from_point_normal(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), hvec3_new(0, 0, F64_ONE));
var _gj_sp = geo_sphere_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), F64_ONE);
var _gj_tr = geo_triangle_new(hvec3_new(f64_neg(F64_ONE), f64_neg(F64_ONE), 0),
  hvec3_new(f64_from(3), f64_neg(F64_ONE), 0), hvec3_new(f64_neg(F64_ONE), f64_from(3), 0));
var _gj_ab = geo_aabb_new(hvec3_new(f64_neg(F64_ONE), f64_neg(F64_ONE), f64_neg(F64_ONE)),
  hvec3_new(F64_ONE, F64_ONE, F64_ONE));
var _gj_cp = geo_capsule_new(hvec3_new(f64_neg(F64_ONE), 0, 0), hvec3_new(F64_ONE, 0, 0),
  F64_HALF);
var _gj_ob = geo_obb_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0),
  hvec3_new(F64_ONE, F64_ONE, F64_ONE), hquat_identity());
for (var _gj_k = 0; _gj_k < 240; _gj_k = _gj_k + 1) {
    var _gj_r = _graw(0, 0, f64_from(5), 0, 0, f64_neg(_gp2(0 - _gj_k)));
    _GJ_RUN = _GJ_RUN + 1;
    # Every one of these is a head-on hit at every direction magnitude. A miss
    # here is the guard fabricating a tangency.
    if (geo_jet_hit(geo_jet_plane(_gj_r, _gj_pl)) != 1) { _GJ_BAD = _GJ_BAD + 1; }
    if (geo_jet_hit(geo_jet_sphere(_gj_r, _gj_sp)) != 1) { _GJ_BAD = _GJ_BAD + 1; }
    if (geo_jet_hit(geo_jet_triangle(_gj_r, _gj_tr)) != 1) { _GJ_BAD = _GJ_BAD + 1; }
    if (geo_jet_hit(geo_jet_aabb(_gj_r, _gj_ab)) != 1) { _GJ_BAD = _GJ_BAD + 1; }
    if (geo_jet_hit(geo_jet_capsule(_gj_r, _gj_cp)) != 1) { _GJ_BAD = _GJ_BAD + 1; }
    # ⚠ obb was omitted from the first draft of this sweep and its mutant SURVIVED.
    if (geo_jet_hit(geo_jet_obb(_gj_r, _gj_ob)) != 1) { _GJ_BAD = _GJ_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_GJ_RUN, 240, "the jet direction-magnitude sweep actually ran");
assert_eq(_GJ_BAD, 0,
  "geo jets: a head-on ray still hits at every direction magnitude to 2^-239");
# ⭐ THE JET MUST AGREE WITH ITS OWN PRIMAL. This is the assertion that would have
# caught the inconsistency the geo_ray_plane repair introduced into geo_jet_plane.
var _GA_RUN = 0;
var _GA_BAD = 0;
for (var _ga_k = 0; _ga_k < 240; _ga_k = _ga_k + 1) {
    var _ga_r = _graw(0, 0, f64_from(5), 0, 0, f64_neg(_gp2(0 - _ga_k)));
    _GA_RUN = _GA_RUN + 1;
    var _ga_pt = geo_ray_plane(_ga_r, _gj_pl);
    var _ga_pj = geo_jet_plane(_ga_r, _gj_pl);
    if ((_ga_pt != 0) != (geo_jet_hit(_ga_pj) == 1)) { _GA_BAD = _GA_BAD + 1; }
    if (geo_jet_hit(_ga_pj) == 1) {
        if (geo_jet_t(_ga_pj) != _ga_pt) { _GA_BAD = _GA_BAD + 1; }
    }
    var _ga_st = geo_ray_sphere(_ga_r, _gj_sp);
    var _ga_sj = geo_jet_sphere(_ga_r, _gj_sp);
    if ((_ga_st != 0) != (geo_jet_hit(_ga_sj) == 1)) { _GA_BAD = _GA_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_GA_RUN, 240, "the jet/primal agreement sweep actually ran");
assert_eq(_GA_BAD, 0,
  "geo jets: hit/miss and t agree with the primal at every direction magnitude");
# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION: a genuinely grazing ray must still report a miss.
assert_eq(geo_jet_hit(geo_jet_plane(_graw(0, 0, f64_from(5), F64_ONE, 0, 0), _gj_pl)), 0,
  "geo_jet_plane: a ray parallel to the plane is still a miss");
# ⛔ THAT ASSERTION IS VACUOUS FOR THE GUARD AND MUTATION PROVED IT: geo_jet_plane
# calls geo_ray_plane FIRST and returns early on t == 0, so a parallel ray never
# reaches the guard at all and deleting the guard still passes. The case that
# does reach it is one the PRIMAL accepts and the jet must still reject: a ray
# exactly TANGENT to the sphere. There den = dot(g, d) is exactly 0 while the
# quadratic has a real (double) root, so 1/den is +Inf and the jet must report
# the non-differentiable point rather than hand back infinite partials.
assert_eq(geo_jet_hit(geo_jet_sphere(_graw(0, F64_ONE, f64_from(5), 0, 0,
        f64_neg(F64_ONE)), _gj_sp)), 0,
  "geo_jet_sphere: an exactly tangent ray is a miss, not an infinite gradient");
assert(geo_ray_sphere(_graw(0, F64_ONE, f64_from(5), 0, 0, f64_neg(F64_ONE)),
    _gj_sp) != 0,
  "the tangency control is not vacuous: the PRIMAL does report a hit there");

# ⭐ THE SUBNORMAL BAND, which is the only place a DBL_MIN threshold and a
# finite-reciprocal test disagree. geo_jet_triangle's den is the same triple
# product geo_ray_triangle guards, so legs of 2^-511 and 2^-512 put den at
# exactly 2^-1023: subnormal, but 1/den = 2^1023 is finite and the gradient is
# perfectly well defined. DBL_MIN would refuse it; the reciprocal test allows it.
var _gjt_tri = geo_triangle_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), hvec3_new(_gp2(0 - 511), 0, 0),
  hvec3_new(0, _gp2(0 - 512), 0));
var _gjt_ray = geo_ray_new(hvec3_new(_gp2(0 - 513), _gp2(0 - 514), F64_ONE),
  hvec3_new(0, 0, f64_neg(F64_ONE)));
assert_eq(geo_jet_hit(geo_jet_triangle(_gjt_ray, _gjt_tri)), 1,
  "geo_jet_triangle: a subnormal den whose reciprocal is finite is still a hit");

fn _gp2(e) { return (1023 + e) << 52; }

# A ray whose direction is NOT normalised. geo_ray_new normalises, which would
# erase the very quantity these guards get wrong, so build the struct directly.
fn _graw(ox, oy, oz, dx, dy, dz) {
    var r = alloc(sizeof(GeoRay));
    GeoRay_set_origin(r, hvec3_new(ox, oy, oz));
    GeoRay_set_direction(r, hvec3_new(dx, dy, dz));
    return r;
}

# --- P. geo_ray_plane: `denom` = n·d carries |d|, so the answer must not depend
# on how long the caller made the direction vector. Plane z = 0, ray from (0,0,5)
# pointing down with |d| = 2^-k: the hit is at t = 5 * 2^k.
var _GP_RUN = 0;
var _GP_BAD = 0;
var _gp_pl = geo_plane_from_point_normal(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), hvec3_new(0, 0, F64_ONE));
for (var _gp_k = 0; _gp_k < 300; _gp_k = _gp_k + 1) {
    var _gp_d = _gp2(0 - _gp_k);
    var _gp_r = _graw(0, 0, f64_from(5), 0, 0, f64_neg(_gp_d));
    _GP_RUN = _GP_RUN + 1;
    # t = 5 / 2^-k = 5 * 2^k, exact for every power of two.
    if (geo_ray_plane(_gp_r, _gp_pl) != f64_mul(f64_from(5), _gp2(_gp_k))) {
        _GP_BAD = _GP_BAD + 1;
    }
}
assert_eq(_GP_RUN, 300, "geo_ray_plane direction-magnitude sweep actually ran");
assert_eq(_GP_BAD, 0,
  "geo_ray_plane: t = 5*2^k bit-exact for |d| from 2^0 down to 2^-299");
# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION: a ray genuinely parallel to the plane must still miss.
assert_eq(geo_ray_plane(_graw(0, 0, f64_from(5), F64_ONE, 0, 0), _gp_pl), 0,
  "geo_ray_plane: a parallel ray is still a miss");

# --- S. geo_ray_sphere / _geo_sphere_roots: `a` = d·d. Unit sphere at the origin,
# ray from (0,0,5) inward with |d| = 2^-k: the near hit is at t = 4 * 2^k.
var _GS_RUN = 0;
var _GS_BAD = 0;
var _gs_sph = geo_sphere_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), F64_ONE);
for (var _gs_k = 0; _gs_k < 300; _gs_k = _gs_k + 1) {
    var _gs_d = _gp2(0 - _gs_k);
    var _gs_r = _graw(0, 0, f64_from(5), 0, 0, f64_neg(_gs_d));
    _GS_RUN = _GS_RUN + 1;
    if (geo_ray_sphere(_gs_r, _gs_sph) != f64_mul(f64_from(4), _gp2(_gs_k))) {
        _GS_BAD = _GS_BAD + 1;
    }
}
assert_eq(_GS_RUN, 300, "geo_ray_sphere direction-magnitude sweep actually ran");
assert_eq(_GS_BAD, 0,
  "geo_ray_sphere: t = 4*2^k bit-exact for |d| from 2^0 down to 2^-299");
assert_eq(geo_ray_sphere(_graw(0, 0, f64_from(5), 0, 0, F64_ONE), _gs_sph), 0,
  "geo_ray_sphere: a ray pointing away is still a miss");

# --- T. geo_ray_triangle. `a` is a scalar triple product, degree TWO in the edge
# lengths for a unit direction, so it is the SMALL-TRIANGLE case that fails.
# Legs 2^i and 2^j, ray dropped from unit height onto the point at u = v = 1/4:
# t is exactly 1.0 at every scale. ⚠ The ray origin stays at height 1, not at the
# triangle's scale — a scaled height makes the PROBE's own cube under/overflow
# and manufactures failures that are not the library's.
var _GT_RUN = 0;
var _GT_BAD = 0;
fn _gt_shot(i, j) {
    var tri = geo_triangle_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), hvec3_new(_gp2(i), 0, 0),
      hvec3_new(0, _gp2(j), 0));
    return geo_ray_triangle(geo_ray_new(hvec3_new(_gp2(i - 2), _gp2(j - 2), F64_ONE),
        hvec3_new(0, 0, f64_neg(F64_ONE))), tri);
}
for (var _gt_k = 0; _gt_k < 500; _gt_k = _gt_k + 1) {
    _GT_RUN = _GT_RUN + 1;
    if (_gt_shot(0 - _gt_k, 0 - _gt_k) != 0x3FF0000000000000) { _GT_BAD = _GT_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_GT_RUN, 500, "geo_ray_triangle edge-scale sweep actually ran");
assert_eq(_GT_BAD, 0,
  "geo_ray_triangle: t = 1.0 bit-exact for legs from 2^0 down to 2^-499");
# ⭐ THE SUBNORMAL BAND, where a DBL_MIN threshold and a finite-reciprocal test
# stop agreeing. a = 2^(i+j) exactly, so these three pin the boundary:
assert_eq(_gt_shot(0 - 511, 0 - 511), 0x3FF0000000000000,
  "geo_ray_triangle: a = DBL_MIN exactly still hits (1/a is finite)");
assert_eq(_gt_shot(0 - 511, 0 - 512), 0x3FF0000000000000,
  "geo_ray_triangle: a = 2^-1023 is SUBNORMAL and 1/a is still finite, so it hits");
assert_eq(_gt_shot(0 - 512, 0 - 512), 0,
  "geo_ray_triangle: a = 2^-1024 overflows its reciprocal, so it must miss");
# ⚠ A CORNER-ON RAY IS THE ONLY CASE THAT REACHES THE CONTRACT VIOLATION: s.h and
# d.q are both exactly 0, so u and v are NaN and the [0,1] range tests cannot
# reject an infinite f. Without this, `f64_gt(f64_abs(a), 0) == 0` survives.
fn _gt_corner(i, j) {
    var tri = geo_triangle_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), hvec3_new(_gp2(i), 0, 0),
      hvec3_new(0, _gp2(j), 0));
    return geo_ray_triangle(geo_ray_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, F64_ONE),
        hvec3_new(0, 0, f64_neg(F64_ONE))), tri);
}
assert_eq(_gt_corner(0 - 512, 0 - 512), 0,
  "geo_ray_triangle: a corner-on ray with an overflowing 1/a returns 0, not +Inf");
assert_eq(_gt_corner(0 - 400, 0 - 400), 0x3FF0000000000000,
  "geo_ray_triangle: the corner-on control is a real hit at a workable scale");
# NaN must be rejected, not propagated.
assert_eq(geo_ray_triangle(geo_ray_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, F64_ONE),
      hvec3_new(0, 0, f64_neg(F64_ONE))),
    geo_triangle_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), hvec3_new(0x7FF8000000000000, 0, 0),
      hvec3_new(0, F64_ONE, 0))), 0,
  "geo_ray_triangle: a NaN vertex is a miss, not a NaN t");

# --- C. geo_closest_point_on_sphere. THREE axes, because they fail differently
# and the third one is what forces the two-step normalise.
var _GC_RUN = 0;
var _GC_BAD = 0;
# (1) uniform scale: same problem in different units.
for (var _gc_k = 0 - 250; _gc_k < 150; _gc_k = _gc_k + 1) {
    var _gc_s = geo_sphere_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), _gp2(_gc_k));
    var _gc_p = geo_closest_point_on_sphere(_gc_s, hvec3_new(0, _gp2(_gc_k - 10), 0));
    _GC_RUN = _GC_RUN + 1;
    if (HVec3_y(_gc_p) != _gp2(_gc_k)) { _GC_BAD = _GC_BAD + 1; }
    if (HVec3_x(_gc_p) != 0) { _GC_BAD = _GC_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_GC_RUN, 400, "closest_point_on_sphere uniform-scale sweep actually ran");
assert_eq(_GC_BAD, 0,
  "geo_closest_point_on_sphere: the +y answer is exact at every sphere scale");
# ⭐ (2) THE RATIO. Offset pinned tiny, radius swept up: `radius / len` overflows
# to +Inf long before either operand does. This is the axis that motivates the
# two-step form — a one-step DBL_MIN repair fails 77 of these.
var _GR_RUN = 0;
var _GR_BAD = 0;
for (var _gr_i = 0; _gr_i < 600; _gr_i = _gr_i + 1) {
    var _gr_s = geo_sphere_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), _gp2(_gr_i));
    var _gr_p = geo_closest_point_on_sphere(_gr_s, hvec3_new(0, _gp2(0 - 500), 0));
    _GR_RUN = _GR_RUN + 1;
    if (HVec3_y(_gr_p) != _gp2(_gr_i)) { _GR_BAD = _GR_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_GR_RUN, 600, "closest_point_on_sphere ratio sweep actually ran");
assert_eq(_GR_BAD, 0,
  "geo_closest_point_on_sphere: radius/len never overflows, radius 2^0..2^599");
# (3) the offset FRACTION, radius pinned at 1: a relative guard passes axis 1 and
# fails here, so this is what rules `len < radius * 1e-12` out.
var _GF_RUN = 0;
var _GF_BAD = 0;
var _gf_s = geo_sphere_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), F64_ONE);
for (var _gf_j = 0 - 530; _gf_j < 0; _gf_j = _gf_j + 1) {
    var _gf_p = geo_closest_point_on_sphere(_gf_s, hvec3_new(0, _gp2(_gf_j), 0));
    _GF_RUN = _GF_RUN + 1;
    if (HVec3_y(_gf_p) != 0x3FF0000000000000) { _GF_BAD = _GF_BAD + 1; }
}
assert_eq(_GF_RUN, 530, "closest_point_on_sphere offset-fraction sweep actually ran");
assert_eq(_GF_BAD, 0,
  "geo_closest_point_on_sphere: a query 2^-530 radii out still answers +y");
# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION: the documented centre case must still take the fallback.
assert_eq(HVec3_x(geo_closest_point_on_sphere(_gf_s, hvec3_new(0, 0, 0))),
  0x3FF0000000000000,
  "geo_closest_point_on_sphere: a point AT the centre still returns +x radius");

# --- B. geo_barycentric_coords. `denom` is the Gram determinant, degree FOUR in
# the triangle's units, and the fallback (1/3,1/3,1/3) is indistinguishable from
# a real answer. Right triangle with legs s, query at u = v = 1/4.
var _GB_RUN = 0;
var _GB_BAD = 0;
for (var _gb_k = 0; _gb_k < 250; _gb_k = _gb_k + 1) {
    var _gb_s = _gp2(0 - _gb_k);
    var _gb_tri = geo_triangle_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), hvec3_new(_gb_s, 0, 0),
      hvec3_new(0, _gb_s, 0));
    var _gb_w = geo_barycentric_coords(_gb_tri,
      hvec3_new(f64_mul(_gb_s, F64_HALF), f64_div(_gb_s, f64_from(4)), 0));
    _GB_RUN = _GB_RUN + 1;
    # u = 1/2, v = 1/4, w = 1 - u - v = 1/4, exact at every power-of-two scale.
    if (HVec3_y(_gb_w) != 0x3FE0000000000000) { _GB_BAD = _GB_BAD + 1; }
    if (HVec3_z(_gb_w) != 0x3FD0000000000000) { _GB_BAD = _GB_BAD + 1; }
}
# --- K. geo_ray_capsule's cylinder guard. `a` is the SQUARED perpendicular
# component of the direction, so like _geo_sphere_roots it fails on the direction
# magnitude, not on the scene. Capsule (0,0,0)-(1,0,0) radius 1/4, ray dropped
# from (1/2, 2, 0) with |d| = 2^-k: the barrel hit is at t = 1.75 * 2^k.
# ⚠ Added because the first mutation run left `_GEO_F64_EPS_SQ` ALIVE here —
# nothing in the tree reached this guard with a short direction vector.
var _GK_RUN = 0;
var _GK_BAD = 0;
var _gk_cap = geo_capsule_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), hvec3_new(F64_ONE, 0, 0),
  f64_div(F64_ONE, f64_from(4)));
for (var _gk_k = 0; _gk_k < 200; _gk_k = _gk_k + 1) {
    var _gk_r = _graw(F64_HALF, F64_TWO, 0, 0, f64_neg(_gp2(0 - _gk_k)), 0);
    var _gk_t = geo_ray_capsule(_gk_r, _gk_cap);
    _GK_RUN = _GK_RUN + 1;
    # 1.75 * 2^k, exact: 7/4 is a dyadic rational and the scale is a power of two.
    if (_gk_t != f64_mul(f64_div(f64_from(7), f64_from(4)), _gp2(_gk_k))) {
        _GK_BAD = _GK_BAD + 1;
    }
}
assert_eq(_GK_RUN, 200, "geo_ray_capsule direction-magnitude sweep actually ran");
assert_eq(_GK_BAD, 0,
  "geo_ray_capsule: barrel hit t = 1.75*2^k exact for |d| from 2^0 to 2^-199");
assert_eq(geo_ray_capsule(_graw(F64_HALF, F64_TWO, 0, 0, F64_ONE, 0), _gk_cap), 0,
  "geo_ray_capsule: a ray pointing away from the barrel is still a miss");

assert_eq(_GB_RUN, 250, "barycentric triangle-scale sweep actually ran");
assert_eq(_GB_BAD, 0,
  "geo_barycentric_coords: (1/4, 1/2, 1/4) exact for legs 2^0 down to 2^-249");
# ⭐ ANTI-DELETION: a genuinely degenerate triangle must still get the centroid.
assert_eq(HVec3_x(geo_barycentric_coords(
      geo_triangle_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), hvec3_new(F64_ONE, 0, 0),
        hvec3_new(F64_TWO, 0, 0)), hvec3_new(F64_HALF, 0, 0))), _GEO_F64_THIRD,
  "geo_barycentric_coords: a colinear triangle still returns centroid weights");
# 3.3.4: and one with A = B, so v0 = 0 and the scale comes from v1 alone (from v0 it
# would be 2^1023, v1 would overflow, and the weights would be NaN).
assert_eq(HVec3_z(geo_barycentric_coords(geo_triangle_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, 0), hvec3_new(0, 0, 0),
        hvec3_new(f64_from(3), 0, 0)), hvec3_new(F64_ONE, F64_ONE, 0))), _GEO_F64_THIRD,
  "geo_barycentric_coords: a triangle with A = B still returns centroid weights");
# 3.3.4: a query whose weights are near 2^1023 holds the scale down, as the closest
# point's offset does. Edges 1.9 * 2^-10, query (2^1014, 0, 0): scaled by the edges alone
# (2^10) the query is 2^1024 = +Inf. 3.3.3's arithmetic gives v 1 ulp under 2^1024 / 1.9.
var _bq_e = 0x3F5E666666666666;   # 1.9 * 2^-10
var _bq_w = geo_barycentric_coords(geo_triangle_new(hvec3_zero(), hvec3_new(_bq_e, 0, 0), hvec3_new(0, _bq_e, 0)),
  hvec3_new(0x7F50000000000000, 0, 0));
assert_eq(HVec3_y(_bq_w), 0x7FE0D79435E50D78, "geo_barycentric_coords: weights near 2^1023, v (bits; not Inf)");
assert_eq(HVec3_x(_bq_w), 0xFFE0D79435E50D78, "geo_barycentric_coords: ... u = 1 - v (bits; not NaN)");
assert_eq(HVec3_z(_bq_w), 0, "geo_barycentric_coords: ... w = +0");

# ================================================================
# 3.2.0 -- public API reached by no test
# ================================================================
# The 3.1.0 surface scan (comments and string literals stripped) listed the public
# items below as referenced by nothing beyond their own declaration. Re-derived
# for 3.2.0 with the same stripping over src/, tests/, examples/ and dist/:
# 16 names have zero references, 8 of them enum NAMES (an enum is reached only
# through its members), and `csr_new` / the six `EULER_*` members / the seven
# `GEO_JET_*` tags / `MAT3_BYTES` / `MAT4_BYTES` are reached inside src/ but by no
# test. `hisab_is_err`, on the 3.1.0 list, has seven assertions (foundation,
# abuse) and is not re-tested here. Every expected value below is derived in the
# comment beside it, never copied from the implementation.
# [measured: scratch census script, comments+strings stripped, 2026-09-14]
test_group("3.2.0 autodiff: ad_neg / ad_cos / ad_ln against their forward-mode twins");

# The module's own oracle for reverse mode is forward mode. On a single node the
# reverse sweep computes adj = 1 * d1 through f64_add(0, f64_mul(1, d1)), which
# is d1 EXACTLY, and the dual twin computes 1 * (the same partial), so the two
# modes must agree BIT FOR BIT on a one-op tape -- no tolerance.
var ut_t = ad_tape_new(32);
var ut_x = ad_var(ut_t, f64_from(3));
var ut_neg = ad_neg(ut_t, ut_x);
assert_eq(ad_val(ut_t, ut_neg), f64_neg(f64_from(3)), "ad_neg: value of -x at 3 is -3");
var _ut1t, _ut1v = ad_grad(ut_t, ut_neg);
assert_eq(is_ok(_ut1t), 1, "ad_neg: gradient sweep succeeds");
assert_eq(ad_grad_of(ut_t, ut_x), f64_neg(F64_ONE), "ad_neg: d(-x)/dx is exactly -1");
assert_eq(ad_grad_of(ut_t, ut_x), Dual_deriv(dual_neg(dual_var(f64_from(3)))),
  "ad_neg agrees with dual_neg bit for bit");
var ut_cos = ad_cos(ut_t, ut_x);
assert_eq(ad_val(ut_t, ut_cos), f64_cos(f64_from(3)), "ad_cos: value is f64_cos(3)");
var _ut2t, _ut2v = ad_grad(ut_t, ut_cos);
assert_eq(is_ok(_ut2t), 1, "ad_cos: gradient sweep succeeds");
assert_eq(ad_grad_of(ut_t, ut_x), f64_neg(f64_sin(f64_from(3))), "ad_cos: d cos/dx at 3 is -sin(3)");
assert_eq(ad_grad_of(ut_t, ut_x), Dual_deriv(dual_cos(dual_var(f64_from(3)))),
  "ad_cos agrees with dual_cos bit for bit");
var ut_ln = ad_ln(ut_t, ut_x);
assert_eq(ad_val(ut_t, ut_ln), f64_ln(f64_from(3)), "ad_ln: value is f64_ln(3)");
var _ut3t, _ut3v = ad_grad(ut_t, ut_ln);
assert_eq(is_ok(_ut3t), 1, "ad_ln: gradient sweep succeeds");
assert_eq(ad_grad_of(ut_t, ut_x), f64_div(F64_ONE, f64_from(3)), "ad_ln: d ln/dx at 3 is 1/3");
assert_eq(ad_grad_of(ut_t, ut_x), Dual_deriv(dual_ln(dual_var(f64_from(3)))),
  "ad_ln agrees with dual_ln bit for bit");
# Chained through the tape: d/dx ln(cos x) = -sin x / cos x. Reverse forms
# (1/c) * (-s) and forward forms (-s) / c -- one rounding apart, so this pair is
# compared to the closed form at ULP_TOL_M rather than bit for bit.
var ut_h = ad_var(ut_t, F64_HALF);
var ut_lc = ad_ln(ut_t, ad_cos(ut_t, ut_h));
var _ut4t, _ut4v = ad_grad(ut_t, ut_lc);
assert_eq(is_ok(_ut4t), 1, "ad_ln(ad_cos(x)): gradient sweep succeeds");
var ut_tan = f64_neg(f64_div(f64_sin(F64_HALF), f64_cos(F64_HALF)));
assert(f64_approx_eq(ad_grad_of(ut_t, ut_h), ut_tan, ULP_TOL_M) == 1,
  "ad_ln(ad_cos(x)): d/dx at 0.5 is -tan(0.5) through the chain rule");
assert(f64_approx_eq(ad_grad_of(ut_t, ut_h), Dual_deriv(dual_ln(dual_cos(dual_var(F64_HALF)))),
    ULP_TOL_M) == 1, "ad_ln(ad_cos(x)) agrees with the dual chain");
# -ln(x) at x = 4: value -ln 4, derivative -1/4 = -0.25 exactly (a power of two).
var ut_f = ad_var(ut_t, f64_from(4));
var ut_nl = ad_neg(ut_t, ad_ln(ut_t, ut_f));
assert_eq(ad_val(ut_t, ut_nl), f64_neg(f64_ln(f64_from(4))), "ad_neg(ad_ln(x)): value is -ln 4");
var _ut5t, _ut5v = ad_grad(ut_t, ut_nl);
assert_eq(is_ok(_ut5t), 1, "ad_neg(ad_ln(x)): gradient sweep succeeds");
assert_eq(ad_grad_of(ut_t, ut_f), f64_neg(f64_div(F64_ONE, f64_from(4))),
  "ad_neg(ad_ln(x)): d/dx at 4 is exactly -1/4");
# A bad input index is AD_FULL and does not grow the tape -- the contract every
# other op in this module already pins.
var ut_len = ad_tape_len(ut_t);
assert_eq(ad_neg(ut_t, 99), AD_FULL, "ad_neg on an out-of-range index is AD_FULL");
assert_eq(ad_cos(ut_t, 0 - 1), AD_FULL, "ad_cos on a negative index is AD_FULL");
assert_eq(ad_ln(ut_t, AD_FULL), AD_FULL, "ad_ln consuming AD_FULL is AD_FULL");
assert_eq(ad_tape_len(ut_t), ut_len, "and none of the three pushed a node");

test_group("3.2.0 linalg_ext: csr_new is the only constructor that keeps an explicit zero");

# A = [[0, 5, 0(explicit)], [-1.5, 0, 0]] handed to csr_new as three flat arrays:
# row_ptr = [0, 2, 3], col_idx = [1, 2, 0], values = [5, 0, -1.5]. csr_from_dense
# stores only non-zero entries, so the stored 0 at (0, 2) is reachable through
# csr_new alone -- a fixed pattern ahead of its values, as an assembly loop does.
var ut_rp = alloc(24); store64(ut_rp, 0); store64(ut_rp + 8, 2); store64(ut_rp + 16, 3);
var ut_ci = alloc(24); store64(ut_ci, 1); store64(ut_ci + 8, 2); store64(ut_ci + 16, 0);
var ut_cv = alloc(24);
store64(ut_cv, f64_from(5)); store64(ut_cv + 8, 0); store64(ut_cv + 16, f64_neg(f64_add(F64_ONE, F64_HALF)));
var ut_A = csr_new(2, 3, 3, ut_rp, ut_ci, ut_cv);
assert_neq(ut_A, 0, "csr_new returns a handle");
assert_eq(csr_nrows(ut_A), 2, "csr_new: nrows stored");
assert_eq(csr_ncols(ut_A), 3, "csr_new: ncols stored");
assert_eq(csr_nnz(ut_A), 3, "csr_new: nnz is the 3 entries GIVEN, the explicit zero included");
assert_eq(csr_row_ptr(ut_A), ut_rp, "csr_new: row_ptr is the caller's array, not a copy");
assert_eq(csr_col_idx(ut_A), ut_ci, "csr_new: col_idx is the caller's array, not a copy");
assert_eq(csr_values(ut_A), ut_cv, "csr_new: values is the caller's array, not a copy");
# The same matrix through csr_from_dense has nnz 2: that is the contrast.
var ut_dense = ganita_mat_new(2, 3);
ganita_mat_set(ut_dense, 0, 1, f64_from(5));
ganita_mat_set(ut_dense, 1, 0, f64_neg(f64_add(F64_ONE, F64_HALF)));
assert_eq(csr_nnz(csr_from_dense(ut_dense)), 2, "csr_from_dense of the same matrix stores 2, not 3");
# A * (1, 2, 4) = (5*2 + 0*4, -1.5*1) = (10, -1.5), all exact.
var ut_xv = alloc(24);
store64(ut_xv, F64_ONE); store64(ut_xv + 8, F64_TWO); store64(ut_xv + 16, f64_from(4));
var ut_y = csr_spmv(ut_A, ut_xv);
assert_eq(load64(ut_y), f64_from(10), "csr_spmv through csr_new: row 0 = 5*2 + 0*4 = 10");
assert_eq(load64(ut_y + 8), f64_neg(f64_add(F64_ONE, F64_HALF)), "csr_spmv through csr_new: row 1 = -1.5");
# A^T is 3x2 with the explicit zero carried along (nnz stays 3), and
# A^T * (1, 1) = (-1.5, 5, 0).
var ut_At = csr_transpose(ut_A);
assert_eq(csr_nrows(ut_At), 3, "csr_transpose of a csr_new matrix: 3 rows");
assert_eq(csr_ncols(ut_At), 2, "csr_transpose of a csr_new matrix: 2 cols");
assert_eq(csr_nnz(ut_At), 3, "csr_transpose keeps the explicit zero (nnz 3)");
var ut_x2 = alloc(16); store64(ut_x2, F64_ONE); store64(ut_x2 + 8, F64_ONE);
var ut_yt = csr_spmv(ut_At, ut_x2);
assert_eq(load64(ut_yt), f64_neg(f64_add(F64_ONE, F64_HALF)), "A^T (1,1): row 0 = -1.5");
assert_eq(load64(ut_yt + 8), f64_from(5), "A^T (1,1): row 1 = 5");
assert_eq(load64(ut_yt + 16), f64_from(0), "A^T (1,1): row 2 is the explicit zero times 1");
# A + A merges same-column entries and drops an exactly-zero SUM, so the explicit
# zero disappears here (nnz 2) while the two real entries double.
var ut_AA = csr_add(ut_A, ut_A);
assert_eq(csr_nnz(ut_AA), 2, "csr_add drops the 0 + 0 sum: nnz 3 -> 2");
var ut_yy = csr_spmv(ut_AA, ut_xv);
assert_eq(load64(ut_yy), f64_from(20), "(A + A)(1,2,4): row 0 = 20");
assert_eq(load64(ut_yy + 8), f64_neg(f64_from(3)), "(A + A)(1,2,4): row 1 = -3");

test_group("3.2.0 diffgeo: hodge_star_2form_4d against the Hodge star on (R^4, +---)");

# Derivation, not copied from the table in diffgeo.cyr. (*F)_{mn} = 1/2 e_{mnrs} F^{rs}
# with e_{0123} = +1 and eta = diag(+1,-1,-1,-1), so F^{0i} = -F_{0i} and
# F^{ij} = +F_{ij}. Then, counting the inversions of each permutation of 0123:
#   (*F)_01 = e_{0123} F^{23} = +F_23            (0123: 0 inversions)
#   (*F)_02 = e_{0213} F^{13} = -F_13            (0213: 1)
#   (*F)_03 = e_{0312} F^{12} = +F_12            (0312: 2)
#   (*F)_12 = e_{1203} F^{03} = +(-F_03) = -F_03 (1203: 2)
#   (*F)_13 = e_{1302} F^{02} = -(-F_02) = +F_02 (1302: 3)
#   (*F)_23 = e_{2301} F^{01} = +(-F_01) = -F_01 (2301: 4)
# Every entry is a copy or a negation, so the results are BIT-EXACT.
fn _t_h6(a, b, c, d, e, f) {
    var p = alloc(48);
    store64(p, a); store64(p + 8, b); store64(p + 16, c);
    store64(p + 24, d); store64(p + 32, e); store64(p + 40, f);
    return p;
}
# Number of slots whose VALUE is non-zero (a negated zero is -0.0, still zero).
fn _t_h_nz(p) {
    var n = 0;
    for (var i = 0; i < 6; i = i + 1) { if (f64_eq(load64(p + i * 8), 0) == 0) { n = n + 1; } }
    return n;
}
# The six basis 2-forms, one at a time: exactly one output slot is non-zero, and
# it is the slot and sign derived above.
var ut_h01 = hodge_star_2form_4d(_t_h6(F64_ONE, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0), F64_ONE);
assert_eq(_t_h_nz(ut_h01), 1, "*(e01) has one non-zero slot");
assert_eq(load64(ut_h01 + 40), f64_neg(F64_ONE), "*(e01) = -e23");
var ut_h02 = hodge_star_2form_4d(_t_h6(0, F64_ONE, 0, 0, 0, 0), F64_ONE);
assert_eq(_t_h_nz(ut_h02), 1, "*(e02) has one non-zero slot");
assert_eq(load64(ut_h02 + 32), F64_ONE, "*(e02) = +e13");
var ut_h03 = hodge_star_2form_4d(_t_h6(0, 0, F64_ONE, 0, 0, 0), F64_ONE);
assert_eq(_t_h_nz(ut_h03), 1, "*(e03) has one non-zero slot");
assert_eq(load64(ut_h03 + 24), f64_neg(F64_ONE), "*(e03) = -e12");
var ut_h12 = hodge_star_2form_4d(_t_h6(0, 0, 0, F64_ONE, 0, 0), F64_ONE);
assert_eq(_t_h_nz(ut_h12), 1, "*(e12) has one non-zero slot");
assert_eq(load64(ut_h12 + 16), F64_ONE, "*(e12) = +e03");
var ut_h13 = hodge_star_2form_4d(_t_h6(0, 0, 0, 0, F64_ONE, 0), F64_ONE);
assert_eq(_t_h_nz(ut_h13), 1, "*(e13) has one non-zero slot");
assert_eq(load64(ut_h13 + 8), f64_neg(F64_ONE), "*(e13) = -e02");
var ut_h23 = hodge_star_2form_4d(_t_h6(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, F64_ONE), F64_ONE);
assert_eq(_t_h_nz(ut_h23), 1, "*(e23) has one non-zero slot");
assert_eq(load64(ut_h23), F64_ONE, "*(e23) = +e01");
# A generic form F = (1,2,3,4,5,6) -> *F = (F23, -F13, F12, -F03, F02, -F01) = (6,-5,4,-3,2,-1).
var ut_F = _t_h6(F64_ONE, F64_TWO, f64_from(3), f64_from(4), f64_from(5), f64_from(6));
var ut_sF = hodge_star_2form_4d(ut_F, F64_ONE);
assert_eq(load64(ut_sF), f64_from(6), "*F slot 01 = F23 = 6");
assert_eq(load64(ut_sF + 8), f64_neg(f64_from(5)), "*F slot 02 = -F13 = -5");
assert_eq(load64(ut_sF + 16), f64_from(4), "*F slot 03 = F12 = 4");
assert_eq(load64(ut_sF + 24), f64_neg(f64_from(3)), "*F slot 12 = -F03 = -3");
assert_eq(load64(ut_sF + 32), F64_TWO, "*F slot 13 = F02 = 2");
assert_eq(load64(ut_sF + 40), f64_neg(F64_ONE), "*F slot 23 = -F01 = -1");
# ** = (-1)^{k(n-k)} (-1)^s on k-forms: k = 2, n = 4, s = 3 negative eigenvalues,
# so ** = -1 -- the Lorentzian 2-form star is an anti-involution (the reason
# self-dual 2-forms need complex coefficients in signature (1,3)).
var ut_ssF = hodge_star_2form_4d(ut_sF, F64_ONE);
var ut_ss_bad = 0;
for (var ut_i1 = 0; ut_i1 < 6; ut_i1 = ut_i1 + 1) {
    if (load64(ut_ssF + ut_i1 * 8) != f64_neg(load64(ut_F + ut_i1 * 8))) { ut_ss_bad = ut_ss_bad + 1; }
}
assert_eq(ut_ss_bad, 0, "**F = -F bit for bit on all six slots (Lorentzian anti-involution)");
# `sign` is the documented overall multiplier: -1 negates every slot.
var ut_nF = hodge_star_2form_4d(ut_F, f64_neg(F64_ONE));
var ut_n_bad = 0;
for (var ut_i2 = 0; ut_i2 < 6; ut_i2 = ut_i2 + 1) {
    if (load64(ut_nF + ut_i2 * 8) != f64_neg(load64(ut_sF + ut_i2 * 8))) { ut_n_bad = ut_n_bad + 1; }
}
assert_eq(ut_n_bad, 0, "sign = -1 negates the sign = +1 dual on all six slots");
# Electromagnetic reading: F_{0i} = E_i, F_{12} = B_3, F_{13} = -B_2, F_{23} = B_1.
# The table above then says (*F)_{0i} = B_i and the magnetic slots carry -E, i.e.
# the duality rotation (E, B) -> (B, -E). E = (1,2,3), B = (4,5,6):
# F = (1,2,3, 6,-5,4) -> *F = (4,5,6, -3,2,-1).
var ut_EM = hodge_star_2form_4d(_t_h6(F64_ONE, F64_TWO, f64_from(3), f64_from(6), f64_neg(f64_from(5)),
    f64_from(4)), F64_ONE);
assert_eq(load64(ut_EM), f64_from(4), "EM duality: (*F)_01 = B_1");
assert_eq(load64(ut_EM + 8), f64_from(5), "EM duality: (*F)_02 = B_2");
assert_eq(load64(ut_EM + 16), f64_from(6), "EM duality: (*F)_03 = B_3");
assert_eq(load64(ut_EM + 24), f64_neg(f64_from(3)), "EM duality: (*F)_12 = -E_3");
assert_eq(load64(ut_EM + 32), F64_TWO, "EM duality: (*F)_13 = +E_2 (so the new B_2 is -E_2)");
assert_eq(load64(ut_EM + 40), f64_neg(F64_ONE), "EM duality: (*F)_23 = -E_1");

test_group("3.2.0 lie_ext: bch_3rd_order on su(2) against the BCH series and the group product");

# su(2) as 2x2 complex matrices: X(v) = -(i/2) (v . sigma), i.e.
#   X = [[ -i vz/2,  (-vy - i vx)/2 ],
#        [ ( vy - i vx)/2,   i vz/2 ]]
# Since [sigma_a, sigma_b] = 2i e_abc sigma_c, this representation satisfies
# [X(u), X(v)] = X(u x v): the matrix bracket IS the cross product, so the BCH
# series can be checked in vector form. The vector is read back from the (0,0)
# and (0,1) entries: vx = -2 Im X01, vy = -2 Re X01, vz = -2 Im X00.
# ⚠ Operands are cmat handles, which is what bch_3rd_order documents -- not
# HVec3s, the 24-into-72-byte shape the 3.1.0 review found in the so(3) fixture.
fn _t_su2mat(v) {
    var vx = HVec3_x(v); var vy = HVec3_y(v); var vz = HVec3_z(v);
    var m = cmat_new(2, 2);
    cmat_set(m, 0, 0, cx_new(0, f64_neg(f64_mul(vz, F64_HALF))));
    cmat_set(m, 0, 1, cx_new(f64_neg(f64_mul(vy, F64_HALF)), f64_neg(f64_mul(vx, F64_HALF))));
    cmat_set(m, 1, 0, cx_new(f64_mul(vy, F64_HALF), f64_neg(f64_mul(vx, F64_HALF))));
    cmat_set(m, 1, 1, cx_new(0, f64_mul(vz, F64_HALF)));
    return m;
}
fn _t_su2vec(m) {
    var a = cmat_get(m, 0, 0); var b = cmat_get(m, 0, 1);
    return hvec3_new(f64_neg(f64_mul(F64_TWO, HComplex_im(b))),
      f64_neg(f64_mul(F64_TWO, HComplex_re(b))), f64_neg(f64_mul(F64_TWO, HComplex_im(a))));
}
# Largest component of |a - b|, NaN-rejecting (a NaN component counts as failing).
fn _t_v3_within(a, b, tol) {
    if (f64_lt(f64_abs(f64_sub(HVec3_x(a), HVec3_x(b))), tol) == 0) { return 0; }
    if (f64_lt(f64_abs(f64_sub(HVec3_y(a), HVec3_y(b))), tol) == 0) { return 0; }
    if (f64_lt(f64_abs(f64_sub(HVec3_z(a), HVec3_z(b))), tol) == 0) { return 0; }
    return 1;
}
var ut_bx = hvec3_new(f64_div(F64_ONE, f64_from(100)), f64_div(F64_TWO, f64_from(100)),
  f64_neg(f64_div(F64_ONE, f64_from(100))));
var ut_by = hvec3_new(f64_neg(f64_div(f64_from(15), f64_from(1000))), f64_div(F64_ONE, f64_from(100)),
  f64_div(F64_TWO, f64_from(100)));
var ut_X = _t_su2mat(ut_bx);
var ut_Y = _t_su2mat(ut_by);
# The representation is faithful: [X, Y] reads back as x cross y.
assert_eq(_t_v3_within(_t_su2vec(cmat_commutator(ut_X, ut_Y)), hvec3_cross(ut_bx, ut_by), ULP_TOL_M), 1,
  "su(2) rep: [X(x), X(y)] = X(x cross y)");
# The truncated series in vector form, written out by hand:
#   z3 = x + y + (1/2) x cross y + (1/12) (x cross (x cross y) - y cross (x cross y)).
var ut_cxy = hvec3_cross(ut_bx, ut_by);
var ut_z3 = hvec3_add(hvec3_add(hvec3_add(ut_bx, ut_by), hvec3_scale(ut_cxy, F64_HALF)),
  hvec3_scale(hvec3_sub(hvec3_cross(ut_bx, ut_cxy), hvec3_cross(ut_by, ut_cxy)),
    f64_div(F64_ONE, f64_from(12))));
var ut_Z = bch_3rd_order(ut_X, ut_Y);
assert_neq(ut_Z, 0, "bch_3rd_order returns a matrix");
assert_eq(_t_v3_within(_t_su2vec(ut_Z), ut_z3, ULP_TOL_M), 1,
  "bch_3rd_order = x + y + 1/2[x,y] + 1/12([x,[x,y]] - [y,[x,y]]) to 1e-15");
# Against the group: exp(X) exp(Y) = exp(Z) exactly, and su2_log(su2_exp(x) su2_exp(y))
# is that Z. The omitted 4th-order term is -(1/24)[Y,[X,[X,Y]]], bounded by
# |x|^2 |y|^2 / 24 = (6.0e-4)(7.25e-4)/24 = 1.8e-8 for these operands, so the
# tolerance is 2e-8; measured |z3 - z| = 3.1e-9. The 2nd-order truncation misses by
# 2.1e-6 (the dropped 3rd-order term), 100x the tolerance -- asserted as the
# control below, so this tolerance is shown to DISCRIMINATE the term under test.
# [measured: scratch probe of this fixture on the 3.1.1 tree, 2026-09-14]
var ut_ze = su2_log(su2_compose(su2_exp(ut_bx), su2_exp(ut_by)));
var ut_bch_tol = 0x3E55798EE2308C3A;   # 2e-8 (1e-8 is 0x3E45798EE2308C3A; one binade up)
assert_eq(_t_v3_within(_t_su2vec(ut_Z), ut_ze, ut_bch_tol), 1,
  "bch_3rd_order matches log(exp X exp Y) within the 4th-order bound 2e-8");
var ut_z2 = _t_su2vec(bch_2nd_order(ut_X, ut_Y));
var ut_1em6 = 0x3EB0C6F7A0B5ED8D;   # 1e-6
var ut_1em5 = 0x3EE4F8B588E368F1;   # 1e-5
assert_eq(_t_v3_within(ut_z2, ut_ze, ut_1em6), 0,
  "control: bch_2nd_order misses the same Z by MORE than 1e-6 (the 3rd-order term is live)");
assert_eq(_t_v3_within(ut_z2, ut_ze, ut_1em5), 1,
  "control: and by less than 1e-5 -- the 2nd-order twin is not simply broken");
# Result stays in su(2): traceless and anti-Hermitian, so the (0,0) entry is
# purely imaginary and the (1,1) entry is its negative.
assert(f64_lt(f64_abs(HComplex_re(cmat_get(ut_Z, 0, 0))), ULP_TOL_M) == 1,
  "bch_3rd_order result is traceless-anti-Hermitian: Re Z00 = 0");
assert(f64_lt(f64_abs(f64_add(HComplex_im(cmat_get(ut_Z, 0, 0)), HComplex_im(cmat_get(ut_Z, 1, 1)))),
    ULP_TOL_M) == 1, "bch_3rd_order result is traceless: Im Z00 = -Im Z11");
# Commuting operands: every bracket is exactly zero, Z = X + Y bit for bit.
# x = (1/2, 0, 0), y = (1, 0, 0): Im Z01 = -(1/2 + 1)/2 = -3/4.
var ut_Zc = bch_3rd_order(_t_su2mat(hvec3_new(F64_HALF, 0, 0)), _t_su2mat(hvec3_new(F64_ONE, 0, 0)));
assert_eq(HComplex_im(cmat_get(ut_Zc, 0, 1)), f64_neg(f64_add(F64_HALF, f64_div(F64_ONE, f64_from(4)))),
  "bch_3rd_order commuting: Im Z01 = -3/4 exactly");
assert_eq(HComplex_re(cmat_get(ut_Zc, 0, 1)), f64_from(0), "bch_3rd_order commuting: Re Z01 = 0");
# Designed-0 propagation: a null or non-conformable operand yields 0, not a crash.
assert_eq(bch_3rd_order(0, ut_Y), 0, "bch_3rd_order(0, Y) is the designed 0");
assert_eq(bch_3rd_order(ut_X, cmat_new(3, 3)), 0, "bch_3rd_order on non-conformable operands is 0");

test_group("3.2.0 transforms: each EulerOrder member against three explicit axis rotations");

# hquat_mul is the Hamilton product and hquat_rotate_vec3 is q v q^-1, so
# q = qa * qb * qc rotates by R_a R_b R_c: c is applied FIRST. EULER_XYZ is
# therefore the matrix product R_x R_y R_z. With all three angles pi/2 on
# v = (1, 2, 3), by hand (each 90-degree axis rotation permutes and signs):
#   XYZ: R_z v = (-2, 1, 3); R_y -> (3, 1, 2);  R_x -> ( 3, -2,  1)
#   XZY: R_y v = ( 3, 2,-1); R_z -> (-2, 3,-1); R_x -> (-2,  1,  3)
#   YXZ: R_z v = (-2, 1, 3); R_x -> (-2,-3, 1); R_y -> ( 1, -3,  2)
#   YZX: R_x v = ( 1,-3, 2); R_z -> ( 3, 1, 2); R_y -> ( 2,  1, -3)
#   ZXY: R_y v = ( 3, 2,-1); R_x -> ( 3, 1, 2); R_z -> (-1,  3,  2)
#   ZYX: R_x v = ( 1,-3, 2); R_y -> ( 2,-3,-1); R_z -> ( 3,  2, -1)
# Six distinct answers, so a swapped dispatch line cannot pass. The half-angle
# sin/cos of pi/4 are not exact, hence TOL_1EM14 (measured worst 1.3e-15).
# [measured: scratch probe of this fixture on the 3.1.1 tree, 2026-09-14]
fn _t_quat_same(a, b) {
    if (HQuat_x(a) != HQuat_x(b)) { return 0; }
    if (HQuat_y(a) != HQuat_y(b)) { return 0; }
    if (HQuat_z(a) != HQuat_z(b)) { return 0; }
    if (HQuat_w(a) != HQuat_w(b)) { return 0; }
    return 1;
}
var ut_ea = F64_PI_2;
var ut_qx = hquat_from_rotation_x(ut_ea);
var ut_qy = hquat_from_rotation_y(ut_ea);
var ut_qz = hquat_from_rotation_z(ut_ea);
var ut_ev = hvec3_new(F64_ONE, F64_TWO, f64_from(3));
assert_eq(EULER_XYZ, 0, "EULER_XYZ = 0 (foundation.tcyr passes this literal)");
assert_eq(EULER_XZY, 1, "EULER_XZY = 1");
assert_eq(EULER_YXZ, 2, "EULER_YXZ = 2");
assert_eq(EULER_YZX, 3, "EULER_YZX = 3");
assert_eq(EULER_ZXY, 4, "EULER_ZXY = 4");
assert_eq(EULER_ZYX, 5, "EULER_ZYX = 5");
var ut_e0 = hquat_from_euler(ut_ea, ut_ea, ut_ea, EULER_XYZ);
assert_eq(_t_quat_same(ut_e0, hquat_mul(hquat_mul(ut_qx, ut_qy), ut_qz)), 1,
  "EULER_XYZ dispatches to qx * qy * qz bit for bit");
assert_eq(_t_v3_within(hquat_rotate_vec3(ut_e0, ut_ev), hvec3_new(f64_from(3), f64_neg(F64_TWO), F64_ONE),
    TOL_1EM14), 1, "EULER_XYZ at (pi/2, pi/2, pi/2) maps (1,2,3) to (3,-2,1)");
var ut_e1 = hquat_from_euler(ut_ea, ut_ea, ut_ea, EULER_XZY);
assert_eq(_t_quat_same(ut_e1, hquat_mul(hquat_mul(ut_qx, ut_qz), ut_qy)), 1,
  "EULER_XZY dispatches to qx * qz * qy bit for bit");
assert_eq(_t_v3_within(hquat_rotate_vec3(ut_e1, ut_ev), hvec3_new(f64_neg(F64_TWO), F64_ONE, f64_from(3)),
    TOL_1EM14), 1, "EULER_XZY at (pi/2, pi/2, pi/2) maps (1,2,3) to (-2,1,3)");
var ut_e2 = hquat_from_euler(ut_ea, ut_ea, ut_ea, EULER_YXZ);
assert_eq(_t_quat_same(ut_e2, hquat_mul(hquat_mul(ut_qy, ut_qx), ut_qz)), 1,
  "EULER_YXZ dispatches to qy * qx * qz bit for bit");
assert_eq(_t_v3_within(hquat_rotate_vec3(ut_e2, ut_ev), hvec3_new(F64_ONE, f64_neg(f64_from(3)), F64_TWO),
    TOL_1EM14), 1, "EULER_YXZ at (pi/2, pi/2, pi/2) maps (1,2,3) to (1,-3,2)");
var ut_e3 = hquat_from_euler(ut_ea, ut_ea, ut_ea, EULER_YZX);
assert_eq(_t_quat_same(ut_e3, hquat_mul(hquat_mul(ut_qy, ut_qz), ut_qx)), 1,
  "EULER_YZX dispatches to qy * qz * qx bit for bit");
assert_eq(_t_v3_within(hquat_rotate_vec3(ut_e3, ut_ev), hvec3_new(F64_TWO, F64_ONE, f64_neg(f64_from(3))),
    TOL_1EM14), 1, "EULER_YZX at (pi/2, pi/2, pi/2) maps (1,2,3) to (2,1,-3)");
var ut_e4 = hquat_from_euler(ut_ea, ut_ea, ut_ea, EULER_ZXY);
assert_eq(_t_quat_same(ut_e4, hquat_mul(hquat_mul(ut_qz, ut_qx), ut_qy)), 1,
  "EULER_ZXY dispatches to qz * qx * qy bit for bit");
assert_eq(_t_v3_within(hquat_rotate_vec3(ut_e4, ut_ev), hvec3_new(f64_neg(F64_ONE), f64_from(3), F64_TWO),
    TOL_1EM14), 1, "EULER_ZXY at (pi/2, pi/2, pi/2) maps (1,2,3) to (-1,3,2)");
var ut_e5 = hquat_from_euler(ut_ea, ut_ea, ut_ea, EULER_ZYX);
assert_eq(_t_quat_same(ut_e5, hquat_mul(hquat_mul(ut_qz, ut_qy), ut_qx)), 1,
  "EULER_ZYX dispatches to qz * qy * qx bit for bit");
assert_eq(_t_v3_within(hquat_rotate_vec3(ut_e5, ut_ev), hvec3_new(f64_from(3), F64_TWO, f64_neg(F64_ONE)),
    TOL_1EM14), 1, "EULER_ZYX at (pi/2, pi/2, pi/2) maps (1,2,3) to (3,2,-1)");

test_group("3.2.0 geo_diff: the GEO_JET_* kind tags, read off each of the six jet constructors");

# The tags are public vars a consumer may store, so the values are pinned; then
# one ray -- origin (0,0,-5), direction +z -- is intersected with each primitive
# so the tag on the returned jet, the typed accessor gate that keys on it, and a
# hand-derived t are all checked on the same object.
assert_eq(GEO_JET_MISS, 0, "GEO_JET_MISS = 0");
assert_eq(GEO_JET_PLANE, 1, "GEO_JET_PLANE = 1");
assert_eq(GEO_JET_SPHERE, 2, "GEO_JET_SPHERE = 2");
assert_eq(GEO_JET_TRIANGLE, 3, "GEO_JET_TRIANGLE = 3");
assert_eq(GEO_JET_AABB, 4, "GEO_JET_AABB = 4");
assert_eq(GEO_JET_OBB, 5, "GEO_JET_OBB = 5");
assert_eq(GEO_JET_CAPSULE, 6, "GEO_JET_CAPSULE = 6");
var ut_ray = geo_ray_new(hvec3_new(0, 0, f64_neg(f64_from(5))), hvec3_unit_z());
# Unit sphere at the origin: hit at z = -1, t = 4. t = 5 - r, so dt/dr = -1.
var ut_js = geo_jet_sphere(ut_ray, geo_sphere_new(hvec3_zero(), F64_ONE));
assert_eq(geo_jet_kind(ut_js), GEO_JET_SPHERE, "geo_jet_sphere tags GEO_JET_SPHERE");
assert_eq(geo_jet_t(ut_js), f64_from(4), "sphere jet: t = 4 exactly");
assert_eq(geo_jet_sphere_dradius(ut_js), f64_neg(F64_ONE), "sphere jet: dt/dr = -1 through its own accessor");
# Plane z = 0 (normal +z, distance 0): t = (dist - n.o)/(n.d) = 5, dt/d(dist) = 1/(n.d) = 1.
var ut_jp = geo_jet_plane(ut_ray, geo_plane_from_point_normal(hvec3_zero(), hvec3_unit_z()));
assert_eq(geo_jet_kind(ut_jp), GEO_JET_PLANE, "geo_jet_plane tags GEO_JET_PLANE");
assert_eq(geo_jet_t(ut_jp), f64_from(5), "plane jet: t = 5 exactly");
assert_eq(geo_jet_plane_ddistance(ut_jp), F64_ONE, "plane jet: dt/d(distance) = 1 through its own accessor");
# Triangle in z = 0 containing the origin: t = 5.
var ut_jt = geo_jet_triangle(ut_ray, geo_triangle_new(hvec3_new(f64_neg(F64_ONE), f64_neg(F64_ONE), 0),
    hvec3_new(F64_ONE, f64_neg(F64_ONE), 0), hvec3_new(0, F64_ONE, 0)));
assert_eq(geo_jet_kind(ut_jt), GEO_JET_TRIANGLE, "geo_jet_triangle tags GEO_JET_TRIANGLE");
assert_eq(geo_jet_t(ut_jt), f64_from(5), "triangle jet: t = 5 exactly");
# Unit cube [-1,1]^3: enters the z-min face at t = 4; face = 2 * axis + plane = 2*2 + 0.
var ut_ja = geo_jet_aabb(ut_ray, geo_aabb_new(hvec3_new(f64_neg(F64_ONE), f64_neg(F64_ONE), f64_neg(F64_ONE)),
    hvec3_one()));
assert_eq(geo_jet_kind(ut_ja), GEO_JET_AABB, "geo_jet_aabb tags GEO_JET_AABB");
assert_eq(geo_jet_t(ut_ja), f64_from(4), "aabb jet: t = 4 exactly");
assert_eq(geo_jet_face(ut_ja), 4, "aabb jet: face = 2*axis + plane = 4 (z, min)");
# The same cube as an OBB with the identity rotation: identical hit.
var ut_jo = geo_jet_obb(ut_ray, geo_obb_new(hvec3_zero(), hvec3_one(), hquat_identity()));
assert_eq(geo_jet_kind(ut_jo), GEO_JET_OBB, "geo_jet_obb tags GEO_JET_OBB");
assert_eq(geo_jet_t(ut_jo), f64_from(4), "obb jet: t = 4 exactly");
assert_eq(geo_jet_face(ut_jo), 4, "obb jet: face 4, same as the aabb it equals");
# Capsule along y through the origin, radius 1: the ray meets the barrel at z = -1, t = 4.
var ut_jc = geo_jet_capsule(ut_ray, geo_capsule_new(hvec3_new(0, f64_neg(F64_ONE), 0),
    hvec3_new(0, F64_ONE, 0), F64_ONE));
assert_eq(geo_jet_kind(ut_jc), GEO_JET_CAPSULE, "geo_jet_capsule tags GEO_JET_CAPSULE");
assert_eq(geo_jet_t(ut_jc), f64_from(4), "capsule jet: t = 4 exactly");
assert_eq(geo_jet_face(ut_jc), GEO_CAP_CYLINDER, "capsule jet: the branch is the barrel (GEO_CAP_CYLINDER)");
# A miss carries GEO_JET_MISS, which is exactly what geo_jet_hit tests.
var ut_jm = geo_jet_sphere(ut_ray, geo_sphere_new(hvec3_new(f64_from(10), 0, 0), F64_ONE));
assert_eq(geo_jet_kind(ut_jm), GEO_JET_MISS, "a missed sphere tags GEO_JET_MISS");
assert_eq(geo_jet_hit(ut_jm), 0, "and geo_jet_hit reads that tag as 0");
assert_eq(geo_jet_hit(ut_js), 1, "while a hit reads as 1");
# The tag is what keeps a plane's dt/d(distance) out of a sphere's dt/d(radius)
# slot: the same `ss` slot, refused through the wrong-kind accessor.
assert_eq(geo_jet_sphere_dradius(ut_jp), 0, "sphere_dradius on a PLANE jet is the sentinel 0");
assert_eq(geo_jet_plane_ddistance(ut_js), 0, "plane_ddistance on a SPHERE jet is the sentinel 0");
assert_eq(geo_jet_plane_dnormal(ut_js), 0, "plane_dnormal on a sphere jet is 0");
assert_eq(geo_jet_aabb_dmin(ut_jo), 0, "aabb_dmin on an OBB jet is 0, even though the boxes coincide");
assert_eq(geo_jet_obb_dcenter(ut_ja), 0, "obb_dcenter on an AABB jet is 0");
assert_eq(geo_jet_capsule_dradius(ut_js), 0, "capsule_dradius on a sphere jet is 0");
assert_eq(geo_jet_tri_dv0(ut_jc), 0, "tri_dv0 on a capsule jet is 0");
assert_neq(geo_jet_tri_dv0(ut_jt), 0, "tri_dv0 on the triangle jet is a vector");
assert_neq(geo_jet_capsule_dstart(ut_jc), 0, "capsule_dstart on the capsule jet is a vector");
assert_neq(geo_jet_obb_drotation(ut_jo), 0, "obb_drotation on the obb jet is a vector");

test_group("3.2.0 mat3/mat4/geo_advanced: the layout constants, read by the code that sizes with them");

# MAT3_BYTES / MAT4_BYTES size the alloc AND bound the copy loop, so a wrong
# value would truncate the copy of the LAST column. The last slot is what is
# checked: (2,2) of a Mat3, (3,3) of a Mat4.
assert_eq(MAT3_BYTES, 9 * 8, "MAT3_BYTES is 9 f64s");
assert_eq(MAT4_BYTES, 16 * 8, "MAT4_BYTES is 16 f64s");
var ut_m3 = m3_new();
m3_set(ut_m3, 2, 2, f64_from(7));
assert_eq(m3_get(m3_copy(ut_m3), 2, 2), f64_from(7), "m3_copy reaches the last slot MAT3_BYTES bounds");
var ut_m4 = m4_new();
m4_set(ut_m4, 3, 3, f64_from(11));
assert_eq(m4_get(m4_copy(ut_m4), 3, 3), f64_from(11), "m4_copy reaches the last slot MAT4_BYTES bounds");
# su2_to_rotation_matrix is sized by MAT3_BYTES since 3.2.0 (it was a bare 72,
# the same number, so no assertion can tell the two allocations apart -- the
# value check that covers the function is the su2_exp-vs-so3_exp comparison in
# the Lie block). What CAN be pinned here is that the last slot of the 72 is
# WRITTEN: a half-turn about x, q = (0, 1, 0, 0), has R[2][2] = 1 - 2(xx + yy)
# = 1 - 2 = -1 exactly, which the identity fixture this replaced could not see.
var ut_su2_hx = su2_from_quat(hquat_new(F64_ONE, 0, 0, 0));   # (x, y, z, w) = (1, 0, 0, 0)
assert_eq(m3_get(su2_to_rotation_matrix(ut_su2_hx), 2, 2), f64_neg(F64_ONE),
  "su2_to_rotation_matrix(half-turn about x): last slot (2,2) reads -1, not identity");
# CGA: 2^5 blades over 5 basis vectors, 8 bytes each, pseudoscalar in the last slot.
assert_eq(CGA_NUM_BLADES, 32, "CGA_NUM_BLADES = 2^5");
assert_eq(CGA_MV_SIZE, CGA_NUM_BLADES * 8, "CGA_MV_SIZE = CGA_NUM_BLADES * 8");
var ut_I = cga_pseudoscalar();
var ut_I_nz = 0;
for (var ut_i3 = 0; ut_i3 < CGA_NUM_BLADES; ut_i3 = ut_i3 + 1) {
    if (f64_eq(cga_mv_get(ut_I, ut_i3), 0) == 0) { ut_I_nz = ut_I_nz + 1; }
}
assert_eq(ut_I_nz, 1, "the pseudoscalar has exactly one non-zero blade in CGA_NUM_BLADES");
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(ut_I, CGA_NUM_BLADES - 1), F64_ONE, "and it is the last one, index CGA_NUM_BLADES - 1");
assert_eq(cga_mv_get(cga_mv_clone(ut_I), CGA_NUM_BLADES - 1), F64_ONE,
  "cga_mv_clone copies the last blade");

var r = assert_summary();
# Clamp before exiting: assert_summary() returns the raw failure COUNT, and a wait
# status is only 8 bits — an unclamped 256 / 512 / 768 failures would exit 0 and
# score PASS. Any failure at all is exit 1.
if (r > 0) { r = 1; }
sys_exit_group(r);
